WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:40.660
Fending is this visceral animal kingdom truth

00:00:42.130 --> 00:00:43.760
that applies to us humans.

00:00:44.976 --> 00:00:45.809
We're mammals.

00:00:45.809 --> 00:00:48.410
We have jackets and cell phones, right,

00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:53.410
we wear fancy watches and sneakers and we're mammals.

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:55.910
And like any mammal parent,

00:00:55.910 --> 00:01:00.050
we have to know for our own biological determination,

00:01:00.050 --> 00:01:02.260
like we have to know our genes get passed on.

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:03.600
How do our genes get passed on?

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:05.400
If our kids can fend for themselves out in the world

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:07.160
without us, like any mammal.

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:08.760
Parent, we need to know that.

00:01:09.681 --> 00:01:12.264
(upbeat music)

00:01:29.413 --> 00:01:32.726
I don't know about you, but despite having great parents,

00:01:32.726 --> 00:01:36.930
despite the privilege of having a world-class education,

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:40.640
nobody actually ever taught me how to be an adult.

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:44.407
And as a result, I made a ton of avoidable mistakes.

00:01:44.407 --> 00:01:46.700
I suffered more than I needed to.

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:49.490
I fumbled around until eventually

00:01:49.490 --> 00:01:50.580
I figured a few things out.

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:54.541
And I think my personal experience, unfortunately,

00:01:54.541 --> 00:01:56.980
is all too common.

00:01:56.980 --> 00:02:00.720
Well today's guest, Julie Lythcott-Haims would agree.

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:03.860
So she decided to do something about it.

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:05.750
Julie is the former Dean of Freshmen

00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:08.700
and Undergraduate Advising at Stanford University,

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:12.110
where she also earned her BA and we were classmates.

00:02:12.110 --> 00:02:14.330
I first met Julie when we were both 18.

00:02:14.330 --> 00:02:15.719
She also earned a law degree from Harvard

00:02:15.719 --> 00:02:18.700
and a master's in fine arts and writing

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:21.010
from California College of the Arts.

00:02:21.010 --> 00:02:23.410
Today she's an author and she's an authority

00:02:23.410 --> 00:02:28.040
on what we now call in Millennial parlance, adulting.

00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:29.890
Her books include the New York Times bestseller,

00:02:29.890 --> 00:02:31.910
"How to Raise an Adult."

00:02:31.910 --> 00:02:33.580
She also wrote "Real American,"

00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:37.100
which is a beautiful memoir centered on coming to terms

00:02:37.100 --> 00:02:38.500
with her racial identity.

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:40.970
And her latest, and the focus of today's conversation

00:02:40.970 --> 00:02:44.870
is called "Your Turn: How to Be an Adult,"

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:47.330
which is a really powerful, beautiful book.

00:02:47.330 --> 00:02:50.240
For those just emerging into the grown-up world,

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:52.900
I think it's a must-read handbook for those people.

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:56.369
For parents, it's a must-gift for the young adults,

00:02:56.369 --> 00:03:00.777
and it's a book I very much wish existed when I was young.

00:03:00.777 --> 00:03:03.690
This one is packed with practical insights

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:06.470
for both parents and young people alike.

00:03:06.470 --> 00:03:08.800
So please hit that subscribe button

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:12.578
and enjoy my conversation with Julie Lythcott-Haims.

00:03:12.578 --> 00:03:13.640
(whooshing sound)

00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:15.484
So nice to see you.

00:03:15.484 --> 00:03:16.980
It's really good to see you

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:19.410
and to be here and to have flown here.

00:03:19.410 --> 00:03:22.920
It's a big experience, I'm glad to be here.

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:25.854
We talked about doing this when the book came out

00:03:25.854 --> 00:03:30.290
and I was just so reluctant to do this on Zoom

00:03:30.290 --> 00:03:31.510
in a remote context.

00:03:31.510 --> 00:03:34.320
So we waited, it took a minute, but here we.

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:35.870
Are, I'm delighted.

00:03:35.870 --> 00:03:40.870
Yeah, and it's wild because we go way back.

00:03:41.330 --> 00:03:45.750
Like I met you first week of freshman year at Stanford,

00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:48.995
we were both 18, 17, something like that.

00:03:48.995 --> 00:03:53.460
I briefly dated your freshman roommate at Brander,

00:03:55.270 --> 00:03:57.320
where I spent a ton of time in that dorm.

00:03:58.220 --> 00:04:03.220
And what's so, the more I reflect on your story and my own,

00:04:03.990 --> 00:04:07.090
what's interesting is that we're very different people.

00:04:07.090 --> 00:04:11.230
We have divergent backgrounds and experiences, of course,

00:04:11.230 --> 00:04:14.270
but here's the thing, I mean, irrespective

00:04:14.270 --> 00:04:18.300
of meeting at 18 and we weren't like friends in college,

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:21.325
but I knew you and I've sort of followed you over the years.

00:04:21.325 --> 00:04:24.820
We're both Gen Xers who grew up

00:04:24.820 --> 00:04:28.070
with very achievement-oriented parents.

00:04:28.070 --> 00:04:30.780
And that made us sort of go getters

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:33.740
among a generation of the, kind of forgotten latchkey kids.

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:36.681
That is the hallmark of what it means to be Gen X.

00:04:36.681 --> 00:04:39.760
We're both hypercompetitive, people pleasers.

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:40.790
What?

00:04:40.790 --> 00:04:41.630
Right?

00:04:41.630 --> 00:04:42.834
Yes.
Yes.

00:04:42.834 --> 00:04:45.990
And, and those tendencies both led us

00:04:45.990 --> 00:04:47.940
into corporate law careers,

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:50.460
the misery that that produces.

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:52.550
You got out earlier than I did,

00:04:54.280 --> 00:04:56.680
but that kind of overlap in our Venn diagram,

00:04:56.680 --> 00:04:57.640
is interesting.
I agree.

00:04:57.640 --> 00:05:00.230
It's been really cool to kind of watch you evolve

00:05:00.230 --> 00:05:05.230
over the years and step into this position of authority

00:05:05.560 --> 00:05:08.180
on the subject matter that we're gonna talk about today.

00:05:08.180 --> 00:05:09.280
Awesome.
Yeah.

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:10.730
Thanks for noticing all that.

00:05:10.730 --> 00:05:12.340
Yeah.
You know, you were this,

00:05:12.340 --> 00:05:15.289
you were part of that cadre of athletes

00:05:15.289 --> 00:05:19.680
who came by and there was this aura surrounding y'all

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:22.630
because the program you were part of was elite

00:05:22.630 --> 00:05:25.130
and we all knew it and respected it.

00:05:25.130 --> 00:05:28.765
You know, Stanford had that unique blend of irreverence

00:05:28.765 --> 00:05:32.670
and high achievement of athletics and academics.

00:05:32.670 --> 00:05:35.350
And here you were part of that group and so I did not,

00:05:35.350 --> 00:05:36.846
we weren't friends, as you said,

00:05:36.846 --> 00:05:39.560
but I definitely knew of you and knew that people

00:05:39.560 --> 00:05:40.865
respected and admired you.

00:05:40.865 --> 00:05:44.307
And I've loved watching your journey.

00:05:44.307 --> 00:05:48.720
I've loved watching the ways in which you have dared

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:51.760
to be vulnerable with the world,

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:54.568
with strangers, about your path.

00:05:54.568 --> 00:05:57.870
And I love how you're serving people.

00:05:57.870 --> 00:05:59.927
I appreciate that, that means a lot.

00:05:59.927 --> 00:06:02.820
It's been a journey for both us.

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:04.110
For sure.

00:06:04.110 --> 00:06:07.090
And I think what, one of the many things

00:06:07.090 --> 00:06:09.790
that I loved so much about Stanford,

00:06:09.790 --> 00:06:12.080
was this sense of possibility.

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:13.641
Like I grew up on the East Coast,

00:06:13.641 --> 00:06:18.641
and you know, was reared in a kind of checklist household,

00:06:18.840 --> 00:06:20.470
which is something you talk a lot about

00:06:20.470 --> 00:06:21.874
where it's like you do these certain things

00:06:21.874 --> 00:06:23.576
and you're on a certain track.

00:06:23.576 --> 00:06:28.576
And I was able to be successful within that construct,

00:06:30.090 --> 00:06:33.544
but it wasn't until I got to Stanford.

00:06:33.544 --> 00:06:37.330
And I wouldn't say Stanford had the most diverse

00:06:37.330 --> 00:06:40.560
student body, but there was a diversity of experiences.

00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:42.000
And I think layered on top of that,

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:43.830
this sense of possibility,

00:06:43.830 --> 00:06:46.280
like an environment that was conducive

00:06:46.280 --> 00:06:49.760
to trying to bring the best out of people

00:06:49.760 --> 00:06:52.760
in whatever it was that they were interested in,

00:06:52.760 --> 00:06:54.870
and it was the one place where, you know,

00:06:54.870 --> 00:06:56.940
I visited a lot of colleges and they would say,

00:06:56.940 --> 00:06:59.900
well, you can either excel as an athlete

00:06:59.900 --> 00:07:01.300
or you can excel academically.

00:07:01.300 --> 00:07:02.840
You're not gonna really be able to do both.

00:07:02.840 --> 00:07:03.680
You're gonna have to choose.

00:07:03.680 --> 00:07:05.590
And Stanford was like, no, you can do both.

00:07:05.590 --> 00:07:08.014
Like let us help you figure out how to make that happen.

00:07:08.014 --> 00:07:12.180
And I just, that, maybe it's a California sensibility,

00:07:12.180 --> 00:07:15.500
I don't know, but I just really fell in love with that.

00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:18.303
And I loved being a student there, even though, you know,

00:07:18.303 --> 00:07:22.050
it, you know, I can't say I made the most

00:07:22.050 --> 00:07:23.065
of that experience.

00:07:23.065 --> 00:07:25.330
Well, we all have our regrets about college,

00:07:25.330 --> 00:07:26.870
is probably not a person listening

00:07:26.870 --> 00:07:28.344
if they went to college who doesn't.

00:07:28.344 --> 00:07:31.220
You know, when I think about that special blend at Stanford,

00:07:31.220 --> 00:07:33.980
Rich, I think there's a profound respect

00:07:33.980 --> 00:07:35.146
for the individual there.

00:07:35.146 --> 00:07:38.500
And yeah, it is a place that contends

00:07:38.500 --> 00:07:39.800
with stereotypes and whatnot,

00:07:39.800 --> 00:07:42.650
but the dominant stereotype nationally,

00:07:42.650 --> 00:07:46.120
that it is not possible to excel as a scholar

00:07:46.120 --> 00:07:50.307
and as an athlete, that stereotype is shattered at Stanford.

00:07:50.307 --> 00:07:54.620
And, I think that's part of the magnificence of the place.

00:07:56.190 --> 00:07:58.885
You spent many years as Dean of Freshmen there.

00:07:58.885 --> 00:08:03.885
And in that capacity, I can only imagine the experiences

00:08:04.250 --> 00:08:07.380
that you had, not so much dealing with the kids,

00:08:07.380 --> 00:08:08.938
but having to deal with the parents,

00:08:08.938 --> 00:08:11.180
which has obviously, you know, of course,

00:08:11.180 --> 00:08:12.970
informed the books that you've written

00:08:12.970 --> 00:08:16.400
and given you this perspective on parenting.

00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:17.309
Yeah, absolutely.

00:08:17.309 --> 00:08:22.309
A real surprise, frankly, I got there actually in '98,

00:08:22.520 --> 00:08:23.933
I left law in '98,

00:08:23.933 --> 00:08:26.210
became the Associate Dean for Student Affairs

00:08:26.210 --> 00:08:28.360
at the law school for two years.

00:08:28.360 --> 00:08:29.780
Spent a couple years in the president's office

00:08:29.780 --> 00:08:33.530
and then said, hey, I think we would be a better place

00:08:33.530 --> 00:08:36.448
as a world-class research university.

00:08:36.448 --> 00:08:39.200
If we had a tiny office dedicated

00:08:39.200 --> 00:08:41.590
to the needs of our newest,

00:08:41.590 --> 00:08:45.128
to the needs of those at the very bottom of the totem pole.

00:08:45.128 --> 00:08:47.883
And they said, yeah, go give it a try, you know,

00:08:47.883 --> 00:08:50.796
give you a dilapidated office, someone has vacated.

00:08:50.796 --> 00:08:54.350
I pitched, you know, myself to apple computer,

00:08:54.350 --> 00:08:57.260
for some computers and made a go of it.

00:08:57.260 --> 00:09:02.260
And, you know, our ethos, our philosophy was,

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:04.950
I wanna be sure, we wanna be sure

00:09:04.950 --> 00:09:06.934
everybody is not just admitted,

00:09:06.934 --> 00:09:08.890
but knows they've been let in.

00:09:08.890 --> 00:09:13.480
And it really speaks to the potential exclusion

00:09:13.480 --> 00:09:15.490
of myriad folks who for whatever reason,

00:09:15.490 --> 00:09:20.490
don't feel entitled to be in the center of the page.

00:09:20.709 --> 00:09:24.720
And so to try to lead with a philosophy of you belong here,

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:28.090
you matter to us, you're not a nameless, faceless number,

00:09:28.090 --> 00:09:30.850
your journey is of relevance to us.

00:09:30.850 --> 00:09:32.850
Your needs matter.

00:09:32.850 --> 00:09:36.110
We are here to try to be alongside you

00:09:36.110 --> 00:09:38.310
as you begin to figure yourself out.

00:09:38.310 --> 00:09:40.300
That was kind of the big picture,

00:09:40.300 --> 00:09:41.170
purpose of what we're doing.

00:09:41.170 --> 00:09:45.240
And yeah, in those years of this sort of in 2002,

00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:46.341
when I began in that role,

00:09:46.341 --> 00:09:49.070
we had begun to see on the campus,

00:09:49.070 --> 00:09:50.696
maybe four to six years earlier,

00:09:50.696 --> 00:09:52.590
parents doing unusual things.

00:09:52.590 --> 00:09:55.320
parents, you know, needing to talk to a faculty member

00:09:55.320 --> 00:09:58.920
about a grade or parents wanting to register their quote,

00:09:58.920 --> 00:10:00.511
air quote, child for class.

00:10:00.511 --> 00:10:04.444
And in the early years of it, Rich, we giggled.

00:10:04.444 --> 00:10:07.210
I'd get a call or an email,

00:10:07.210 --> 00:10:09.770
and you know, very politely explained to the parent,

00:10:09.770 --> 00:10:14.000
I'm sorry, I can't give you your son or daughter's password.

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:15.190
We can't give them to third parties.

00:10:15.190 --> 00:10:17.170
Oh, I'm not a third party, I'm his mom.

00:10:17.170 --> 00:10:19.540
Well, no, actually you are, you know.

00:10:19.540 --> 00:10:22.267
Or it's too hard or he's so busy or she's so busy.

00:10:22.267 --> 00:10:25.440
And you know, I'd be polite and respectful

00:10:25.440 --> 00:10:26.610
and articulate the rules

00:10:26.610 --> 00:10:28.931
and then I'd hang up or hit send on the email

00:10:28.931 --> 00:10:31.270
and go down the hall and find a colleague

00:10:31.270 --> 00:10:32.310
and relay the story.

00:10:32.310 --> 00:10:35.727
Like you're not gonna believe this, parents so crazy.

00:10:35.727 --> 00:10:38.520
And then they really increased in number.

00:10:38.520 --> 00:10:42.180
So it became less, this sort of one-off weird parent

00:10:42.180 --> 00:10:44.848
you joked about, this would have been the early 2000s.

00:10:44.848 --> 00:10:46.560
And as the numbers grew,

00:10:46.560 --> 00:10:48.586
it really became a population of people

00:10:48.586 --> 00:10:50.980
We had to take seriously and contend with.

00:10:50.980 --> 00:10:53.580
It was clear that childhood had changed.

00:10:53.580 --> 00:10:56.980
So parents were arriving differently on campus.

00:10:56.980 --> 00:10:59.250
They were arriving and staying,

00:10:59.250 --> 00:11:02.130
they weren't just dropping you off or helping you move in.

00:11:02.130 --> 00:11:04.340
They felt they had a role to play

00:11:04.340 --> 00:11:06.870
in the day-to-day management of life,

00:11:06.870 --> 00:11:08.327
in the life of their child.

00:11:08.327 --> 00:11:10.500
And I found myself thinking, you know,

00:11:10.500 --> 00:11:13.590
this child could be in the Marines,

00:11:13.590 --> 00:11:14.700
but here they are, you know,

00:11:14.700 --> 00:11:16.160
not putting their lives in danger.

00:11:16.160 --> 00:11:18.770
They're at a well-resourced university,

00:11:18.770 --> 00:11:20.810
which you're paying a tremendous amount

00:11:20.810 --> 00:11:22.268
for them to attend, quite likely.

00:11:22.268 --> 00:11:24.390
What are you so afraid of, parent?

00:11:24.390 --> 00:11:27.197
Why is it that you think you need to be the one

00:11:27.197 --> 00:11:29.380
to have the run of the mill conversation

00:11:29.380 --> 00:11:30.355
with a faculty member?

00:11:30.355 --> 00:11:32.500
To get involved if there's a roommate dispute,

00:11:32.500 --> 00:11:36.010
to attend an overseas studies orientation,

00:11:36.010 --> 00:11:38.360
'cause you don't think your child can go abroad

00:11:38.360 --> 00:11:39.496
unless you've had the orientation

00:11:39.496 --> 00:11:42.540
so that we know how we can be successful abroad.

00:11:42.540 --> 00:11:46.124
We listened to the pronouns changing.

00:11:46.124 --> 00:11:49.228
It was we're going abroad.

00:11:49.228 --> 00:11:51.999
We've gotten into this seminar.

00:11:51.999 --> 00:11:53.950
So those were some the changes I noticed,

00:11:53.950 --> 00:11:56.680
and my job was to root for young people to thrive.

00:11:56.680 --> 00:11:59.070
And I could see that those who were over-managed

00:11:59.070 --> 00:12:02.520
were lacking in agency,

00:12:02.520 --> 00:12:05.230
And I found myself asking, hey kid,

00:12:05.230 --> 00:12:06.714
have you ever made a choice?

00:12:06.714 --> 00:12:09.840
Have they let you make a choice?

00:12:09.840 --> 00:12:13.869
Or you just incredibly good at doing what you're told?

00:12:13.869 --> 00:12:15.200
And it saddened me.

00:12:15.200 --> 00:12:18.900
Yeah, produces a certain handicap where the child,

00:12:18.900 --> 00:12:20.790
not only lacks self-efficacy,

00:12:20.790 --> 00:12:22.646
but that produces lower self-esteem

00:12:22.646 --> 00:12:26.480
and a lower sense of their own inherent capabilities.

00:12:26.480 --> 00:12:29.340
And certainly, you know, Stanford is your experience,

00:12:29.340 --> 00:12:33.150
but this is not, you know, endemic simply to Stanford.

00:12:33.150 --> 00:12:36.350
This is a nationwide, if not a global thing

00:12:37.590 --> 00:12:40.180
that is so fascinating.

00:12:40.180 --> 00:12:45.117
And I feel like has only continued to metastasize.

00:12:45.117 --> 00:12:47.940
So the catch all kind of umbrella phrase for this

00:12:47.940 --> 00:12:51.757
is helicopter parenting, of course,

00:12:51.757 --> 00:12:56.390
or, you know, the tiger mom phenomenon.

00:12:56.390 --> 00:13:00.610
What is your sense of how this started

00:13:00.610 --> 00:13:03.250
and how we got to this situation?

00:13:04.570 --> 00:13:05.403
Very interestingly,

00:13:05.403 --> 00:13:07.160
there were a set of changes happening

00:13:07.160 --> 00:13:08.848
in this country in the mid '80s

00:13:08.848 --> 00:13:12.500
that conspired to in the aggregate change all of childhood.

00:13:12.500 --> 00:13:15.342
So in no particular order, Rich, five things.

00:13:15.342 --> 00:13:19.460
Stranger danger was born with a made for TV movie, Adam,

00:13:19.460 --> 00:13:22.539
about the abduction and murder of Adam Walsh in 1983.

00:13:22.539 --> 00:13:25.780
And everybody watched it, there was no internet.

00:13:25.780 --> 00:13:27.890
All of your entertainment came through the big screen

00:13:27.890 --> 00:13:29.880
or the small screen or your headphones.

00:13:31.120 --> 00:13:32.410
Everybody tuned in for that show

00:13:32.410 --> 00:13:34.150
And it scared the bejesus out of everybody.

00:13:34.150 --> 00:13:36.270
So stranger danger was born '83.

00:13:36.270 --> 00:13:38.960
And then that guy's dad became the host

00:13:38.960 --> 00:13:41.202
of what was the show that he did-

00:13:41.202 --> 00:13:42.640
"America's Most Wanted."

00:13:42.640 --> 00:13:43.473
Exactly.

00:13:43.473 --> 00:13:44.308
Yeah, John Walsh, exactly.

00:13:44.308 --> 00:13:47.180
And missing kids began appearing on,

00:13:47.180 --> 00:13:49.211
photos began appearing on milk cartons.

00:13:49.211 --> 00:13:52.560
And yes, occasionally children were abducted by strangers.

00:13:52.560 --> 00:13:54.319
And it is of course, as we know, as parents,

00:13:54.319 --> 00:13:56.840
the most horrific thing we can imagine,

00:13:56.840 --> 00:13:59.330
and yet children are more likely to die

00:13:59.330 --> 00:14:00.690
at the hands of a family member

00:14:00.690 --> 00:14:02.170
than they are at the hands of a stranger.

00:14:02.170 --> 00:14:04.091
They're more likely to die as a passenger in a car,

00:14:04.091 --> 00:14:06.403
yet we drive them in cars constantly

00:14:06.403 --> 00:14:08.738
to get them to and from their activities.

00:14:08.738 --> 00:14:10.830
So we reshaped childhood

00:14:10.830 --> 00:14:14.620
around the very, very, very infinitesimal likelihood

00:14:14.620 --> 00:14:16.972
of a stranger harming our children.

00:14:16.972 --> 00:14:19.910
And we can really trace that back to that movie.

00:14:19.910 --> 00:14:23.480
"Adam," 1983, the play date was born in 1984.

00:14:23.480 --> 00:14:25.380
Kids used to play with each other.

00:14:25.380 --> 00:14:27.100
We found each other, right, Rich?

00:14:27.100 --> 00:14:28.660
It was like, where are the bikes?

00:14:28.660 --> 00:14:29.700
That's where my friends are.

00:14:29.700 --> 00:14:30.850
That was a Gen X childhood.

00:14:30.850 --> 00:14:32.189
That changed.

00:14:32.189 --> 00:14:35.650
Parents started arranging play with other parents,

00:14:35.650 --> 00:14:36.910
deciding with whom and when,

00:14:36.910 --> 00:14:39.870
but also watching over it hovering, micromanaging.

00:14:39.870 --> 00:14:41.330
Are they playing with the right things?

00:14:41.330 --> 00:14:42.495
Are they bored, let me help them.

00:14:42.495 --> 00:14:46.320
The self-esteem movement, give them ribbons and trophies,

00:14:46.320 --> 00:14:49.330
certificates and awards just for playing soccer

00:14:49.330 --> 00:14:52.240
or just for swimming, rather for being any good at it.

00:14:52.240 --> 00:14:54.926
The notion that we need to always praise, great job, Billy,

00:14:54.926 --> 00:14:56.396
you slid down the slide, great job.

00:14:56.396 --> 00:14:58.590
He didn't hit jack, you know.

00:14:59.450 --> 00:15:03.060
A nation at risk was published saying American teenagers

00:15:03.060 --> 00:15:05.360
needed to be, you know, more high achieving

00:15:05.360 --> 00:15:07.250
vis-a-vis their international counterparts.

00:15:07.250 --> 00:15:09.850
We needed more testing, more teaching to the test.

00:15:09.850 --> 00:15:13.000
All of these things meant that childhood

00:15:13.000 --> 00:15:15.507
was now watched and managed by parents.

00:15:15.507 --> 00:15:18.937
We also got safer in cars with seatbelt laws

00:15:18.937 --> 00:15:21.708
and car seat laws and bike helmet laws, and bicycles.

00:15:21.708 --> 00:15:24.244
We got safer in our transportation,

00:15:24.244 --> 00:15:27.810
it led to this mindset of, you can put a helmet on them,

00:15:27.810 --> 00:15:30.250
always you can bubble-wrap every aspect

00:15:30.250 --> 00:15:32.940
of their environment, prepare the road for the kid,

00:15:32.940 --> 00:15:34.760
instead of prepare the kid for the road.

00:15:34.760 --> 00:15:35.748
All of those things happened

00:15:35.748 --> 00:15:37.511
in the span of three to five years.

00:15:37.511 --> 00:15:41.130
The first kids to come to college on mask with parents

00:15:41.130 --> 00:15:43.590
who couldn't let go in the late '90s,

00:15:43.590 --> 00:15:47.328
were the first have been subjected to play dates in 1984.

00:15:47.328 --> 00:15:49.700
And it, and why does it persist?

00:15:49.700 --> 00:15:50.743
'Cause it works, Rich.

00:15:50.743 --> 00:15:53.570
When you stand next to the rock wall

00:15:53.570 --> 00:15:55.930
and prevent your kid from falling

00:15:55.930 --> 00:15:59.450
onto the plastic woodchips, they go unharmed.

00:15:59.450 --> 00:16:00.838
So you've saved them in that moment.

00:16:00.838 --> 00:16:03.520
But over the longterm, your kid hasn't developed

00:16:03.520 --> 00:16:05.360
that kinesthetic sense of how their body works

00:16:05.360 --> 00:16:08.610
or of how to do something different or better next time.

00:16:08.610 --> 00:16:10.920
So short-term win, long-term loss.

00:16:10.920 --> 00:16:12.447
Right, and on top of that,

00:16:12.447 --> 00:16:16.920
it's all set up within the construct of performance, right.

00:16:16.920 --> 00:16:19.810
How do I set my kid up to get into the best college?

00:16:19.810 --> 00:16:22.130
Like that is the ultimate end game.

00:16:22.130 --> 00:16:26.120
And so when you're looking at short-term gains,

00:16:26.120 --> 00:16:28.750
it's all oriented around academic performance,

00:16:28.750 --> 00:16:32.209
making sure that they have the appropriate extracurriculars

00:16:32.209 --> 00:16:33.680
and the only way to ensure

00:16:33.680 --> 00:16:36.520
that is this level of over-involvement.

00:16:36.520 --> 00:16:38.990
But, of course, even in the successful cases

00:16:38.990 --> 00:16:41.286
where a kid ends up at Stanford,

00:16:41.286 --> 00:16:43.678
that kid ends up knocking on your door,

00:16:43.678 --> 00:16:48.190
you know, having all kinds of, you know, mental issues

00:16:48.190 --> 00:16:52.420
or self-esteem issues or, you know, body dysmorphia,

00:16:52.420 --> 00:16:54.460
I'm sure it shows up in a million different ways

00:16:54.460 --> 00:16:57.720
where they're handicapped and unable to really figure out

00:16:57.720 --> 00:17:01.310
how to live and exist and thrive on their own.

00:17:01.310 --> 00:17:02.210
That's exactly right.

00:17:02.210 --> 00:17:04.920
So we've done the thinking for them, the planning for them,

00:17:04.920 --> 00:17:07.750
the troubleshooting, the fixing,

00:17:07.750 --> 00:17:10.200
the managing, the handling all in further,

00:17:10.200 --> 00:17:11.110
and some of these outcomes,

00:17:11.110 --> 00:17:13.440
we think lead to a successful life.

00:17:13.440 --> 00:17:15.781
And it's almost like we take our helicopter rotors

00:17:15.781 --> 00:17:18.620
and we lift them in the helicopter

00:17:18.620 --> 00:17:21.540
and arrive them at the future we have in mind.

00:17:21.540 --> 00:17:23.071
And then we can say, look, you've arrived.

00:17:23.071 --> 00:17:24.060
You're here.

00:17:24.060 --> 00:17:27.330
And then the kid is bewildered at the place of arrival,

00:17:27.330 --> 00:17:31.460
because they haven't done the heavy-lifting.

00:17:31.460 --> 00:17:32.310
They've certainly worked hard.

00:17:32.310 --> 00:17:33.740
It's not to say that kids aren't hardworking

00:17:33.740 --> 00:17:36.880
in this context, but they've been so over-managed

00:17:36.880 --> 00:17:39.020
and over helped, they really are unfamiliar

00:17:39.020 --> 00:17:39.853
with their own selves.

00:17:39.853 --> 00:17:41.520
And in my first book, how to reason at all,

00:17:41.520 --> 00:17:45.720
I call it "Existential Impotence: Unfamiliar With the Self."

00:17:47.405 --> 00:17:49.932
My sense is that there's a pendulum that swings

00:17:49.932 --> 00:17:54.932
and that's that pendulum swings generation to generation

00:17:55.930 --> 00:17:58.830
where parents end up parenting and opposition

00:17:58.830 --> 00:18:01.110
to the way that they were parented

00:18:01.110 --> 00:18:03.050
to ensure that their kid gets

00:18:03.050 --> 00:18:05.110
what they feel like they didn't get, right.

00:18:05.110 --> 00:18:06.560
So if you have the Gen Xers,

00:18:06.560 --> 00:18:07.880
who were the latchkey kids

00:18:07.880 --> 00:18:10.038
who were completely unattended to,

00:18:10.038 --> 00:18:12.620
they're gonna be the ones who are more likely

00:18:12.620 --> 00:18:14.740
to show up and over-parents.

00:18:14.740 --> 00:18:17.210
Is that how you feel like it works

00:18:17.210 --> 00:18:19.410
or have we arrived in a situation now

00:18:19.410 --> 00:18:22.000
where the pendulum has kind of locked in this place

00:18:22.000 --> 00:18:26.320
where parents are fixated in an unhealthy way

00:18:26.320 --> 00:18:29.240
on how their kids are doing and performing.

00:18:29.240 --> 00:18:31.870
I certainly agree with you that many of us

00:18:31.870 --> 00:18:35.790
want to parent either the opposite of how we were raised,

00:18:35.790 --> 00:18:37.590
or we appreciate how we were raised,

00:18:37.590 --> 00:18:39.052
and we wanna do it the exact same way.

00:18:39.052 --> 00:18:41.453
We don't seem to have room for nuance.

00:18:41.453 --> 00:18:44.580
I wanna point out that it was the Baby Boomers

00:18:44.580 --> 00:18:46.840
that started the helicopter parenting phenomenon.

00:18:46.840 --> 00:18:51.090
We Gen Xers inherited it because you're in a community

00:18:51.090 --> 00:18:53.320
and you see how other parents are doing their kids' homework

00:18:53.320 --> 00:18:54.340
in the fourth grade, in the seventh grade.

00:18:54.340 --> 00:18:56.200
Well, how are you not gonna do your kid's homework?

00:18:56.200 --> 00:18:58.320
'Cause you need your kid to keep up, right?

00:18:58.320 --> 00:19:00.450
So we arrived as parents in an environment

00:19:00.450 --> 00:19:03.539
where helicopter parenting was already in many communities.

00:19:03.539 --> 00:19:07.147
The norm, and I find it ironic that Baby Boomers

00:19:07.147 --> 00:19:09.130
who questioned authority,

00:19:09.130 --> 00:19:11.772
I mean, that was their bumper sticker.

00:19:11.772 --> 00:19:15.100
When they were young, they were now questioning authority

00:19:15.100 --> 00:19:16.830
on behalf of their own kids.

00:19:16.830 --> 00:19:20.010
They had forgotten that they created and matured

00:19:20.010 --> 00:19:21.764
their own voice by using it.

00:19:21.764 --> 00:19:25.340
And instead they sort of treated their own kids

00:19:25.340 --> 00:19:26.670
like little pets and projects

00:19:26.670 --> 00:19:28.513
that they would continually question authority

00:19:28.513 --> 00:19:30.110
on their behalf.

00:19:30.110 --> 00:19:34.960
With Gen X, you know, many of us can appreciate

00:19:34.960 --> 00:19:37.050
the freedoms of that latchkey childhood.

00:19:37.050 --> 00:19:39.590
Yes, there were examples that were neglectful

00:19:39.590 --> 00:19:41.646
and harmful and none of us want to replicate that.

00:19:41.646 --> 00:19:45.316
But you see in the Gen X memes on social media,

00:19:45.316 --> 00:19:49.030
the kind of lauding, the pride of like, you know,

00:19:49.030 --> 00:19:50.340
look what I was able to do.

00:19:50.340 --> 00:19:51.797
Look what my childhood entailed.

00:19:51.797 --> 00:19:53.280
There's pride in that.

00:19:53.280 --> 00:19:55.760
And I hope that Gen Xers listening will ask themselves,

00:19:55.760 --> 00:19:58.514
why am I not offering that same degree of freedom

00:19:58.514 --> 00:19:59.710
to my own child?

00:19:59.710 --> 00:20:02.540
Why do I want less for them?

00:20:02.540 --> 00:20:05.270
The justification that that the parent will make

00:20:05.270 --> 00:20:08.315
is that this is all for the best,

00:20:08.315 --> 00:20:10.620
all in the best interest of the child.

00:20:10.620 --> 00:20:12.070
Like that's all that I care about.

00:20:12.070 --> 00:20:14.418
I just wanna set my kid up for success,

00:20:14.418 --> 00:20:19.418
but beneath that, what you find is a really unhealthy

00:20:19.920 --> 00:20:21.760
level of inmeshment.

00:20:21.760 --> 00:20:24.541
This like projection onto their child,

00:20:24.541 --> 00:20:29.460
that their performance is a reflection of how well

00:20:29.460 --> 00:20:31.730
the parent parented the kid, right.

00:20:31.730 --> 00:20:34.080
You know, I grew up in Washington, DC,

00:20:34.080 --> 00:20:35.447
that's a community, you know,

00:20:35.447 --> 00:20:37.550
in upwardly mobile neighborhoods

00:20:37.550 --> 00:20:40.070
where everybody puts the bumper sticker on the car

00:20:40.070 --> 00:20:41.705
of the school that the kid goes to.

00:20:41.705 --> 00:20:46.240
And it's such an unhealthy thing where the parent

00:20:46.240 --> 00:20:49.660
is so wrapped up in how well the kid does,

00:20:49.660 --> 00:20:51.990
because they wanna be able to go to the cocktail party

00:20:51.990 --> 00:20:56.100
and say this, that or the other that reflects well on them.

00:20:56.100 --> 00:21:00.640
Right, so I talk about, we treat our kids

00:21:00.640 --> 00:21:02.550
like they're our bonsai trees.

00:21:02.550 --> 00:21:06.135
You know, the bonsai is such an exquisite creation

00:21:06.135 --> 00:21:07.860
of the gardener.

00:21:07.860 --> 00:21:09.810
The gardener decides the direction

00:21:09.810 --> 00:21:10.950
in which that tree will grow,

00:21:10.950 --> 00:21:12.530
which branches will be clipped

00:21:12.530 --> 00:21:15.130
and which will flourish and the shape of it.

00:21:15.130 --> 00:21:17.951
And it's a lovely creation.

00:21:17.951 --> 00:21:21.430
But at the end of the day, it is a possession

00:21:21.430 --> 00:21:22.710
of the gardener.

00:21:22.710 --> 00:21:24.420
It's something to be ooed and odd over,

00:21:24.420 --> 00:21:25.710
and people can come to a gardener and say,

00:21:25.710 --> 00:21:27.534
look what you've done, aren't you amazing?

00:21:27.534 --> 00:21:31.115
And you know, I'm super clear

00:21:31.115 --> 00:21:32.857
and let me say, as a caveat,

00:21:32.857 --> 00:21:34.990
I have over-parented my own two.

00:21:34.990 --> 00:21:36.430
They are 20 and 22.

00:21:36.430 --> 00:21:39.100
I am in this both as quote, unquote expert

00:21:39.100 --> 00:21:41.310
and as subject, okay.

00:21:42.780 --> 00:21:45.670
We now treat our kids like they're our bonsai trees

00:21:45.670 --> 00:21:50.090
that, you know, we need them to be glorious

00:21:50.090 --> 00:21:52.844
so that we can feel better about ourselves,

00:21:52.844 --> 00:21:54.560
and it's harmful.

00:21:54.560 --> 00:21:59.560
And it speaks to an unwellness in our psyche, in our spirit.

00:21:59.870 --> 00:22:03.170
How many of us are devoting countless hours

00:22:03.170 --> 00:22:07.060
to chauffeuring, concierging, helping with the homework,

00:22:07.060 --> 00:22:09.340
over-helping outright doing the homework,

00:22:09.340 --> 00:22:11.930
planning and fixing and managing our kids' lives.

00:22:11.930 --> 00:22:14.276
And we have a primary partner over there in the wings.

00:22:14.276 --> 00:22:16.980
And our relationship with that person

00:22:16.980 --> 00:22:19.770
is dwindling and diminishing and suffering

00:22:19.770 --> 00:22:21.850
'cause we're not watering that plant.

00:22:21.850 --> 00:22:23.750
We're not giving that relationship attention,

00:22:23.750 --> 00:22:26.640
the very relationship that might've created these children,

00:22:26.640 --> 00:22:30.890
you know, languishes because we are investing everything

00:22:30.890 --> 00:22:34.850
in the project that is making these children excellent

00:22:34.850 --> 00:22:37.630
so that we can feel that we have, you know,

00:22:37.630 --> 00:22:41.315
the right sort of accolades to boast about

00:22:41.315 --> 00:22:42.921
at a cocktail party.

00:22:42.921 --> 00:22:44.690
Yeah, I think it was in your TED talk

00:22:44.690 --> 00:22:46.650
where you said they're not bonsai trees,

00:22:46.650 --> 00:22:47.930
They're wildflowers.

00:22:47.930 --> 00:22:48.763
Yeah.

00:22:48.763 --> 00:22:49.596
Yeah.
Yeah.

00:22:49.596 --> 00:22:52.973
And you know, that image instantly comes to mind for me of,

00:22:52.973 --> 00:22:54.810
you know, being out on a trail

00:22:54.810 --> 00:22:56.154
somewhere in our beautiful California

00:22:56.154 --> 00:23:00.574
and taking in the majesty of a wild landscape,

00:23:00.574 --> 00:23:03.740
you know, that's who we, humans are.

00:23:03.740 --> 00:23:05.440
Yes, there are some rules and regulations

00:23:05.440 --> 00:23:06.441
we all need to abide by

00:23:06.441 --> 00:23:08.736
in order to play nicely with one another.

00:23:08.736 --> 00:23:13.736
But there's so much room for variants, for difference.

00:23:14.010 --> 00:23:16.800
We have, you know, some core things in common

00:23:16.800 --> 00:23:21.630
and yet each of one of us is this unique, precious thing.

00:23:21.630 --> 00:23:24.247
You know, I like the poet, Mary Oliver,

00:23:24.247 --> 00:23:28.634
is very much in my heart as I talk to young people,

00:23:28.634 --> 00:23:29.650
you know, they'll say,

00:23:29.650 --> 00:23:33.020
how do I tell my parents I don't wanna be a,

00:23:33.020 --> 00:23:35.436
whatever the parents have decided is the right path,

00:23:35.436 --> 00:23:36.737
the sort of tiger type.

00:23:36.737 --> 00:23:39.820
How do I, you know, and I'm trying to incentivize

00:23:39.820 --> 00:23:41.900
a young person to claim their own agency.

00:23:41.900 --> 00:23:44.500
And one of the phrases I use is from Mary Oliver,

00:23:44.500 --> 00:23:45.499
who said in one of her poems,

00:23:45.499 --> 00:23:47.650
what is it, tell me, she said,

00:23:47.650 --> 00:23:50.120
"Tell me, what is it you plan to do

00:23:50.120 --> 00:23:52.919
"with this one wild, precious life."

00:23:52.919 --> 00:23:54.420
It is wild.

00:23:54.420 --> 00:23:55.253
It should be.

00:23:55.253 --> 00:23:56.250
It is precious.

00:23:56.250 --> 00:23:58.740
And unlike the bonsai tree that requires

00:23:58.740 --> 00:24:02.190
that pruning and that level of deep involvement,

00:24:02.190 --> 00:24:04.000
the wildflower is fine on its own.

00:24:04.000 --> 00:24:06.130
Maybe you gotta water it a little bit once in a while,

00:24:06.130 --> 00:24:07.430
but you walk away from it-

00:24:07.430 --> 00:24:08.263
That's right.

00:24:08.263 --> 00:24:09.096
And let it do its thing.

00:24:09.096 --> 00:24:09.931
Let it become what it is.

00:24:09.931 --> 00:24:12.290
And that's terrifying for a lot of parents.

00:24:12.290 --> 00:24:13.377
And as a parent of four, you know,

00:24:13.377 --> 00:24:15.160
I brought up against that as well.

00:24:15.160 --> 00:24:17.865
I appreciate how you bring your own experiences

00:24:17.865 --> 00:24:18.940
into your writing.

00:24:18.940 --> 00:24:21.630
And you're very frank about where you've done it right,

00:24:21.630 --> 00:24:24.390
and where you still find yourself, you know,

00:24:24.390 --> 00:24:28.060
tripping up and repeating patterns that you learned

00:24:28.060 --> 00:24:32.431
from your parents and how unconscious those impulses can be.

00:24:32.431 --> 00:24:35.298
Absolutely, you know, I'm a memoirist.

00:24:35.298 --> 00:24:40.298
I try to write non-fiction because I believe in the beauty

00:24:42.300 --> 00:24:46.230
that can come when we can dare to be vulnerable

00:24:46.230 --> 00:24:47.494
about our lived experience.

00:24:47.494 --> 00:24:52.494
I know that a deep, profound, meaningful connection

00:24:53.620 --> 00:24:57.690
with other humans is available when we can open up

00:24:57.690 --> 00:25:00.280
or when we can pull the facade or mask away

00:25:00.280 --> 00:25:02.830
and say, you know what, I did this.

00:25:02.830 --> 00:25:06.610
And I try to tell the truth to the extent I can bear it.

00:25:06.610 --> 00:25:07.750
There are still plenty of truths

00:25:07.750 --> 00:25:11.080
I can't yet bear to tell, but I'm only 53.

00:25:11.080 --> 00:25:12.777
We'll see if I get more years,

00:25:12.777 --> 00:25:14.092
who knows, I may write more.

00:25:14.092 --> 00:25:18.430
It gives people permission to express,

00:25:18.430 --> 00:25:21.270
or at least contend with their own vulnerabilities.

00:25:21.270 --> 00:25:24.250
Like if you're creating a space in which,

00:25:24.250 --> 00:25:25.630
look, I can say this,

00:25:25.630 --> 00:25:28.230
maybe you can bring to the surface

00:25:28.230 --> 00:25:30.310
something that you've been repressing or hiding.

00:25:30.310 --> 00:25:31.835
And I think we need more of that now.

00:25:31.835 --> 00:25:36.310
And you know, if there is a fear with Gen Z

00:25:36.310 --> 00:25:38.621
and the younger generation, the digital natives,

00:25:38.621 --> 00:25:43.140
it's that they're so accustomed to communicating

00:25:43.140 --> 00:25:47.110
in a digital space and yet completely handicapped

00:25:47.110 --> 00:25:49.160
when it comes to how to actually interact

00:25:49.160 --> 00:25:52.210
with another individual in three-dimensional space

00:25:52.210 --> 00:25:53.240
in real time.

00:25:53.240 --> 00:25:55.773
Absolutely, one-on-one, in-person.

00:25:55.773 --> 00:25:59.728
You know, in my new book which is aimed at helping

00:25:59.728 --> 00:26:00.810
young adults thrive or really helping anyone

00:26:00.810 --> 00:26:02.290
live their best life as an adult,

00:26:02.290 --> 00:26:04.400
I talk about humans being everything

00:26:04.400 --> 00:26:06.984
about human relationships at the end of the day,

00:26:06.984 --> 00:26:10.600
being the predictor or one of the greatest predictors

00:26:10.600 --> 00:26:11.531
of our longevity.

00:26:11.531 --> 00:26:14.160
I know you have done plenty of work on your own

00:26:14.160 --> 00:26:16.840
and you know plenty of people who have all kinds of wisdom

00:26:16.840 --> 00:26:18.354
about how to live and thrive.

00:26:18.354 --> 00:26:23.354
And certainly we know from the Harvard Grant Project,

00:26:23.790 --> 00:26:25.985
Harvard Grant study, that's lasted for decades,

00:26:25.985 --> 00:26:29.050
that it's not your cholesterol level at 50

00:26:29.050 --> 00:26:30.970
that determines whether you live a long life.

00:26:30.970 --> 00:26:32.548
It's the quality of your relationships at 50.

00:26:32.548 --> 00:26:35.740
And so I'm trying to help young people

00:26:35.740 --> 00:26:38.034
who haven't had a childhood

00:26:38.034 --> 00:26:40.800
where they just had to sit around with their friends

00:26:40.800 --> 00:26:42.491
and figure out what they wanted to do.

00:26:42.491 --> 00:26:45.809
And instead of had access to friends via technology,

00:26:45.809 --> 00:26:50.809
I'm trying to help them learn how to go deep with a person.

00:26:52.170 --> 00:26:53.690
What are the questions you ask

00:26:53.690 --> 00:26:56.620
when you're really seeking to know.

00:26:56.620 --> 00:26:59.000
How do you show up with your body and your mind

00:26:59.000 --> 00:27:01.590
and your energy, if you're really seeking

00:27:01.590 --> 00:27:05.897
to demonstrate care and concern and interest.

00:27:05.897 --> 00:27:08.610
These are the things, I think if I was a parent today

00:27:08.610 --> 00:27:12.290
of young ones, I would be really focused

00:27:12.290 --> 00:27:15.100
on helping them develop that emotional intelligence,

00:27:15.100 --> 00:27:18.250
those soft skills around interacting

00:27:18.250 --> 00:27:19.730
with your fellow humans.

00:27:19.730 --> 00:27:24.730
Yeah, it's so crucial, and yet our educational system

00:27:25.430 --> 00:27:28.424
is bereft of any of that type of education.

00:27:28.424 --> 00:27:30.410
And I think it requires

00:27:30.410 --> 00:27:32.200
a certain level of emotional maturity

00:27:32.200 --> 00:27:35.329
on behalf of parents to recognize

00:27:35.329 --> 00:27:38.470
the importance of that and to emphasize it.

00:27:38.470 --> 00:27:41.020
You know, I just reflect back on, you know,

00:27:41.020 --> 00:27:44.793
when I was in my early 20s, I didn't have the facility

00:27:44.793 --> 00:27:47.260
to have that type of conversation.

00:27:47.260 --> 00:27:50.352
And I often lament the fact that I was at this

00:27:50.352 --> 00:27:54.020
unbelievable institution and didn't make

00:27:54.020 --> 00:27:59.020
appropriate use of it to engage with my inner voice.

00:27:59.190 --> 00:28:00.940
Never once that I asked myself,

00:28:00.940 --> 00:28:02.180
what is it that I wanna be?

00:28:02.180 --> 00:28:05.135
I was on this track, this is what I was gonna do.

00:28:05.135 --> 00:28:09.930
This was what I was, you know, sort of set up to do.

00:28:09.930 --> 00:28:13.721
And didn't have any emotional intelligence

00:28:13.721 --> 00:28:17.480
to even ask myself what it is that, you know,

00:28:17.480 --> 00:28:18.810
makes me uniquely me,

00:28:18.810 --> 00:28:21.330
what gets all these questions that you ask in the book,

00:28:21.330 --> 00:28:23.460
like what gets you excited?

00:28:23.460 --> 00:28:25.100
What gets you out of bed in the morning?

00:28:25.100 --> 00:28:27.230
Like, what are the activities

00:28:27.230 --> 00:28:29.300
that you naturally gravitate towards?

00:28:29.300 --> 00:28:30.687
I was just on this habit trail.

00:28:30.687 --> 00:28:33.795
And I do remember, I mean, times are very different now.

00:28:33.795 --> 00:28:35.600
I do remember, and I'm interested

00:28:35.600 --> 00:28:37.058
in whether you have this experience.

00:28:37.058 --> 00:28:39.703
I remember going to, and I've told this story before,

00:28:39.703 --> 00:28:42.028
going to the Career Center at Stanford,

00:28:42.028 --> 00:28:44.295
'cause I was like, I dunno what I wanna do.

00:28:44.295 --> 00:28:47.670
And I remember there was a coffee table

00:28:47.670 --> 00:28:49.060
with some brochures on it

00:28:49.060 --> 00:28:51.020
and it was like Boston Consulting Group,

00:28:51.020 --> 00:28:54.140
Anderson Consulting, you know, Merrill Lynch.

00:28:54.140 --> 00:28:55.410
And that was kind of it.

00:28:56.620 --> 00:28:58.630
None of that, I was like, I don't even know what that is.

00:28:58.630 --> 00:29:00.010
Like what is consulting?

00:29:00.010 --> 00:29:00.972
Everybody seems to be interviewing

00:29:00.972 --> 00:29:02.760
with these consulting companies.

00:29:02.760 --> 00:29:05.330
Like what exactly am I supposed to consult on?

00:29:05.330 --> 00:29:06.430
I don't know anything.

00:29:07.790 --> 00:29:10.420
And so law school just sort of happened

00:29:10.420 --> 00:29:12.930
because I didn't know quite what to do.

00:29:12.930 --> 00:29:15.410
And that was the socially approved route.

00:29:16.440 --> 00:29:17.400
Yeah.

00:29:17.400 --> 00:29:19.470
Oh, rich, what was your major?

00:29:20.560 --> 00:29:21.393
Home bio.

00:29:21.393 --> 00:29:23.820
I started out as home bio and then I thought

00:29:23.820 --> 00:29:25.024
I would wanna to be a doctor.

00:29:25.024 --> 00:29:27.780
And I've often thought, you know, I kind of,

00:29:27.780 --> 00:29:30.280
I started partying too much and lost interest in that

00:29:30.280 --> 00:29:33.090
and pivoted to American studies, which I enjoyed.

00:29:33.090 --> 00:29:35.490
I was an American studies major.

00:29:35.490 --> 00:29:36.660
I'm sure we were in the same classes.

00:29:36.660 --> 00:29:37.570
I can't believe this.

00:29:37.570 --> 00:29:38.403
Yeah.

00:29:38.403 --> 00:29:41.420
Yeah, you know, yeah.

00:29:41.420 --> 00:29:43.270
So first of all, I'm feeling a lot of compassion

00:29:43.270 --> 00:29:47.520
for your 18 to 22-year-old self who didn't know,

00:29:47.520 --> 00:29:49.510
who didn't ask the right questions, so to speak,

00:29:49.510 --> 00:29:53.250
who wishes, maybe he could go back and do those

00:29:53.250 --> 00:29:55.350
or some of those opportunities over again.

00:29:56.404 --> 00:29:59.420
I think it's a pretty universal lament though.

00:29:59.420 --> 00:30:02.569
You know, at 18 to 22, as we now know your prefrontal cortex

00:30:02.569 --> 00:30:06.360
is just starting to get itself locked in.

00:30:07.625 --> 00:30:10.010
And the expectation that you should know at that time,

00:30:10.010 --> 00:30:11.960
I think is a really ridiculous burden

00:30:11.960 --> 00:30:13.440
that we place on young people.
It is ridiculous.

00:30:13.440 --> 00:30:15.997
And it's gotten worse because nowadays

00:30:15.997 --> 00:30:19.240
in any independent high school

00:30:19.240 --> 00:30:22.540
and any well-healed public high school in America today,

00:30:22.540 --> 00:30:27.540
a child is asked at ninth grade, what is your passion?

00:30:28.730 --> 00:30:31.460
Find your passion, find your passion, kid, quick.

00:30:31.460 --> 00:30:34.390
You know, like it's hiding on a bookshelf

00:30:34.390 --> 00:30:36.870
and if they just work hard enough, they can find it.

00:30:36.870 --> 00:30:37.703
Why?

00:30:37.703 --> 00:30:40.254
Because a college admissions dean wants to know about it

00:30:40.254 --> 00:30:42.562
in their senior year.

00:30:42.562 --> 00:30:45.810
In January of their senior year or preferably November,

00:30:45.810 --> 00:30:47.600
'cause we want you to apply early, right.

00:30:47.600 --> 00:30:51.595
Find your passion in pursuit of pleasing somebody else

00:30:51.595 --> 00:30:56.340
who is going to admit or deny a new opportunity to you.

00:30:56.340 --> 00:30:59.710
It's become this utilitarian perfunctory thing.

00:30:59.710 --> 00:31:02.400
I mean, I believe in passion and path and purpose

00:31:02.400 --> 00:31:04.890
and the rudder that steers you

00:31:04.890 --> 00:31:07.220
no matter which way the wind is going,

00:31:07.220 --> 00:31:08.520
that I know my why.

00:31:08.520 --> 00:31:09.640
That's what I think of as passionate.

00:31:09.640 --> 00:31:12.230
I'm just sort of really against the notion

00:31:12.230 --> 00:31:14.140
that every 18-year-old must have one

00:31:14.140 --> 00:31:17.070
outside of the fellow 18-year-old they're attracted to,

00:31:17.070 --> 00:31:18.520
I mean, that's a natural passion

00:31:18.520 --> 00:31:19.373
for someone at 18 to have.

00:31:19.373 --> 00:31:23.230
To know your life's pursuit at 18, I think is rare.

00:31:23.230 --> 00:31:26.880
Yeah, I mean, defacto, when you're that age,

00:31:26.880 --> 00:31:29.491
you've only been exposed to a limited number of experiences.

00:31:29.491 --> 00:31:32.290
So how could you possibly know that?

00:31:32.290 --> 00:31:36.720
I think a work that has been instrumental

00:31:36.720 --> 00:31:38.170
in helping me rethink that,

00:31:38.170 --> 00:31:39.970
and I think every young person should read

00:31:39.970 --> 00:31:41.316
is David Epstein's "Range"

00:31:41.316 --> 00:31:45.278
because we have this idea that a young person

00:31:45.278 --> 00:31:48.813
who toggles between many different things

00:31:48.813 --> 00:31:51.803
and can't quite commit and, you know,

00:31:51.803 --> 00:31:56.120
appears on the surface to sort of be lazy and disengaged.

00:31:56.120 --> 00:31:57.911
And somebody we would call a dilettante

00:31:57.911 --> 00:32:01.220
is actually just experimenting and exposing themselves

00:32:01.220 --> 00:32:05.497
to a number of experiences as part of that necessary process

00:32:05.497 --> 00:32:08.850
of either elimination or selection, right.

00:32:08.850 --> 00:32:10.150
So that book, I don't know if you've read it.

00:32:10.150 --> 00:32:11.190
Haven't.

00:32:11.190 --> 00:32:13.661
But it basically puts to the test

00:32:13.661 --> 00:32:18.661
the idea that high performers in all areas of life,

00:32:18.740 --> 00:32:22.320
from academia to sports, science, et cetera, art,

00:32:22.320 --> 00:32:25.050
were people who at a very young age splashed onto it

00:32:25.050 --> 00:32:26.950
and did the 10,000 hours thing,

00:32:26.950 --> 00:32:28.120
distinguished themselves,

00:32:28.120 --> 00:32:32.828
when in truth the reality is that these people

00:32:32.828 --> 00:32:35.260
are people who did lots of different things

00:32:35.260 --> 00:32:38.700
as a young person and have many, many experiences

00:32:38.700 --> 00:32:43.360
that then all inform the thing that they finally key in on

00:32:43.360 --> 00:32:45.880
and become an expert in.

00:32:45.880 --> 00:32:49.040
You know, I think I've done my 10,000 hours

00:32:49.040 --> 00:32:51.500
across three different careers,

00:32:51.500 --> 00:32:53.830
and more than 10,000 hours.

00:32:53.830 --> 00:32:57.730
It took me some time to tell myself

00:32:57.730 --> 00:33:00.840
the truth that I'm a people-person go damn it,

00:33:00.840 --> 00:33:02.080
and I'm gonna be a people person

00:33:02.080 --> 00:33:04.100
and I'm here to be a people person.

00:33:04.100 --> 00:33:04.971
I think when I was young,

00:33:04.971 --> 00:33:07.430
I thought the fact that I had people skills,

00:33:07.430 --> 00:33:10.070
I knew I had people skills when I was little

00:33:10.070 --> 00:33:12.557
people would come to me and ask me questions

00:33:12.557 --> 00:33:14.610
revealing vulnerability.

00:33:14.610 --> 00:33:16.040
They seem to value my perspective,

00:33:16.040 --> 00:33:18.170
they seem to feel safe with me.

00:33:18.170 --> 00:33:19.890
That has been true all my life

00:33:19.890 --> 00:33:21.270
as far back as I can remember.

00:33:21.270 --> 00:33:23.930
But I think, Rich, I thought that being a people person

00:33:23.930 --> 00:33:25.548
was somehow attached to my gender.

00:33:25.548 --> 00:33:27.465
I'm female, female-presenting,

00:33:27.465 --> 00:33:30.632
maybe it's a, you know, sort of female, girly thing.

00:33:30.632 --> 00:33:33.020
I didn't value it as a skill.

00:33:33.020 --> 00:33:35.080
We weren't speaking about soft skills or EQ

00:33:35.080 --> 00:33:36.612
when you and I were in college.

00:33:36.612 --> 00:33:38.670
I think I just discarded it.

00:33:38.670 --> 00:33:43.373
I was hell bent on being a left brain, analytical lawyer

00:33:43.373 --> 00:33:45.790
who was gonna have, you know, a profession

00:33:45.790 --> 00:33:48.290
that would allow me to make change in the world

00:33:48.290 --> 00:33:49.600
and help people.

00:33:49.600 --> 00:33:50.750
I knew I wanted to help people,

00:33:50.750 --> 00:33:54.950
but I wanted to do it in a way that society told me

00:33:54.950 --> 00:33:55.920
was the right way.

00:33:55.920 --> 00:33:58.676
Tap into your intellect, tap into your left brain.

00:33:58.676 --> 00:34:00.700
Whereas the right brain side of me

00:34:00.700 --> 00:34:05.090
and the very soft EQ side of me was really underserved,

00:34:05.090 --> 00:34:08.690
massively underserved when I chose corporate law,

00:34:08.690 --> 00:34:10.040
which of course was not a way to help people.

00:34:10.040 --> 00:34:12.180
It was a way to help me feel

00:34:12.180 --> 00:34:13.882
that I was meeting society's expectations.

00:34:13.882 --> 00:34:15.860
That was my people-pleasing side,

00:34:15.860 --> 00:34:17.940
as you alluded to at the start.

00:34:17.940 --> 00:34:22.800
And so finally in my misery, I came around to,

00:34:22.800 --> 00:34:24.130
you know what, I'm a people person.

00:34:24.130 --> 00:34:25.450
I need to do work, helping people.

00:34:25.450 --> 00:34:26.990
I need to figure that out, Julie,

00:34:26.990 --> 00:34:31.560
get back on the path that you know is your right path.

00:34:31.560 --> 00:34:34.560
And I keep iterating around to, okay,

00:34:34.560 --> 00:34:37.920
so what is the best way for me to try to help people now,

00:34:37.920 --> 00:34:39.830
now that I know what I know.

00:34:39.830 --> 00:34:43.080
I've been observing people for 53 years

00:34:43.080 --> 00:34:45.650
and in my work I'm continually trying

00:34:45.650 --> 00:34:48.488
to take whatever I've learned across those many hours

00:34:48.488 --> 00:34:50.990
of being immersed with humans,

00:34:50.990 --> 00:34:54.383
to try to turn that into something I can offer people back

00:34:54.383 --> 00:34:56.470
by way of assistance.

00:34:56.470 --> 00:34:59.800
Right, and perhaps all those different avenues

00:34:59.800 --> 00:35:03.310
that you pursued, all inform the,

00:35:03.310 --> 00:35:07.930
you know, the depth of the resonance

00:35:07.930 --> 00:35:09.680
that you can now share, right?

00:35:09.680 --> 00:35:11.840
It's not like, oh, I regret doing that.

00:35:11.840 --> 00:35:14.590
On some level, I'm sure those experiences contribute

00:35:14.590 --> 00:35:16.520
to the value that you can now share.

00:35:16.520 --> 00:35:17.960
100%.

00:35:17.960 --> 00:35:20.110
Yeah, for example I know doesn't matter

00:35:20.110 --> 00:35:21.432
how much money they pay you,

00:35:21.432 --> 00:35:23.230
if you're miserable in the work,

00:35:23.230 --> 00:35:25.660
the money will never be compensation enough.

00:35:25.660 --> 00:35:27.518
And we can hear that a million times,

00:35:27.518 --> 00:35:29.089
but until you experience it.

00:35:29.089 --> 00:35:30.020
That's right.

00:35:30.020 --> 00:35:30.853
It's different.

00:35:30.853 --> 00:35:32.820
But I'm also rooting for us not to have

00:35:32.820 --> 00:35:35.249
the high blood pressure before we leave the work

00:35:35.249 --> 00:35:37.330
or not to have our hair falling out

00:35:37.330 --> 00:35:38.683
before we decide to pivot.

00:35:38.683 --> 00:35:41.890
That's when we tend to give ourselves permission, right.

00:35:41.890 --> 00:35:44.046
I am dying here.

00:35:44.046 --> 00:35:45.800
We have that objective evidence

00:35:45.800 --> 00:35:46.860
that this isn't working for me.

00:35:46.860 --> 00:35:50.400
And that allows us to say to our dads and moms and friends

00:35:50.400 --> 00:35:53.047
and society like, okay, I couldn't take this.

00:35:53.047 --> 00:35:56.940
And I'm rooting for us all to find that truer north

00:35:56.940 --> 00:36:00.914
before the body starts to scream at us,

00:36:00.914 --> 00:36:03.320
stop, you're hurting me.

00:36:03.320 --> 00:36:06.940
What was the Rubicon for you with corporate law

00:36:06.940 --> 00:36:07.800
and getting out?

00:36:07.800 --> 00:36:10.300
It was the high blood pressure.

00:36:10.300 --> 00:36:14.840
I mean, nine months in, I had a knot in my stomach

00:36:14.840 --> 00:36:17.260
at 2:00 PM on a Sunday,

00:36:17.260 --> 00:36:19.050
knowing I had to go back.

00:36:19.050 --> 00:36:21.170
I loved the people, you know, the feeling.

00:36:21.170 --> 00:36:23.120
If you weren't at the office already-

00:36:23.120 --> 00:36:24.990
Yeah, I was gonna say it, right, exactly, exactly.

00:36:24.990 --> 00:36:26.790
Every lawyer would make that caveat.

00:36:27.800 --> 00:36:29.570
Let me be clear.

00:36:29.570 --> 00:36:32.130
I was at a great firm as far as firms go,

00:36:32.130 --> 00:36:33.223
great place, good people.

00:36:33.223 --> 00:36:34.280
You were at Cooley, right?

00:36:34.280 --> 00:36:35.235
I was at Cooley in Palo Alto.

00:36:35.235 --> 00:36:36.344
Where were you?

00:36:36.344 --> 00:36:39.802
I was at Littler Mendelson in San Francisco for a while.

00:36:39.802 --> 00:36:41.740
And then I moved down here

00:36:41.740 --> 00:36:43.770
to work in a entertainment litigation firms.

00:36:43.770 --> 00:36:46.880
Okay, I was at Cooley, then I was in the house at Intel,

00:36:46.880 --> 00:36:49.430
pam, pam, pam, pam, as a trademark lawyer,

00:36:49.430 --> 00:36:50.780
which is why I have to do that.

00:36:50.780 --> 00:36:53.280
So you took (indistinct) you had some self-awareness

00:36:53.280 --> 00:36:55.630
to know, like maybe the law firm thing isn't good enough,

00:36:55.630 --> 00:36:59.270
but that's the typical sort of sidestep,

00:36:59.270 --> 00:37:00.103
like, oh, in-house-

00:37:00.103 --> 00:37:02.750
Go in-house, the hours are better, right?

00:37:02.750 --> 00:37:03.970
But here's the thing.

00:37:03.970 --> 00:37:07.877
After nine months, I had the knot in the stomach

00:37:07.877 --> 00:37:09.300
on the weekend.

00:37:10.482 --> 00:37:14.010
I was going into my doctor to frankly,

00:37:14.010 --> 00:37:16.768
I think, try to get pregnant.

00:37:16.768 --> 00:37:19.710
And she discovered my blood pressure was high.

00:37:19.710 --> 00:37:22.110
And she wanted me to wear a cuff five times a day.

00:37:22.110 --> 00:37:24.530
And it was low in the morning and it was low at night

00:37:24.530 --> 00:37:25.823
and it was high at work.

00:37:25.823 --> 00:37:27.549
And so that was data.

00:37:27.549 --> 00:37:31.258
And I began to, I did this exercise with myself.

00:37:31.258 --> 00:37:34.400
I was, you know, sort of miserable out of my back porch

00:37:34.400 --> 00:37:37.650
and Menlo park one night it just,

00:37:37.650 --> 00:37:39.260
and mad at myself for being miserable.

00:37:39.260 --> 00:37:41.840
'Cause I'd done everything right, air quotes, right.

00:37:41.840 --> 00:37:43.950
The right college, the right law school,

00:37:43.950 --> 00:37:45.496
the right career, the right life,

00:37:45.496 --> 00:37:48.495
good money, mentor, great opportunity,

00:37:48.495 --> 00:37:51.673
like they were telling me, hey kid, you're good at this.

00:37:51.673 --> 00:37:54.840
And so I couldn't understand my own discomfort

00:37:54.840 --> 00:37:56.110
because I'd done everything right, so to speak.

00:37:56.110 --> 00:37:58.573
So I was impatient with myself

00:37:58.573 --> 00:38:01.820
and I had gone to law school to help people

00:38:01.820 --> 00:38:04.830
and now here I was miserable as a lawyer, you know,

00:38:04.830 --> 00:38:07.820
wanted to help those in misery now feeling misery myself.

00:38:07.820 --> 00:38:09.470
And I gave myself this sort of tongue lashing,

00:38:09.470 --> 00:38:10.641
like you are not suffering.

00:38:10.641 --> 00:38:13.180
You know, you are unhappy, but figure it out

00:38:13.180 --> 00:38:16.210
because you have been given so much by way of opportunity.

00:38:16.210 --> 00:38:18.660
You've got to figure out your way back to helping people.

00:38:18.660 --> 00:38:19.852
The solution lies in your will,

00:38:19.852 --> 00:38:22.950
your application of your will to this problem.

00:38:22.950 --> 00:38:27.450
Yeah, so I tried to leave three times.

00:38:27.450 --> 00:38:30.700
I knew our alma mater was up the street.

00:38:30.700 --> 00:38:33.380
I still had these very fond feelings for it.

00:38:33.380 --> 00:38:34.348
As I do to this day, I was driving past Hoover Tower,

00:38:34.348 --> 00:38:36.347
you know, between home and work.

00:38:36.347 --> 00:38:37.550
And it was beckoning.

00:38:37.550 --> 00:38:38.700
Like you can work with people,

00:38:38.700 --> 00:38:40.069
you can help people here, Julie.

00:38:40.069 --> 00:38:42.610
So I tried to get a job in student affairs

00:38:42.610 --> 00:38:44.440
and I tried to get a job in admissions.

00:38:44.440 --> 00:38:46.044
And I tried for another job in student affairs.

00:38:46.044 --> 00:38:48.501
And every single time I'd make it far in the process

00:38:48.501 --> 00:38:51.370
with a great cover letter and great interviewing skills.

00:38:51.370 --> 00:38:54.280
And then they'd say you don't have the skills actually

00:38:54.280 --> 00:38:56.360
to be a student affairs person

00:38:56.360 --> 00:38:58.780
'cause you didn't study this, you studied law.

00:38:58.780 --> 00:39:00.468
And finally, I caught a lucky break.

00:39:00.468 --> 00:39:03.120
I filled in for somebody on maternity leave.

00:39:03.120 --> 00:39:06.060
That tells you where my will was,

00:39:06.060 --> 00:39:08.306
and that tells you the risk I was willing to take.

00:39:08.306 --> 00:39:10.740
I went to my boss at Intel and said,

00:39:10.740 --> 00:39:14.070
I think I don't wanna be this type of lawyer anymore.

00:39:14.070 --> 00:39:15.150
I think I want a new career.

00:39:15.150 --> 00:39:17.480
I have a chance to test drive it by filling in for somebody,

00:39:17.480 --> 00:39:22.050
will you hold my job for 10 weeks, 12 weeks?

00:39:22.050 --> 00:39:25.666
And she said, yeah, for your sake, I hope you like it,

00:39:25.666 --> 00:39:27.348
for my sake, I hope you don't.

00:39:27.348 --> 00:39:29.460
(indistinct)

00:39:29.460 --> 00:39:31.740
Well, if she had said, no, I would've quit.

00:39:31.740 --> 00:39:32.950
Yeah, but I thought,

00:39:32.950 --> 00:39:35.400
'cause I was so determined at that point,

00:39:35.400 --> 00:39:37.700
I thought I better at least ask if I can,

00:39:37.700 --> 00:39:40.380
if she'll save my job for me, if she'll hold it.

00:39:40.380 --> 00:39:42.227
But if she doesn't, I know I'm gonna leave.

00:39:42.227 --> 00:39:44.180
It felt like an escape hatch.

00:39:44.180 --> 00:39:45.350
And I had to take it

00:39:45.350 --> 00:39:47.730
even if it was only a 12-week opportunity.

00:39:47.730 --> 00:39:50.470
And the stakes even higher,

00:39:50.470 --> 00:39:52.772
because you're the primary breadwinner

00:39:52.772 --> 00:39:54.000
in the household, right?

00:39:54.000 --> 00:39:58.300
Like your husband works part-time and is a caregiver.

00:39:58.300 --> 00:39:59.643
I certainly did once the kids came.

00:39:59.643 --> 00:40:01.407
At that time, we didn't have our kids yet,

00:40:01.407 --> 00:40:05.900
but that would be our pattern,

00:40:07.460 --> 00:40:09.315
our way of being parents, yeah.

00:40:09.315 --> 00:40:13.320
Yeah, so, and I take pride in not just being a breadwinner,

00:40:13.320 --> 00:40:17.310
but a huge piece of my identity is wrapped up in my work.

00:40:17.310 --> 00:40:19.046
My husband is far less so that way.

00:40:19.046 --> 00:40:24.046
And so, yeah, I was determined to find work

00:40:24.790 --> 00:40:27.478
I was both good at and loved.

00:40:27.478 --> 00:40:30.526
I think that's what the knot in my stomach

00:40:30.526 --> 00:40:31.960
and the blood pressure taught me.

00:40:31.960 --> 00:40:34.700
Doesn't matter if you're good at it if you hate it.

00:40:34.700 --> 00:40:37.370
You know, Andre Agassi, famous athlete,

00:40:37.370 --> 00:40:40.870
who's, you know, reportedly had that experience

00:40:40.870 --> 00:40:43.010
of, you know, best in the world for a time,

00:40:43.010 --> 00:40:45.760
but pretty darn miserable at times.

00:40:45.760 --> 00:40:48.680
And, you know, I tell parents like,

00:40:48.680 --> 00:40:49.970
you don't wanna raise an Andre Agassi.

00:40:49.970 --> 00:40:51.765
You wanna raise a Jeremy Lin, you know,

00:40:51.765 --> 00:40:56.765
who was improbably great at basketball and loved it

00:40:57.140 --> 00:41:00.991
and had his moment of absolute ecstasy in that sport.

00:41:00.991 --> 00:41:01.840
(whooshing sound)

00:41:01.840 --> 00:41:03.220
Sorry to interrupt the flow.

00:41:03.220 --> 00:41:04.820
We'll be right back with more awesome.

00:41:04.820 --> 00:41:07.230
But I wanna snack a moment to talk to you

00:41:07.230 --> 00:41:10.030
about the importance of nutrition.

00:41:10.030 --> 00:41:13.620
The thing is most people I know actually already know

00:41:13.620 --> 00:41:16.770
how to eat better and aspire to incorporate

00:41:16.770 --> 00:41:18.630
more whole plants, more fruits,

00:41:18.630 --> 00:41:21.330
vegetables, seeds, beans, and legumes

00:41:21.330 --> 00:41:22.690
into their daily routine.

00:41:22.690 --> 00:41:26.040
Sadly, however, without the kitchen tools and support,

00:41:26.040 --> 00:41:28.190
very few end up sticking with it.

00:41:28.190 --> 00:41:31.443
So because adopting a plant-based diet

00:41:31.443 --> 00:41:34.060
transformed my life so profoundly,

00:41:34.060 --> 00:41:36.550
and because I want everybody to experience

00:41:36.550 --> 00:41:39.030
some version of what I've experienced,

00:41:39.030 --> 00:41:43.000
we decided to tackle and solve this very common problem.

00:41:43.000 --> 00:41:45.440
The solution we've devised, I'm proud to say,

