an annual membership.

00:42:33.430 --> 00:42:35.585
All right, let's get back to the show.

00:42:35.585 --> 00:42:36.418
(whooshing sound)

00:42:36.418 --> 00:42:38.370
It's interesting what's happening in sports right now,

00:42:38.370 --> 00:42:42.851
particularly women's sports with Naomi Osaka,

00:42:42.851 --> 00:42:46.150
like basically saying I'm taking a mental health break

00:42:46.150 --> 00:42:49.340
and all the kind of, you know,

00:42:49.340 --> 00:42:51.480
kerfuffle that happened around that.

00:42:51.480 --> 00:42:54.271
And then did you see what just happened with Simone Biles?

00:42:54.271 --> 00:42:55.960
Not a physical injury,

00:42:55.960 --> 00:42:58.970
just saying it's a mental health thing and I'm opting out.

00:42:58.970 --> 00:43:00.671
Like, that is wild.

00:43:00.671 --> 00:43:02.016
It's gotta be wild.

00:43:02.016 --> 00:43:04.800
I know that you have a degree of appreciation

00:43:04.800 --> 00:43:07.350
as an elite athlete that many can't possibly have

00:43:07.350 --> 00:43:08.440
for that circumstance.

00:43:08.440 --> 00:43:11.732
From my vantage point, they're both black women as am I,

00:43:11.732 --> 00:43:16.732
and the pressure we are under when we step out

00:43:17.410 --> 00:43:19.794
onto our platform, whatever it is,

00:43:19.794 --> 00:43:24.790
the pressure we are under in this America is immense.

00:43:24.790 --> 00:43:27.665
We are said to be sort of the exception

00:43:27.665 --> 00:43:29.944
when we break stereotype

00:43:29.944 --> 00:43:33.410
and we are held to such impossibly high standards,

00:43:33.410 --> 00:43:38.410
and when we fall, then the vitriol comes.

00:43:39.600 --> 00:43:41.170
And I know without knowing

00:43:41.170 --> 00:43:43.159
either of these two remarkable young women,

00:43:43.159 --> 00:43:48.159
I know that the hate that they get on social media,

00:43:48.360 --> 00:43:50.693
the way in which they're excoriated,

00:43:50.693 --> 00:43:52.456
impacts their mental health.

00:43:52.456 --> 00:43:55.130
In addition to, I'm sure, the stress and pressure

00:43:55.130 --> 00:43:57.983
of being an elite athlete and all that comes with it.

00:43:57.983 --> 00:44:01.350
So my goodness, what a remarkable thing it is

00:44:01.350 --> 00:44:03.690
that they can say, you know what, I'm out.

00:44:03.690 --> 00:44:06.320
I can't, I'm not, I'm choosing not to do this.

00:44:06.320 --> 00:44:10.020
I'm choosing to step to the side and do something

00:44:10.020 --> 00:44:13.130
that is even more important than winning this tournament

00:44:13.130 --> 00:44:14.314
or this medal, whatever it may be.

00:44:14.314 --> 00:44:17.503
To do that at the Olympics when,

00:44:17.503 --> 00:44:21.320
you know, Simone being set up to be the darling

00:44:21.320 --> 00:44:25.310
of the entire games and all the success leading up to that

00:44:25.310 --> 00:44:26.540
and all the expectations,

00:44:26.540 --> 00:44:30.890
like I can't begin to fathom what her interior experience

00:44:30.890 --> 00:44:34.650
must be like and the level of courage it must've taken

00:44:34.650 --> 00:44:35.799
to just make that stand

00:44:35.799 --> 00:44:39.119
when so many people are invested in that success.

00:44:39.119 --> 00:44:42.482
And we treat our elite athletes like their race horses,

00:44:42.482 --> 00:44:47.482
and that is we're betting on them and we need them to win.

00:44:47.610 --> 00:44:49.990
And we're on the side of this one, not this one.

00:44:49.990 --> 00:44:51.190
We see it in the NFL.

00:44:51.190 --> 00:44:52.330
We see it in the NBA.

00:44:52.330 --> 00:44:55.110
We see someone like Laura Ingraham saying to,

00:44:55.110 --> 00:44:58.147
I believe it was LeBron James, shut up and play, right.

00:44:58.147 --> 00:45:01.730
And I'm here to say, as an African American woman,

00:45:01.730 --> 00:45:02.848
we are not your race horses.

00:45:02.848 --> 00:45:05.384
We are not your animals.

00:45:05.384 --> 00:45:09.496
We are human beings who have dreams and fears,

00:45:09.496 --> 00:45:12.820
and we bleed, and we cry.

00:45:12.820 --> 00:45:15.550
And, my God, every one of us, Rich,

00:45:15.550 --> 00:45:18.322
just wants to be treated with dignity and kindness.

00:45:18.322 --> 00:45:21.330
You know, you know, 'cause I've heard you talk about it.

00:45:21.330 --> 00:45:23.620
That's the message of Black Lives Matter.

00:45:23.620 --> 00:45:25.108
It's not we matter more.

00:45:25.108 --> 00:45:27.111
I'm not trying to be black supremacists.

00:45:27.111 --> 00:45:32.111
We're saying can't black life and black lives matter, too.

00:45:32.889 --> 00:45:37.889
And I think, you know, this dignity is probably

00:45:38.930 --> 00:45:40.600
one of the main adjectives I would use

00:45:40.600 --> 00:45:42.080
to describe Simone Biles right now.

00:45:42.080 --> 00:45:44.750
I mean, I think she even, if I read it correctly,

00:45:44.750 --> 00:45:47.220
one of her quotes was,

00:45:47.220 --> 00:45:48.750
you know, I was worried about my performance,

00:45:48.750 --> 00:45:51.140
my ability to perform and I didn't wanna jeopardize

00:45:51.140 --> 00:45:53.081
the team's chance of meddling.

00:45:53.081 --> 00:45:55.340
Here are people saying now you're not gonna get the gold

00:45:55.340 --> 00:45:56.694
cause Simone withdrew.

00:45:56.694 --> 00:45:58.890
She was saying, I owe it to my team.

00:45:58.890 --> 00:46:00.233
I'm so worried about my performance.

00:46:00.233 --> 00:46:03.530
We might not medal if I show up the way that I am.

00:46:03.530 --> 00:46:05.730
So in some ways, the ultimate sacrifice.

00:46:05.730 --> 00:46:08.120
Yeah, yeah, that's an interesting lens.

00:46:08.120 --> 00:46:10.900
I only just saw that had happened today

00:46:10.900 --> 00:46:14.906
and I didn't like delve deeper into better understanding it.

00:46:14.906 --> 00:46:19.906
But that idea of expectations and making sense

00:46:22.940 --> 00:46:26.010
of racial identity is obviously terrain

00:46:26.010 --> 00:46:27.780
that you've explored in your writing.

00:46:27.780 --> 00:46:29.786
You wrote a memoir called "Real American."

00:46:29.786 --> 00:46:34.268
And in that book, you know, what I intuit

00:46:34.268 --> 00:46:36.990
or extrapolate from that is this journey

00:46:36.990 --> 00:46:39.300
towards self-love and self-acceptance,

00:46:39.300 --> 00:46:40.709
which was this missing piece

00:46:40.709 --> 00:46:44.866
driven by not really feeling like you fit in

00:46:44.866 --> 00:46:48.940
to any of the respective communities being biracial

00:46:48.940 --> 00:46:51.350
and with the parents, with whom you were raised

00:46:51.350 --> 00:46:53.130
and the experiences that you had.

00:46:54.261 --> 00:46:57.230
And it broke my heart to hear you share stories

00:46:57.230 --> 00:47:00.393
about being at Stanford and trying to seek out community

00:47:00.393 --> 00:47:03.930
and not really feeling like you could find it

00:47:03.930 --> 00:47:05.080
in the way that you wanted

00:47:05.080 --> 00:47:07.000
and understanding only later

00:47:07.000 --> 00:47:09.670
that you had to heal like your own sense of self,

00:47:09.670 --> 00:47:11.842
that it wasn't as much about you

00:47:11.842 --> 00:47:15.760
or about the community welcoming or not welcoming you.

00:47:15.760 --> 00:47:18.910
It was more about like how you perceived yourself.

00:47:18.910 --> 00:47:20.060
That's exactly right.

00:47:21.080 --> 00:47:25.220
Black biracial kid, white mother, black father.

00:47:25.220 --> 00:47:29.020
My father, Rich, was raised in the Jim Crow South.

00:47:29.020 --> 00:47:30.620
He would have been 103 if he was still alive.

00:47:30.620 --> 00:47:33.320
So really different era, born in 1918,

00:47:33.320 --> 00:47:35.260
escaped through track.

00:47:35.260 --> 00:47:37.770
Was a great runner, they called him the Oklahoma Flyer

00:47:37.770 --> 00:47:40.310
when he got to Bates was running track.

00:47:40.310 --> 00:47:44.800
And his trajectory was do not be impeded by white racism.

00:47:44.800 --> 00:47:47.250
He helped to eradicate smallpox in West Africa

00:47:47.250 --> 00:47:49.870
in the 60s and being among Africans,

00:47:49.870 --> 00:47:53.126
his blackness was not an impediment to his thriving.

00:47:53.126 --> 00:47:56.580
And he really came of age as a black man in his 40s

00:47:56.580 --> 00:47:59.030
in West Africa on the smallpox team,

00:47:59.030 --> 00:48:03.110
came back to America in 1969 and was met with a,

00:48:03.110 --> 00:48:04.810
what he was familiar with.

00:48:04.810 --> 00:48:09.810
I was born to people who transgressed law

00:48:10.390 --> 00:48:12.700
by daring to marry.

00:48:12.700 --> 00:48:14.980
I was born to people who broke the rules

00:48:14.980 --> 00:48:18.060
by falling in love, marrying and having me.

00:48:18.060 --> 00:48:21.930
And I got the message from age four,

00:48:21.930 --> 00:48:23.574
as a farthest back, I can trace it.

00:48:23.574 --> 00:48:26.070
I knew as a four-year-old

00:48:26.070 --> 00:48:28.680
that in the eyes of some white strangers,

00:48:28.680 --> 00:48:29.970
my daddy was problematic

00:48:29.970 --> 00:48:32.880
and that something was wrong with me too.

00:48:32.880 --> 00:48:34.820
I gleaned that.

00:48:34.820 --> 00:48:37.870
And I think my having lived outside the lines,

00:48:37.870 --> 00:48:40.512
having been unconsummated positively,

00:48:40.512 --> 00:48:43.750
certainly in the late sixties when biraciality,

00:48:43.750 --> 00:48:46.789
multiraciality was not accepted the way it is now.

00:48:46.789 --> 00:48:49.223
I've lived outside the lines my whole life.

00:48:49.223 --> 00:48:53.080
And I think when I think about why do I,

00:48:53.080 --> 00:48:54.806
why do I try to help people on their journey?

00:48:54.806 --> 00:48:57.358
I was otherized from the start,

00:48:57.358 --> 00:48:59.670
I was rejected, dismissed,

00:48:59.670 --> 00:49:04.670
and I think that gives me a deep care

00:49:05.010 --> 00:49:08.860
and concern for anyone who, for whatever reason

00:49:08.860 --> 00:49:09.960
has been marginalized.

00:49:11.247 --> 00:49:14.980
And your dad became Assistant Surgeon General

00:49:14.980 --> 00:49:15.813
under Carter.

00:49:15.813 --> 00:49:17.765
So we lived in Reston, Virginia, north Virginia,

00:49:17.765 --> 00:49:18.598
when you wouldn't say-

00:49:18.598 --> 00:49:19.510
Yeah, I noticed that.

00:49:19.510 --> 00:49:20.970
But it was before high school, right?

00:49:20.970 --> 00:49:23.960
It was because Reagan beat Carter, right,

00:49:23.960 --> 00:49:25.770
as we were starting high school.

00:49:25.770 --> 00:49:28.330
Right, and your mom being white,

00:49:28.330 --> 00:49:33.330
but she would dress herself in beautiful African landings

00:49:34.290 --> 00:49:35.950
and fabrics and things like that.

00:49:35.950 --> 00:49:36.940
And that had its own-

00:49:36.940 --> 00:49:38.730
'Cause she had spent seven years there.

00:49:38.730 --> 00:49:39.563
Right, and, you know,

00:49:39.563 --> 00:49:42.689
her always wanting you to be, you know,

00:49:42.689 --> 00:49:46.900
proud of your blackness and your dad being someone who,

00:49:46.900 --> 00:49:49.202
coming from that experience and having to weather,

00:49:49.202 --> 00:49:51.721
you know, what he went through,

00:49:51.721 --> 00:49:55.200
I'm sure compensating by wanting you

00:49:55.200 --> 00:49:57.440
to be achievement-oriented so that, you know,

00:49:57.440 --> 00:49:59.270
the color of your skin would be irrelevant

00:49:59.270 --> 00:50:01.070
and you would be able to make your way in the world.

00:50:01.070 --> 00:50:04.270
But the piece that was missing was you coming to terms

00:50:04.270 --> 00:50:06.020
with that and what it meant to you.

00:50:06.939 --> 00:50:07.772
Is that fair.

00:50:07.772 --> 00:50:09.450
Thank you for reading so closely.

00:50:09.450 --> 00:50:14.450
It's really quite moving when somebody holds your words

00:50:15.570 --> 00:50:17.232
that close, it really is, thank you.

00:50:17.232 --> 00:50:19.730
Yeah, that's exactly it.

00:50:19.730 --> 00:50:22.350
They would, if my father was alive today he would say this.

00:50:22.350 --> 00:50:24.190
My mother is alive, very much part of my life,

00:50:24.190 --> 00:50:25.023
my amazing mother.

00:50:25.023 --> 00:50:27.460
She would say if she was sitting here with me right now,

00:50:27.460 --> 00:50:29.280
which she would love to be, by the way,

00:50:29.280 --> 00:50:31.320
she would say, you know, we just knew

00:50:31.320 --> 00:50:34.185
we wanted you to be comfortable everywhere.

00:50:34.185 --> 00:50:37.281
We wanted to raise you so you could walk into any room

00:50:37.281 --> 00:50:39.910
and get what you needed, command attention,

00:50:39.910 --> 00:50:42.235
get the respect you deserved.

00:50:42.235 --> 00:50:45.480
And I appreciate that that was their reality

00:50:45.480 --> 00:50:46.335
and their truth.

00:50:46.335 --> 00:50:51.090
What they overlooked is how desperately important it is

00:50:51.090 --> 00:50:52.288
for a black or brown child,

00:50:52.288 --> 00:50:55.190
and I can't speak for people with other identities,

00:50:55.190 --> 00:50:56.583
but I'm sure it may apply to others

00:50:56.583 --> 00:50:59.520
to have peers who look like them,

00:50:59.520 --> 00:51:01.305
to have a few teachers who look like them,

00:51:01.305 --> 00:51:03.660
to have neighbors who look like them,

00:51:03.660 --> 00:51:06.110
to have a pediatrician or a librarian

00:51:06.110 --> 00:51:09.310
or another adult authority figure who looks like them.

00:51:09.310 --> 00:51:10.940
We say representation matters today

00:51:10.940 --> 00:51:14.910
and I have lived a life that bears out that truth, Rich.

00:51:14.910 --> 00:51:16.790
When somebody wrote, look,

00:51:16.790 --> 00:51:18.490
I was at an all-white high school.

00:51:19.800 --> 00:51:21.940
By all-white, I mean 1200 kids,

00:51:21.940 --> 00:51:24.743
two Jews who were not considered white and me.

00:51:24.743 --> 00:51:27.390
And on my 17th birthday,

00:51:29.170 --> 00:51:31.488
somebody wrote the N-word on my locker.

00:51:31.488 --> 00:51:35.060
And I was president of the student council,

00:51:35.060 --> 00:51:37.969
doing very well academically, pompom girl,

00:51:37.969 --> 00:51:41.270
singer in the choir, popular, if you will.

00:51:42.240 --> 00:51:45.194
I was so ashamed to be the person

00:51:45.194 --> 00:51:46.970
who had had the N-word

00:51:46.970 --> 00:51:49.579
written on her birthday sign on her locker

00:51:49.579 --> 00:51:51.440
that I didn't tell a soul.

00:51:52.560 --> 00:51:55.090
But I'm here to tell you that if one teacher

00:51:55.090 --> 00:51:56.633
over the course of those four years,

00:51:56.633 --> 00:51:57.890
and they were all white,

00:51:57.890 --> 00:51:59.376
so I don't need to say white teacher,

00:51:59.376 --> 00:52:01.855
had pulled me aside and said, hey, kid,

00:52:01.855 --> 00:52:04.530
you look a lot different from everyone else here.

00:52:04.530 --> 00:52:07.620
I want you to know I'm here for you.

00:52:07.620 --> 00:52:10.786
Should anything ever go down, you come see me.

00:52:10.786 --> 00:52:15.260
If one teacher had thought

00:52:15.260 --> 00:52:17.680
to have had that conversation with me,

00:52:17.680 --> 00:52:22.070
I feel like my experience would have been so much better.

00:52:22.070 --> 00:52:25.650
I would have felt less ashamed, less bewildered

00:52:25.650 --> 00:52:26.550
and less alone.

00:52:27.610 --> 00:52:30.680
I'd like to think that that would be the case today.

00:52:30.680 --> 00:52:31.513
I do too.

00:52:31.513 --> 00:52:33.692
A lot has changed for sure.

00:52:33.692 --> 00:52:36.970
And yet look at us tumbling backward.

00:52:38.070 --> 00:52:42.510
Yeah, it's a confusing time-
It is.

00:52:42.510 --> 00:52:43.500
With all of this.

00:52:46.229 --> 00:52:48.850
And as much as I feel like we've made progress,

00:52:48.850 --> 00:52:52.740
there are so many glaring blind spots in all of this.

00:52:52.740 --> 00:52:55.233
Yeah, agreed.

00:52:55.233 --> 00:53:00.233
How does it look at Stanford these days on that tip.

00:53:01.900 --> 00:53:03.330
With regard to race relations?

00:53:03.330 --> 00:53:04.163
Yeah.

00:53:04.163 --> 00:53:05.840
You know, so I did leave nine years ago.

00:53:05.840 --> 00:53:10.100
I can't speak for the ins and outs of campus life today,

00:53:10.100 --> 00:53:12.220
but of course know plenty of people who are there,

00:53:12.220 --> 00:53:16.710
and I'm proud of our alma mater.

00:53:16.710 --> 00:53:20.320
You know, Stanford was co-ed from the start.

00:53:20.320 --> 00:53:23.550
Back in 1891 when the Ivys were only accepting men,

00:53:23.550 --> 00:53:26.601
our alma mater excepted people of all genders,

00:53:26.601 --> 00:53:28.080
as we would put it today.

00:53:30.340 --> 00:53:34.770
Stanford is very much a place where diversity is cherished

00:53:34.770 --> 00:53:35.660
and championed.

00:53:36.520 --> 00:53:40.570
I think kids of color thrive there.

00:53:40.570 --> 00:53:42.940
I think queer kids thrive there.

00:53:42.940 --> 00:53:45.050
I think poor and working class kids

00:53:45.050 --> 00:53:47.020
are increasingly thriving there

00:53:47.020 --> 00:53:50.230
with concerted efforts within student affairs,

00:53:50.230 --> 00:53:53.540
to ensure that folks in that community are supported

00:53:53.540 --> 00:53:57.180
and mentored by folks who have walked that path.

00:53:57.180 --> 00:53:59.686
There's, you know, a real intentionality

00:53:59.686 --> 00:54:02.990
around supporting the individual on their journey.

00:54:02.990 --> 00:54:04.730
And that place not to say it's perfect,

00:54:04.730 --> 00:54:06.390
it is not perfect.

00:54:06.390 --> 00:54:07.930
It is not an, you know, an institution

00:54:07.930 --> 00:54:11.200
simply made up of its people and we are all imperfect,

00:54:11.200 --> 00:54:12.860
but I think it's a place that can be proud

00:54:12.860 --> 00:54:14.630
of what it's doing even as it seeks

00:54:14.630 --> 00:54:15.850
to continue to be better.

00:54:15.850 --> 00:54:18.500
Yeah, I hope so.

00:54:18.500 --> 00:54:19.589
Yeah, well.

00:54:19.589 --> 00:54:23.240
Well, how did you feel with the response

00:54:23.240 --> 00:54:24.800
during the pandemic when they announced

00:54:24.800 --> 00:54:27.080
that so many sports were going to be cut?

00:54:27.080 --> 00:54:30.150
I watched the community feel anguish,

00:54:30.150 --> 00:54:33.975
going back to this notion of Stanford always marrying

00:54:33.975 --> 00:54:36.585
excellence in athletics with excellence in academics,

00:54:36.585 --> 00:54:40.560
winning that NC2A Cup every single year,

00:54:40.560 --> 00:54:41.882
making a decision to cut sports.

00:54:41.882 --> 00:54:44.600
I mean, I just felt heartbroken for the athletes

00:54:44.600 --> 00:54:46.110
because I know what it feels like

00:54:46.110 --> 00:54:50.229
when you're so invested in your sport

00:54:50.229 --> 00:54:53.770
and you're on kind of a ticking clock timetable,

00:54:53.770 --> 00:54:56.510
like the lifespan of the athlete, you know,

00:54:56.510 --> 00:54:58.980
particularly the ounce to a athlete is not very long.

00:54:58.980 --> 00:55:02.610
So a year is like eons, you know.

00:55:02.610 --> 00:55:04.398
That makes the difference between you

00:55:04.398 --> 00:55:08.070
achieving your potential and missing your window

00:55:08.070 --> 00:55:09.030
to do your thing.

00:55:09.030 --> 00:55:11.960
And I just couldn't help, but think what I would have felt

00:55:11.960 --> 00:55:15.060
like had I been in that situation and I would have been,

00:55:15.060 --> 00:55:16.250
I would have been devastated.

00:55:16.250 --> 00:55:17.198
Absolutely.

00:55:17.198 --> 00:55:21.960
Yeah, I can't imagine having to figure that out

00:55:21.960 --> 00:55:24.391
and just now watching the Olympics,

00:55:24.391 --> 00:55:27.975
you know, you see Katie Ledecky got a silver the other day

00:55:27.975 --> 00:55:31.181
in an event that, you know, I'm sure she,

00:55:31.181 --> 00:55:34.280
and everybody expected that, that she would win,

00:55:34.280 --> 00:55:37.020
and I couldn't help but think if the Olympics had gone on

00:55:37.020 --> 00:55:38.000
as scheduled last year,

00:55:38.000 --> 00:55:39.900
there's no way she would've lost that race.

00:55:39.900 --> 00:55:42.370
And I don't know what her past year has been like.

00:55:42.370 --> 00:55:43.639
It's been challenging for everybody,

00:55:43.639 --> 00:55:46.430
and a silver's still amazing.

00:55:46.430 --> 00:55:48.800
And she's an extraordinary human and athletes,

00:55:48.800 --> 00:55:52.800
so good for her, but it's not without its toll.

00:55:52.800 --> 00:55:55.640
And yet in the grand scheme of things like,

00:55:56.890 --> 00:55:58.630
you know, we're dealing with a global pandemic

00:55:58.630 --> 00:56:00.100
and people are dying, and you know,

00:56:00.100 --> 00:56:02.480
we're trying to solve this problem and figure out

00:56:02.480 --> 00:56:03.770
how to keep everybody safe.

00:56:03.770 --> 00:56:05.540
These are not easy problems to solve

00:56:05.540 --> 00:56:08.260
and compromises are made and decisions are made.

00:56:08.260 --> 00:56:10.003
Some of which we'll look back on in retrospect

00:56:10.003 --> 00:56:11.543
and say those were the wrong decisions,

00:56:11.543 --> 00:56:13.960
and some of which of course will say,

00:56:13.960 --> 00:56:15.860
it's good that we did that as soon as we did.

00:56:15.860 --> 00:56:19.010
So, you know, I don't know beyond that.

00:56:19.010 --> 00:56:21.100
I'm not sure I have any great insight,

00:56:21.100 --> 00:56:24.640
but other than just compassion for all of this.

00:56:26.590 --> 00:56:31.590
Back to this idea of raising healthy adults,

00:56:31.630 --> 00:56:33.860
how to become a healthy adult.

00:56:33.860 --> 00:56:37.140
What I loved about the first book,

00:56:37.140 --> 00:56:38.610
"How to Raise an Adult,"

00:56:38.610 --> 00:56:40.620
is that the New York Times called it

00:56:40.620 --> 00:56:43.520
the black hawk down of helicopter parenting.

00:56:43.520 --> 00:56:44.964
Like that is like the ultimate line.

00:56:44.964 --> 00:56:46.545
That's so awesome (laughs)

00:56:46.545 --> 00:56:49.480
Yeah, it was, look, it's pretty cool

00:56:49.480 --> 00:56:51.921
when you get the call that your book's gonna be reviewed,

00:56:51.921 --> 00:56:54.287
and then you read the review and it's positive,

00:56:54.287 --> 00:56:57.180
and then they use an awesome line like that.

00:56:57.180 --> 00:57:00.129
It was pretty much kind of a grand slam for me.

00:57:00.129 --> 00:57:03.430
Right, and when I think of you in the work,

00:57:03.430 --> 00:57:08.430
I think of Jonathan Haidt, Jessica Lahey, Susan David,

00:57:12.096 --> 00:57:14.500
who tweeted this morning, you saw that tweet, right?

00:57:14.500 --> 00:57:15.333
Yeah, yeah.

00:57:15.333 --> 00:57:17.640
In response to us getting together.

00:57:17.640 --> 00:57:19.740
Lisa D'Amour, Lori Gottlieb,

00:57:19.740 --> 00:57:22.270
who comes up quite a bit in your writing,

00:57:22.270 --> 00:57:24.000
as this sort of brain trust of people

00:57:24.000 --> 00:57:27.040
who have thought deeply about the issue

00:57:27.040 --> 00:57:30.490
of how to effectively raise young people

00:57:30.490 --> 00:57:34.201
and position them for success in the world.

00:57:34.201 --> 00:57:36.990
Yeah, I missed the question.

00:57:36.990 --> 00:57:37.920
It's not really a question.

00:57:37.920 --> 00:57:39.220
It's more just an observation.

00:57:39.220 --> 00:57:44.220
Like, I suppose I would say you're not alone.

00:57:45.320 --> 00:57:46.980
And having this perspective,

00:57:46.980 --> 00:57:48.370
there are other people that share it,

00:57:48.370 --> 00:57:51.570
who are excellent writers and deep thinkers

00:57:51.570 --> 00:57:54.070
and academics, et cetera.

00:57:55.050 --> 00:58:00.050
And so I feel like these ideas are percolating

00:58:00.550 --> 00:58:01.923
in the culture at the moment.

00:58:01.923 --> 00:58:06.923
And I wonder how much of it is being put into practice.

00:58:07.370 --> 00:58:10.555
And I say this because when you share the experiences

00:58:10.555 --> 00:58:12.310
that you've had with parents

00:58:12.310 --> 00:58:14.250
when you were Dean of Freshmen at Stanford,

00:58:14.250 --> 00:58:16.259
and I kind of see what I see out in the world.

00:58:16.259 --> 00:58:21.020
There's this sense that we're producing a generation

00:58:21.020 --> 00:58:24.440
of a very fragile human beings who are overly sensitive,

00:58:24.440 --> 00:58:28.480
and it's all about safe spaces and entitlement,

00:58:28.480 --> 00:58:31.940
and kind of the other catch words that you hear.

00:58:31.940 --> 00:58:35.680
And I feel like for a while there was this sense that, well,

00:58:35.680 --> 00:58:39.450
when they leave the protective enclave of the college

00:58:39.450 --> 00:58:42.030
or the university, and go out into the real world,

00:58:42.030 --> 00:58:45.239
they're gonna get a dose of, you know, real-life medicine.

00:58:45.239 --> 00:58:47.830
And they're gonna figure out that, you know,

00:58:47.830 --> 00:58:51.630
what worked for them in university

00:58:51.630 --> 00:58:52.780
is no longer gonna work.

00:58:52.780 --> 00:58:54.439
But instead, I think what we're seeing is,

00:58:54.439 --> 00:58:57.140
you know, generations of people

00:58:57.140 --> 00:58:59.502
who are matriculating into the grownup world

00:58:59.502 --> 00:59:03.750
and then, you know, sort of rise into positions of influence

00:59:03.750 --> 00:59:05.140
where they're hiring people.

00:59:05.140 --> 00:59:09.800
And now we're seeing entire industries,

00:59:09.800 --> 00:59:12.188
whether it's journalism or entertainment,

00:59:12.188 --> 00:59:17.188
where that sensibility is now part and parcel

00:59:17.760 --> 00:59:22.760
of kind of what we consume in terms of the news that we read

00:59:23.200 --> 00:59:25.860
and the programming that we consume.

00:59:25.860 --> 00:59:28.600
So how do you, yeah, that's not a question either, but-

00:59:28.600 --> 00:59:31.150
Yeah, no, but I appreciate you're elucidating,

00:59:32.580 --> 00:59:33.676
your perspective on this.

00:59:33.676 --> 00:59:38.670
You know, there are a lot of folks who would call

00:59:38.670 --> 00:59:41.909
the concept of safe spaces a snowflake concept.

00:59:41.909 --> 00:59:45.340
And I think there are extremes one can go to,

00:59:45.340 --> 00:59:48.540
or one is unwilling to hear a thought they don't like,

00:59:48.540 --> 00:59:49.770
or that they find offensive.

00:59:49.770 --> 00:59:51.660
And I find that very problematic

00:59:51.660 --> 00:59:53.540
because I am very much a believer

00:59:53.540 --> 00:59:55.950
that you counteract harmful speech with more speech,

00:59:55.950 --> 00:59:58.220
that you have to know your enemy quote, unquote,

00:59:58.220 --> 01:00:00.520
perspective in order to strengthen your own perspectives.

01:00:00.520 --> 01:00:04.563
You have to be aware of quote, unquote, the other side.

01:00:04.563 --> 01:00:06.830
So I'm not interested in safe spaces

01:00:06.830 --> 01:00:08.420
where people put their fingers in their ears

01:00:08.420 --> 01:00:10.580
and go la, la, la, and not listening.

01:00:11.760 --> 01:00:16.000
At the same time, I think the requests for safe spaces

01:00:16.000 --> 01:00:18.381
and the request for kinder language

01:00:18.381 --> 01:00:23.381
has come out of the, let's not harass people doctrine

01:00:24.010 --> 01:00:27.340
and has come out of the social justice doctrine.

01:00:27.340 --> 01:00:29.460
Like let's treat people with dignity/

01:00:29.460 --> 01:00:32.104
let's not be assholes here.

01:00:32.104 --> 01:00:35.150
So I think I'm longing for that middle ground,

01:00:35.150 --> 01:00:39.788
which is we can be frank and blunt, we can disagree.

01:00:39.788 --> 01:00:42.000
And yet we're not demeaning people

01:00:42.000 --> 01:00:45.490
on the basis of some aspect of their identity.

01:00:45.490 --> 01:00:50.490
And I think Gen Z frankly, has the tenacity to get us there.

01:00:53.890 --> 01:00:57.655
I try not to be too much of a blanket,

01:00:57.655 --> 01:00:59.710
you know, Gen Z are this,

01:00:59.710 --> 01:01:01.320
Millennials are that, Gen X is that,

01:01:01.320 --> 01:01:04.860
but Gen Z has been raised,

01:01:04.860 --> 01:01:06.360
first of all, the most diverse generation

01:01:06.360 --> 01:01:07.550
in American history,

01:01:07.550 --> 01:01:09.270
they've been raised with social and emotional learning

01:01:09.270 --> 01:01:10.460
in their schooling.

01:01:10.460 --> 01:01:14.050
Their media has depicted a more diverse range of characters

01:01:14.050 --> 01:01:15.080
than any prior generation.

01:01:15.080 --> 01:01:17.210
They've had active shooter drills

01:01:17.210 --> 01:01:18.572
since they were kindergartners.

01:01:18.572 --> 01:01:21.920
They have lived in a different America.

01:01:21.920 --> 01:01:23.490
They have learned that the adults

01:01:23.490 --> 01:01:25.036
have not solved the problems

01:01:25.036 --> 01:01:28.380
and they seem collectively to have a voice

01:01:28.380 --> 01:01:30.800
that says, move over, we're here.

01:01:30.800 --> 01:01:32.040
Oh, and I forgot to mention,

01:01:32.040 --> 01:01:34.560
they understand technology innately.

01:01:34.560 --> 01:01:38.330
So I'm actually here as a Gen Xer 53 to say,

01:01:38.330 --> 01:01:39.312
you know what?

01:01:39.312 --> 01:01:42.470
I wanna stand with you, I'm not gonna be

01:01:42.470 --> 01:01:45.560
that okay Boomer, elder, who tries to tell them what to do.

01:01:45.560 --> 01:01:49.510
They are reshaping a world they arrived into broken.

01:01:49.510 --> 01:01:51.780
The world was broken and they,

01:01:51.780 --> 01:01:53.996
I think are people we can learn from.

01:01:53.996 --> 01:01:55.519
So I'm here with curiosity

01:01:55.519 --> 01:01:58.030
and a little bit of that older wisdom.

01:01:58.030 --> 01:02:00.460
Like what don't forget, you have to try to talk to people,

01:02:00.460 --> 01:02:03.550
look them in the eye, if you can, asterisk, I will say,

01:02:03.550 --> 01:02:05.120
because what we now know more than ever

01:02:05.120 --> 01:02:06.280
is some people have social anxiety

01:02:06.280 --> 01:02:08.160
and some people are on the autism spectrum.

01:02:08.160 --> 01:02:09.820
And you can't just say, look me in the eye,

01:02:09.820 --> 01:02:10.653
what's wrong with you.

01:02:10.653 --> 01:02:13.040
You have to appreciate not everyone can.

01:02:13.040 --> 01:02:15.770
So I think we are in this 21st century

01:02:15.770 --> 01:02:17.220
arriving at an understanding

01:02:17.220 --> 01:02:19.645
of our many, many myriad differences

01:02:19.645 --> 01:02:23.040
and the ways in which appreciating those differences

01:02:23.040 --> 01:02:25.630
can actually evolve us forward as a human community.

01:02:25.630 --> 01:02:26.463
Very well said.

01:02:26.463 --> 01:02:30.480
I mean, certainly an appreciation for those differences

01:02:30.480 --> 01:02:34.330
and a recognition and a respect for that

01:02:34.330 --> 01:02:35.969
is crucial and important.

01:02:35.969 --> 01:02:38.400
On the resiliency piece though,

01:02:38.400 --> 01:02:41.440
I think perhaps what's getting missed,

01:02:41.440 --> 01:02:43.770
maybe I'm totally off base here,

01:02:43.770 --> 01:02:48.770
but is a resiliency to be able to engage with people

01:02:50.500 --> 01:02:52.130
who have a different point of view

01:02:52.130 --> 01:02:56.880
without it being necessarily, quote, unquote unsafe, right?

01:02:56.880 --> 01:02:59.590
Can we have a nuanced conversation where two people

01:02:59.590 --> 01:03:03.190
who disagree can sit down and maybe lead that conversation

01:03:03.190 --> 01:03:06.586
still not agreeing, but do it in, you know,

01:03:06.586 --> 01:03:09.633
in a way in which each is conducting themselves

01:03:09.633 --> 01:03:13.190
with some level of like maturity and respect.

01:03:13.190 --> 01:03:14.023
100%.

01:03:14.023 --> 01:03:19.023
And I would say back to you, where did we go wrong?

01:03:19.160 --> 01:03:20.714
Or maybe the more interesting question is

01:03:20.714 --> 01:03:25.714
where do we turn to reinstate, if it ever did exist?

01:03:26.440 --> 01:03:28.390
Where do we go to reinstate that manner of being?

01:03:28.390 --> 01:03:29.380
Is it the family?

01:03:29.380 --> 01:03:32.340
Is it youth groups that no longer exists

01:03:32.340 --> 01:03:33.173
the way they used to,

01:03:33.173 --> 01:03:35.260
because now it's all about performance-based youth groups

01:03:35.260 --> 01:03:36.669
and it's not about character.

01:03:36.669 --> 01:03:38.237
You know, where are we,

01:03:38.237 --> 01:03:40.940
what institutions are currently failing us

01:03:40.940 --> 01:03:42.379
and which ones are we gonna shore up?

01:03:42.379 --> 01:03:44.460
You know, look at what's happening in America.

01:03:44.460 --> 01:03:47.755
And it's like, my God, when did we stop teaching science?

01:03:47.755 --> 01:03:49.629
When did we stop teaching ethics?

01:03:49.629 --> 01:03:51.821
When did we stop teaching how a democracy works

01:03:51.821 --> 01:03:53.210
and how it functions?

01:03:53.210 --> 01:03:55.770
Like, why is it that all of these things we consider

01:03:55.770 --> 01:03:57.612
the underpinnings of this experiment called America

01:03:57.612 --> 01:04:00.078
seem to be fragile now.

01:04:00.078 --> 01:04:02.047
And where do we go to shore them up

01:04:02.047 --> 01:04:03.807
or completely rebuild the foundations?

01:04:03.807 --> 01:04:07.884
I do think our universities have to be places

01:04:07.884 --> 01:04:10.796
where people with wildly differing viewpoints,

01:04:10.796 --> 01:04:13.610
can and will talk with one another.

01:04:13.610 --> 01:04:15.630
Can and will find spaces to be heard.

01:04:15.630 --> 01:04:17.110
I think that's essential, but-

01:04:17.110 --> 01:04:18.060
It has to be that way.

01:04:18.060 --> 01:04:20.112
It has to, but before university,

01:04:20.112 --> 01:04:22.429
I think in our K-12 environment,

01:04:22.429 --> 01:04:26.220
it shouldn't be about clamping ear muffs

01:04:26.220 --> 01:04:28.540
and blinders on our kids so that they don't see

01:04:28.540 --> 01:04:30.078
and don't hear but rather we get better

01:04:30.078 --> 01:04:34.260
at engaging in difficult discourse.

01:04:34.260 --> 01:04:35.910
And character is at the heart

01:04:35.910 --> 01:04:38.220
of how we can bridge the gaps, right.

01:04:38.220 --> 01:04:40.251
If you and I can be of good character,

01:04:40.251 --> 01:04:42.886
be kind, treat each other with dignity

01:04:42.886 --> 01:04:46.360
and have completely different viewpoints,

01:04:46.360 --> 01:04:49.401
but not excoriate each other, not demean each other.

01:04:49.401 --> 01:04:50.711
You know, we can get somewhere.

01:04:50.711 --> 01:04:52.660
Right, right.

01:04:52.660 --> 01:04:56.440
So we can point the finger at the family or the university

01:04:56.440 --> 01:04:58.007
or the institution or the corporation,

01:04:58.007 --> 01:05:00.710
but ultimately those are things

01:05:00.710 --> 01:05:02.394
that we have very little control over.

01:05:02.394 --> 01:05:04.234
What we do have control over

01:05:04.234 --> 01:05:06.041
is how we acquit ourselves, right.

01:05:06.041 --> 01:05:10.030
So in this book, your turn, you go through,

01:05:10.030 --> 01:05:12.150
I mean, it's like fricking 500 pages long-

01:05:12.150 --> 01:05:13.760
I'm sorry, I know, thank you for reading.

01:05:13.760 --> 01:05:14.762
But what's great about it is-

01:05:14.762 --> 01:05:16.392
'Cause life is long, Rich, or it should be-

01:05:16.392 --> 01:05:18.500
I know.
You can't TLDR-

01:05:18.500 --> 01:05:22.000
This thing is, I mean, it is a book, right?

01:05:22.000 --> 01:05:22.833
It is a book.

01:05:24.090 --> 01:05:29.027
And what's great about it is, it's perennial, right?

01:05:30.710 --> 01:05:33.270
This is like a book that parents can give

01:05:33.270 --> 01:05:35.440
to their young ones as they're emerging

01:05:35.440 --> 01:05:36.445
into the grown-up world,

01:05:36.445 --> 01:05:39.194
every single year upon graduation.

01:05:39.194 --> 01:05:41.750
It's a book that I think every young person

01:05:41.750 --> 01:05:46.012
who is grappling with how they're gonna navigate adulthood

01:05:46.012 --> 01:05:47.431
can benefit from.

01:05:47.431 --> 01:05:52.431
And me, being 54, wishing that I had a book like this

01:05:52.695 --> 01:05:55.467
when we were back, you know,

01:05:55.467 --> 01:05:56.480
finishing up our years at Stanford

01:05:56.480 --> 01:05:59.920
to help me make sense of how I was gonna, you know,

01:05:59.920 --> 01:06:01.866
try to figure out how to be a man in the world.

01:06:01.866 --> 01:06:03.450
There was no book like this.

01:06:03.450 --> 01:06:05.660
You know, I basically fumbled around

01:06:05.660 --> 01:06:06.950
and made a million mistakes.

01:06:06.950 --> 01:06:09.110
And until I finally started to figure

01:06:09.110 --> 01:06:11.145
out a few things for myself,

01:06:11.145 --> 01:06:15.515
but this is really kind of a roadmap and a manifesto

01:06:15.515 --> 01:06:19.597
and a primer for things that young people

01:06:19.597 --> 01:06:22.890
need to take responsibility for a need to think about.

01:06:22.890 --> 01:06:25.950
But it really all begins with this inward journey

01:06:25.950 --> 01:06:28.570
of self-connection, learning how to love yourself

01:06:28.570 --> 01:06:33.390
and also learning how to pay attention to what moves you.

01:06:33.390 --> 01:06:36.770
That's really, you know, the first base of this whole thing.

01:06:36.770 --> 01:06:37.603
I love that.

01:06:38.720 --> 01:06:40.780
You know, as you know, 'cause you've read it.

01:06:40.780 --> 01:06:42.733
I opened by saying, I failed to write this book

01:06:42.733 --> 01:06:43.900
that you're now reading

01:06:43.900 --> 01:06:45.480
because I couldn't claim the authority

01:06:45.480 --> 01:06:46.602
my publisher had given me

01:06:46.602 --> 01:06:49.480
and given me a book contract to write it.

01:06:49.480 --> 01:06:50.633
And then I appreciated.

01:06:50.633 --> 01:06:53.060
Who is an authority on adulting?

01:06:53.060 --> 01:06:54.710
Either we all are or one of us is, right.

01:06:54.710 --> 01:06:56.035
It's not an authority in the traditional sense.

01:06:56.035 --> 01:06:56.890
How dare you-

01:06:56.890 --> 01:06:57.980
There isn't a PhD-

01:06:57.980 --> 01:06:58.813
Hold yourself out as an authority?

01:06:58.813 --> 01:06:59.646
Exactly.

01:06:59.646 --> 01:07:02.710
There isn't a PhD in adulting I failed to obtain for myself.

01:07:02.710 --> 01:07:07.006
I came to appreciate, I care about humans, I observe humans.

01:07:07.006 --> 01:07:09.900
I've studied my own life pretty thoroughly.

01:07:09.900 --> 01:07:12.230
Therefore I think being older,

01:07:12.230 --> 01:07:15.690
farther into this landscape called adulting,

01:07:15.690 --> 01:07:18.090
I can take what I know, turn around

01:07:18.090 --> 01:07:22.490
and shine a warm light back on those younger than me.

01:07:22.490 --> 01:07:23.669
That's all I'm trying to do.

01:07:23.669 --> 01:07:26.630
The failings and the fumblings and the failings

01:07:26.630 --> 01:07:28.930
and all the other stuff that you talk about

01:07:28.930 --> 01:07:30.080
having gone through,

01:07:30.080 --> 01:07:31.419
I call those life's beautiful efforts.

01:07:31.419 --> 01:07:34.170
We need them.

01:07:34.170 --> 01:07:35.639
They are our teachers.

01:07:35.639 --> 01:07:38.550
You know, when you screwed up in the pool,

01:07:38.550 --> 01:07:39.990
your coach told you you screwed up.

01:07:39.990 --> 01:07:41.270
They told, maybe you watch tape,

01:07:41.270 --> 01:07:42.710
I don't know what swimmers do, but like you,

01:07:42.710 --> 01:07:44.460
you focus on bend your arm this way

01:07:44.460 --> 01:07:47.090
or kick your legs that way, or take your breath this way.

01:07:47.090 --> 01:07:49.360
You know, you screwed up and that allows you

01:07:49.360 --> 01:07:51.763
to focus in on what you need to do differently and improve.

01:07:51.763 --> 01:07:53.537
This is what life is.

01:07:53.537 --> 01:07:56.160
It's a continual opportunity

01:07:56.160 --> 01:07:58.980
to get better and better and better and clearer

01:07:58.980 --> 01:08:01.380
on being who we are.

01:08:01.380 --> 01:08:02.960
And so I'm hearing this book say, yeah,

01:08:02.960 --> 01:08:03.793
you're gonna screw up.

01:08:03.793 --> 01:08:04.850
Yeah, you're not perfect.

01:08:04.850 --> 01:08:08.160
Forget that, that is not the destination you seek.

01:08:08.160 --> 01:08:09.750
It is a fallacy.

01:08:09.750 --> 01:08:12.040
You're here to learn and grow.

01:08:12.040 --> 01:08:13.850
You know, I think we need a referendum

01:08:13.850 --> 01:08:16.560
on what we're talking when we talk about failure.

01:08:16.560 --> 01:08:19.380
I wish there was a different word that could replace that.

01:08:19.380 --> 01:08:20.280
Yeah, 'cause nobody wants to fail.

01:08:20.280 --> 01:08:24.970
Nobody does, but also no success is born

01:08:24.970 --> 01:08:26.270
out of failing to fail.

01:08:26.270 --> 01:08:31.270
Like all success comes from a resiliency around failure

01:08:31.760 --> 01:08:34.300
and a tolerance level for messing up

01:08:34.300 --> 01:08:37.400
because those are the experiences that teach us the most.

01:08:37.400 --> 01:08:38.270
That's exactly right.

01:08:38.270 --> 01:08:40.960
And let me model what can happen after you fail.

01:08:40.960 --> 01:08:42.040
And you forget what you're about to say,

01:08:42.040 --> 01:08:45.626
you can come back to it, which is I came to realize,

01:08:45.626 --> 01:08:47.210
as I'm writing this book,

01:08:47.210 --> 01:08:48.909
maybe I'm not attending to my mind and body

01:08:48.909 --> 01:08:50.550
the way that I need to.

01:08:50.550 --> 01:08:52.220
I'm advising my younger readers,

01:08:52.220 --> 01:08:54.480
take good care of your mind and body.

01:08:54.480 --> 01:08:56.285
I say are sort of like the wheels of a bicycle.

01:08:56.285 --> 01:08:58.850
And you know, you're not gonna get where you wanna go

01:08:58.850 --> 01:09:00.924
if you're not attending to them.

01:09:00.924 --> 01:09:04.161
I'm now at a place relatively speaking

01:09:04.161 --> 01:09:06.966
through mindfulness of much greater self-awareness,

01:09:06.966 --> 01:09:09.090
which has led to self-love,

01:09:09.090 --> 01:09:13.450
having exercised the demons of internalized oppression

01:09:13.450 --> 01:09:16.050
that were in me saying you're not worthy as a black person.

01:09:16.050 --> 01:09:19.690
I have dislodged them from my spirit and soul.

01:09:19.690 --> 01:09:21.566
I've become clear on who I am and what I want

01:09:21.566 --> 01:09:24.924
and where I wanna learn and grow now next.

01:09:24.924 --> 01:09:28.900
And now my prayer, I'm not a religious person,

01:09:28.900 --> 01:09:30.437
I'm a spiritual, but not religious person.

01:09:30.437 --> 01:09:35.437
Now my ask is please let me live long enough

01:09:35.815 --> 01:09:40.520
in this being that finally loves and knows herself.

01:09:41.540 --> 01:09:44.700
And this is a sentence I could not have uttered at 21.

01:09:45.623 --> 01:09:49.630
And I am delighted for the joy that fills me

01:09:49.630 --> 01:09:51.320
when I say that.

01:09:51.320 --> 01:09:55.450
I think I'm saying like, I am directly working my shit out.

01:09:55.450 --> 01:09:58.030
I am in that process, and I have been through a lot

01:09:58.030 --> 01:09:59.221
and there will be more.

01:09:59.221 --> 01:10:03.381
And I know that the process is the work and is the life.

01:10:03.381 --> 01:10:04.886
And I am loving it.

01:10:04.886 --> 01:10:08.540
Please, now that I have come to this understanding

01:10:08.540 --> 01:10:13.130
of what life is, let me have as much of it as possible.

01:10:14.710 --> 01:10:15.543
Beautiful.

01:10:15.543 --> 01:10:16.520
Oh, shit.

01:10:16.520 --> 01:10:18.576
The poet in you just came out.

01:10:18.576 --> 01:10:20.700
(Julie laughs)

01:10:20.700 --> 01:10:21.533
I love that.

01:10:21.533 --> 01:10:24.380
I mean, what is the process look like for you

01:10:24.380 --> 01:10:25.550
to get to that place?

01:10:25.550 --> 01:10:28.680
Like what specifically were the things that you were doing

01:10:28.680 --> 01:10:33.680
to wrestle with the demons and exercise that?

01:10:34.410 --> 01:10:36.739
You know, I have a little hesitancy

01:10:36.739 --> 01:10:41.000
engaging with you on this because of who you are,

01:10:41.000 --> 01:10:43.400
because you're an elite athlete,

01:10:43.400 --> 01:10:45.770
because you are plant-based and vegan.

01:10:45.770 --> 01:10:48.460
And I'm just saying like, let me just say, right,

01:10:48.460 --> 01:10:49.480
I'm not that person.

01:10:49.480 --> 01:10:51.610
You are that person, I know you're not judging me,

01:10:51.610 --> 01:10:52.627
I'm pretty clear.

01:10:52.627 --> 01:10:56.391
And yet just as if I was in the presence

01:10:56.391 --> 01:10:59.840
of an Olympic athlete and I was an amateur in the sport,

01:10:59.840 --> 01:11:01.990
I would be like, okay, can I really share my struggles

01:11:01.990 --> 01:11:02.840
with this person?

01:11:04.200 --> 01:11:06.910
Rich, I was afraid to go to doctors most of my life.

01:11:06.910 --> 01:11:10.520
And that's because when you and I were juniors

01:11:10.520 --> 01:11:11.664
at Stanford University,

01:11:11.664 --> 01:11:15.069
I went into the Student Health Center

01:11:15.069 --> 01:11:19.690
for a bronchial cough that had dogged me for five weeks.

01:11:19.690 --> 01:11:22.190
And the doctor put me on a 1200 calorie diet.

01:11:23.374 --> 01:11:25.980
He said, do you know how much weight you've gained

01:11:25.980 --> 01:11:27.442
since you got to Stanford?

01:11:27.442 --> 01:11:31.400
I was rowing for the women's crew team freshman year.

01:11:31.400 --> 01:11:32.260
I shouldn't be a rower.

01:11:32.260 --> 01:11:34.220
I'm not tall enough to row, but I loved it.

01:11:34.220 --> 01:11:37.560
I stroked the third boat, Women's Novice Crew,

01:11:37.560 --> 01:11:38.982
the best of the worst.

01:11:38.982 --> 01:11:41.870
And felt the pride of wearing my uniform

01:11:41.870 --> 01:11:43.038
and rowing for Stanford.

01:11:43.038 --> 01:11:46.010
And I put on weight when I stopped rowing.

01:11:46.010 --> 01:11:48.560
I'd put on about 30 pounds in two and a half years.

01:11:50.090 --> 01:11:52.820
So I was a lot less than I am now, I assure you.

01:11:52.820 --> 01:11:55.970
I was about 175 pounds that day,

01:11:55.970 --> 01:11:58.090
which is far less than I am now.

01:11:58.090 --> 01:12:00.214
And that doctor, instead of treating my cough,

01:12:00.214 --> 01:12:03.060
said, do you know how much weight you've gained

01:12:03.060 --> 01:12:03.893
since you got here?

01:12:03.893 --> 01:12:04.726
And I didn't know.

01:12:04.726 --> 01:12:07.210
And he came out with, went out and found a flyer

01:12:07.210 --> 01:12:09.635
with a 1200 calorie diet.

01:12:09.635 --> 01:12:13.600
And I learned, and I said, okay, what about my cough?

01:12:13.600 --> 01:12:15.513
And he said, oh, we'll give you antibiotics for that.

01:12:15.513 --> 01:12:18.150
And I learned in that day

01:12:18.150 --> 01:12:20.310
that when I have a legitimate health issue,

01:12:20.310 --> 01:12:22.340
it will be about my weight.

01:12:22.340 --> 01:12:25.320
And it kept me from seeking a doctor's advice

01:12:25.320 --> 01:12:27.250
when I couldn't conceive for two and a half years,

01:12:27.250 --> 01:12:29.650
'cause I was worried I was too heavy to have a child.

01:12:29.650 --> 01:12:31.870
I wasn't, okay.

01:12:31.870 --> 01:12:33.530
I was afraid.

01:12:33.530 --> 01:12:35.570
And in writing this book to try to help other people

01:12:35.570 --> 01:12:37.520
on their journey, I said, go damn, Julie,

01:12:37.520 --> 01:12:38.650
get into the doctor.

01:12:40.000 --> 01:12:43.656
And I discovered that through a sleep study,

01:12:43.656 --> 01:12:47.761
I wake up gasping for air at night

01:12:47.761 --> 01:12:51.630
and I've been wearing a C-PAP machine now for two weeks.

01:12:51.630 --> 01:12:54.391
And it has changed my life.

01:12:54.391 --> 01:12:59.391
I'm thinking clearly now, I have this sense of what it means

01:13:01.920 --> 01:13:04.000
to wake rested.

01:13:04.000 --> 01:13:06.800
I'm looking after that self and it may sound weird

01:13:06.800 --> 01:13:09.890
or simple to people, but I'm telling you my journey

01:13:09.890 --> 01:13:14.890
to that mask, to that diagnosis, to that doctor's office

01:13:15.580 --> 01:13:16.570
was a long one.

01:13:16.570 --> 01:13:20.190
And I'm glad that I went and anyone listening,

01:13:20.190 --> 01:13:22.370
who's like got a nagging anything,

01:13:22.370 --> 01:13:24.430
wherever it might be in your mind or body.

01:13:24.430 --> 01:13:27.310
And you're like, yeah, I probably need to get on in there.

01:13:27.310 --> 01:13:31.180
Go, my voice is hear saying go, you know.

01:13:31.180 --> 01:13:36.180
Information is largely good and you deserve it.

01:13:37.140 --> 01:13:40.835
If you have the privilege of access to healthcare

01:13:40.835 --> 01:13:45.190
and a way to pay for it, go and get it.

01:13:45.190 --> 01:13:48.631
I'm sorry that you had that experience at Stanford

01:13:48.631 --> 01:13:53.631
and that it took so long for you to come to terms with that.

01:13:54.737 --> 01:13:57.490
That's heartbreaking, but I'm glad.

01:13:57.490 --> 01:14:00.350
It sounds like, you know, that was a big solution for you.

01:14:00.350 --> 01:14:01.410
So that's cool.

01:14:01.410 --> 01:14:05.150
And I can't help but think that there's a relationship

01:14:05.150 --> 01:14:09.860
between your writing and problem solving.

01:14:09.860 --> 01:14:13.819
Like real American, really is the product of you

01:14:13.819 --> 01:14:18.130
trying to make sense of this idea of racial identity

01:14:18.130 --> 01:14:21.990
for yourself and in the process of writing these other,

01:14:21.990 --> 01:14:25.622
you know, kind of parenting, adulting books,

01:14:25.622 --> 01:14:27.720
you're able to kind of solve

01:14:27.720 --> 01:14:30.400
some of these other lingering problems

01:14:30.400 --> 01:14:31.280
that you've had, right.

01:14:31.280 --> 01:14:33.450
Like they're living, breathing documents

01:14:33.450 --> 01:14:36.710
that are compelling you to live the truth

01:14:36.710 --> 01:14:39.260
that you're speaking on the pages.

01:14:39.260 --> 01:14:43.310
Absolutely, I'm trying to achieve that.

01:14:43.310 --> 01:14:44.810
I think I do experience that

01:14:46.141 --> 01:14:48.035
and I'm trying to be of service.

01:14:48.035 --> 01:14:50.860
I believe you can write all you want

01:14:50.860 --> 01:14:52.050
about your life and your journal,

01:14:52.050 --> 01:14:53.500
but if you're gonna take the leap and say,

01:14:53.500 --> 01:14:55.880
other people should read it, I'm going to publish it.

01:14:55.880 --> 01:14:57.950
It damn well better be because you wanna be of service.

01:14:57.950 --> 01:14:59.133
And think you can.

01:14:59.133 --> 01:15:00.880
And the joy that I've experienced

01:15:00.880 --> 01:15:03.910
is the author of "Real American," which came out about,

01:15:03.910 --> 01:15:04.902
close to four years ago now,

01:15:04.902 --> 01:15:06.780
and the book signing line,

01:15:06.780 --> 01:15:09.720
black and brown people will linger to the back.

01:15:09.720 --> 01:15:11.260
Queer people will linger to the back.

01:15:11.260 --> 01:15:13.550
People who've been marginalized and otherized

01:15:13.550 --> 01:15:14.664
for other reasons will linger

01:15:14.664 --> 01:15:16.960
and then they'll come, and you know what this is like

01:15:16.960 --> 01:15:19.060
when people come to the book signing line,

01:15:20.066 --> 01:15:22.190
and they lean in and they reach out for your hand

01:15:22.190 --> 01:15:25.318
and they say, thank you for telling your story,

01:15:25.318 --> 01:15:29.610
I feel seen, I feel less alone.

01:15:29.610 --> 01:15:31.340
I've never said the things out loud,

01:15:31.340 --> 01:15:33.510
you just delivered in your reading

01:15:33.510 --> 01:15:36.120
or that are between the pages of this book.

01:15:36.120 --> 01:15:39.316
And when that happens to me, I hold the person's hand

01:15:39.316 --> 01:15:41.140
and we go back and forth and I smile

01:15:41.140 --> 01:15:42.460
and I look at them in the eye and I say,

01:15:42.460 --> 01:15:43.870
I wrote this for all of us.

01:15:45.240 --> 01:15:46.782
And that's how I feel about the new book.

01:15:46.782 --> 01:15:50.280
I'm just, I'm here rooting for everyone to make it.

01:15:50.280 --> 01:15:52.590
And I feel like I don't have all the answers,

01:15:52.590 --> 01:15:55.900
but maybe I can write a book that is like a mirror

01:15:55.900 --> 01:15:58.190
that I hold up to you and you see yourself

01:15:58.190 --> 01:16:00.310
where you need to in its pages.

01:16:01.180 --> 01:16:04.579
Every reader is gonna relate differently, right.

01:16:04.579 --> 01:16:06.880
Well, one of the reasons why this book is so long,

01:16:06.880 --> 01:16:08.130
and I should qualify not,

01:16:08.130 --> 01:16:10.382
I haven't gotten all the way through it.

01:16:10.382 --> 01:16:11.907
I'm still in the middle of it.

01:16:11.907 --> 01:16:14.430
But one of the reasons why it's like 500 pages

01:16:14.430 --> 01:16:17.897
is because a lot of it is sharing the stories

01:16:17.897 --> 01:16:21.270
of this just wide diversity

01:16:21.270 --> 01:16:23.010
of young people's experiences, right.

01:16:23.010 --> 01:16:25.510
Like you interviewed or conversed

01:16:25.510 --> 01:16:28.970
with so many different types of people

01:16:28.970 --> 01:16:30.400
from different walks of life

01:16:30.400 --> 01:16:35.400
so that I can only presume, the reader will be able to find

01:16:36.370 --> 01:16:37.900
the thing that they're looking for.

01:16:37.900 --> 01:16:38.733
That's exactly right-

01:16:38.733 --> 01:16:41.050
Or what they need to hear from the right person.

01:16:41.050 --> 01:16:43.003
Like it's not just about the information,

01:16:43.003 --> 01:16:44.942
it's who who's delivering the information

01:16:44.942 --> 01:16:47.130
in a manner in which I can hear it

01:16:47.130 --> 01:16:48.677
because I relate to that person's experience.

01:16:48.677 --> 01:16:50.170
Yeah.

01:16:50.170 --> 01:16:53.450
So back to what I said about never belonging,

01:16:53.450 --> 01:16:54.680
being otherized from the start,

01:16:54.680 --> 01:16:55.727
I know what that feels like.

01:16:55.727 --> 01:16:58.130
I know what it feels like to open a non-fiction book

01:16:58.130 --> 01:17:00.400
and feel like it was written for white middle-class

01:17:00.400 --> 01:17:01.233
straight folk.

01:17:01.233 --> 01:17:02.840
Right, that's like my book.

01:17:02.840 --> 01:17:05.475
Well, But now you know, don't do that, right.

01:17:05.475 --> 01:17:09.676
White folks had to think about those as a routine matter

01:17:09.676 --> 01:17:12.705
because y'all are the, what we call the majority

01:17:12.705 --> 01:17:13.780
and the norm.

01:17:13.780 --> 01:17:15.196
And well, those things are thankfully

01:17:15.196 --> 01:17:17.030
starting to be questioned and change.

01:17:17.030 --> 01:17:19.210
And I'm trying to do my small part as an author to say,

01:17:19.210 --> 01:17:20.478
I'm gonna write a nonfiction book

01:17:20.478 --> 01:17:22.167
that purports to be for everybody

01:17:22.167 --> 01:17:23.791
and to demonstrate that it is.

01:17:23.791 --> 01:17:26.560
I'm gonna try to make sure that I'm careful

01:17:26.560 --> 01:17:29.580
to mention and bring in, quote, unquote, everybody.

01:17:29.580 --> 01:17:31.430
If I say you need to make eye contact,

01:17:31.430 --> 01:17:32.672
I'm gonna say this may be hard for you

01:17:32.672 --> 01:17:34.261
if culturally people like you

01:17:34.261 --> 01:17:35.535
aren't supposed to make eye contact,

01:17:35.535 --> 01:17:37.996
or you have social anxiety, you know,

01:17:37.996 --> 01:17:39.680
or you are on the spectrum.

01:17:39.680 --> 01:17:42.850
I try to, in the advice-giving, acknowledge the folks,

01:17:42.850 --> 01:17:45.707
you know, to whom, for whom it might be different.

01:17:45.707 --> 01:17:50.130
I'm also trying to say, don't dare give examples

01:17:50.130 --> 01:17:51.760
that only emanate from one narrow swath

01:17:51.760 --> 01:17:53.035
of the human community.

01:17:53.035 --> 01:17:55.620
Everybody does matter.

01:17:55.620 --> 01:17:57.850
Everybody should matter.

01:17:57.850 --> 01:18:01.260
If everyone is to matter, we got to do a much better job

01:18:01.260 --> 01:18:04.260
of bringing those who have traditionally been marginalized

01:18:04.260 --> 01:18:05.987
onto the center of the page.

01:18:05.987 --> 01:18:10.647
There's so many interesting and compelling ideas

01:18:10.647 --> 01:18:13.170
in the book, and we obviously don't have time

01:18:13.170 --> 01:18:16.460
to talk about all of them,

01:18:16.460 --> 01:18:18.810
but there's a couple that I would like to highlight.

01:18:18.810 --> 01:18:23.030
Not the least of which is this idea of fending, right.

01:18:23.030 --> 01:18:24.030
So explain that meaning.

01:18:24.030 --> 01:18:25.130
So what is fending?
Yeah.

01:18:25.130 --> 01:18:26.130
You know, it's funny, Rich,

01:18:26.130 --> 01:18:27.224
since the book came out,

01:18:27.224 --> 01:18:29.440
people have really called out that term.

01:18:29.440 --> 01:18:30.660
They're like, fending, do you use that term?

01:18:30.660 --> 01:18:32.370
I was like, wait, what does that mean, exactly?

01:18:32.370 --> 01:18:36.440
I know defending, fending for yourself.

01:18:36.440 --> 01:18:38.140
So all I can say is this term

01:18:38.140 --> 01:18:39.380
was such a part of my childhood.

01:18:39.380 --> 01:18:41.200
My parents must have constantly been talking

01:18:41.200 --> 01:18:43.780
about when you're growing up, you fend for yourself.

01:18:43.780 --> 01:18:46.755
Because to me, it's a word that we use, like any other word.

01:18:46.755 --> 01:18:51.755
Fending is this visceral animal kingdom truth

01:18:53.640 --> 01:18:55.770
that applies to us humans.

01:18:55.770 --> 01:18:58.570
We're mammals, can we posit that, right?

01:18:58.570 --> 01:18:59.730
We we're mammals.

01:18:59.730 --> 01:19:02.830
We have jackets and cell phones, right?

01:19:02.830 --> 01:19:07.366
We wear fancy watches and sneakers and we are mammals.

01:19:07.366 --> 01:19:09.267
And like any mammal parent,

01:19:09.267 --> 01:19:14.267
we have to know for our own biological determination,

01:19:14.370 --> 01:19:16.570
like we have to know our genes will get passed on.

01:19:16.570 --> 01:19:17.920
How do our genes get passed on?

01:19:17.920 --> 01:19:19.710
If our kids can fend for themselves out in the world

01:19:19.710 --> 01:19:22.260
without us like any mammal parent.

01:19:22.260 --> 01:19:23.560
We need to know that.

01:19:23.560 --> 01:19:26.417
So fending comprises those small set of things,

01:19:26.417 --> 01:19:30.200
small, basic, fundamental set of things

01:19:30.200 --> 01:19:31.337
that humans gotta be able to do

01:19:31.337 --> 01:19:33.980
in order to take care of business.

01:19:33.980 --> 01:19:37.097
Take care of their body, their belongings, their bills.

01:19:37.097 --> 01:19:41.420
Adulting is you know you are more or less responsible

01:19:41.420 --> 01:19:42.253
for yourself.

01:19:42.253 --> 01:19:43.620
It doesn't mean you go to loan,

01:19:43.620 --> 01:19:45.200
but more or less your responsible.

01:19:45.200 --> 01:19:46.520
So childhood someone else

01:19:46.520 --> 01:19:48.230
was more or less responsible for you.

01:19:48.230 --> 01:19:49.187
Some of your parents could show up

01:19:49.187 --> 01:19:50.581
and did show up to look after you.

01:19:50.581 --> 01:19:54.569
So fending is that I'm responsible for myself more or less.

01:19:54.569 --> 01:19:57.690
It's the basics, which is why it's only, you know,

01:19:57.690 --> 01:19:59.680
chapter two out of 13.

01:19:59.680 --> 01:20:01.591
Right, and I feel like

01:20:01.591 --> 01:20:05.910
parents understand this intellectually,

01:20:05.910 --> 01:20:09.750
but in practice, end up kind of doing it ourselves for them

01:20:09.750 --> 01:20:10.980
because it's just easier.

01:20:10.980 --> 01:20:14.200
And we believe, or we delude ourselves into thinking

01:20:14.200 --> 01:20:17.070
that by doing it while they're observing it,

01:20:17.070 --> 01:20:18.460
that they're learning how to do it,

01:20:18.460 --> 01:20:20.404
but actually you're handicapping them

01:20:20.404 --> 01:20:22.289
and creating a dependency.

01:20:22.289 --> 01:20:24.810
So there's actually a four-step method

01:20:24.810 --> 01:20:26.890
for teaching any kid any skill.

01:20:26.890 --> 01:20:30.310
And we'd seem to be ignoring steps two and three

01:20:30.310 --> 01:20:33.050
and expecting them to go from step one to step four.

01:20:33.050 --> 01:20:34.490
Step one is we do it for them.

01:20:34.490 --> 01:20:35.817
Step two as we do it with them.

01:20:35.817 --> 01:20:39.090
Step three is we flip the tables and watch them do it,

01:20:39.090 --> 01:20:40.290
but we're still there.

01:20:40.290 --> 01:20:41.763
Step four, as they can do it.

01:20:41.763 --> 01:20:43.920
When we're over parenting we're in step one,

01:20:43.920 --> 01:20:46.810
may be step two, where they're handling fixing.

01:20:46.810 --> 01:20:48.346
Of course we can do it, Rich.

01:20:48.346 --> 01:20:50.836
We've been fourth graders.

01:20:50.836 --> 01:20:52.682
It's their turn, right.

01:20:52.682 --> 01:20:53.927
Okay, we're -

01:20:53.927 --> 01:20:56.415
But I'm late, you know, I got to get to work and you know,

01:20:56.415 --> 01:20:58.860
this kid can't tie his shoes and I gotta get going-

01:20:58.860 --> 01:21:00.630
Exactly, so if they gonna tie it,

01:21:00.630 --> 01:21:02.447
then you have an eight year old who can't tie their shoes

01:21:02.447 --> 01:21:04.620
or I'm too busy to teach them to cross the streets.

01:21:04.620 --> 01:21:07.670
Then you have a 12-year-old who's never crossed the street

01:21:07.670 --> 01:21:08.503
by themselves.

01:21:08.503 --> 01:21:10.930
So you can see the ways in which we undermined.

01:21:10.930 --> 01:21:12.932
We have to parent with a little bit more patience.

01:21:12.932 --> 01:21:16.048
And for the longer term, I like to tell folks,

01:21:16.048 --> 01:21:18.722
like it or not, and nobody likes it,

01:21:18.722 --> 01:21:19.790
we'll be dead one day,

01:21:19.790 --> 01:21:23.170
we have to raise our kids so that when we are gone,

01:21:23.170 --> 01:21:25.788
and hopefully well before, they've got it.

01:21:25.788 --> 01:21:28.757
They can look after us in our old age.

01:21:28.757 --> 01:21:32.357
Obviously, there's a spectrum between helicopter parenting

01:21:32.357 --> 01:21:37.180
and like kind of, you know, flagrant disregard.

01:21:37.180 --> 01:21:38.990
So where's the line.

01:21:38.990 --> 01:21:40.810
How do we find that sweet spot?

01:21:40.810 --> 01:21:42.010
Or where is that line?

01:21:42.010 --> 01:21:43.420
Like identifying that.

01:21:43.420 --> 01:21:46.890
I suspect it's gonna be different with every kid, yes.

01:21:48.340 --> 01:21:49.510
Sure, it's different for every kid.

01:21:49.510 --> 01:21:50.750
Just as you've got your four

01:21:50.750 --> 01:21:52.380
and you're gonna pay attention to their needs

01:21:52.380 --> 01:21:53.366
and proclivities and whatnot,

01:21:53.366 --> 01:21:55.310
and do things slightly differently.

01:21:55.310 --> 01:21:58.440
Every kid is different and yet there are fundamental things.

01:21:58.440 --> 01:22:01.960
So I'm gonna use the sport of bowling as a visual metaphor

01:22:01.960 --> 01:22:04.650
for where you draw the line

01:22:04.650 --> 01:22:05.752
or where you put up the bumpers.

01:22:05.752 --> 01:22:08.513
Okay, when somebody is brand new at bowling,

01:22:08.513 --> 01:22:11.690
you raise the bumpers so that their ball

01:22:11.690 --> 01:22:12.940
doesn't go into the gutter.

01:22:12.940 --> 01:22:15.461
So they have some chance of hitting up in

01:22:15.461 --> 01:22:18.730
and not just flailing, not just, right.

01:22:19.760 --> 01:22:22.960
When we over parent it's as if we're taking those bumpers.

01:22:22.960 --> 01:22:24.690
And instead of just putting them at the edge

01:22:24.690 --> 01:22:25.523
where the gutters are,

01:22:25.523 --> 01:22:27.173
which the parenting equivalent of is,

01:22:27.173 --> 01:22:28.705
make sure you kid doesn't drown,

01:22:28.705 --> 01:22:31.252
make sure your kid doesn't walk into traffic.

01:22:31.252 --> 01:22:33.580
Okay, life or death,

01:22:33.580 --> 01:22:35.961
we're supposed to protect them from dying, okay.

01:22:35.961 --> 01:22:38.260
And let them have the experiences of life.

01:22:38.260 --> 01:22:40.480
Let that ball meander and maybe it'll hit a pen or two

01:22:40.480 --> 01:22:42.500
and maybe over time, it'll hit three and then five

01:22:42.500 --> 01:22:43.380
and then 10.

01:22:43.380 --> 01:22:45.280
Van we bring the bumpers or the guardrails

01:22:45.280 --> 01:22:46.360
or whatever they're called, clearly,

01:22:46.360 --> 01:22:47.509
I'm not actually a bowler.

01:22:47.509 --> 01:22:50.020
We bring them in so narrowly.

01:22:50.020 --> 01:22:50.853
And if you could see me now,

01:22:50.853 --> 01:22:53.680
I'm holding my hands at the width of a bowling ball,

01:22:53.680 --> 01:22:56.200
so that the kid just throws the ball and we are right there,

01:22:56.200 --> 01:22:57.437
making sure it goes straight down.

01:22:57.437 --> 01:22:59.961
And they miss out on the experiences

01:22:59.961 --> 01:23:03.800
that will teach them how to throw the ball straighter

01:23:03.800 --> 01:23:07.820
and more confidently and with greater heft, okay.

01:23:07.820 --> 01:23:11.320
That's that's the visual, okay.

01:23:11.320 --> 01:23:13.400
We're also supposed to be delighted

01:23:13.400 --> 01:23:14.990
by them learning a skill.

01:23:14.990 --> 01:23:17.687
So overpay, parents say, when do you start?

01:23:17.687 --> 01:23:19.026
Where's the line, is a question I get,

01:23:19.026 --> 01:23:21.200
when do you start giving them independence?

01:23:21.200 --> 01:23:24.260
I also get, and I say back, why did you stop?

01:23:24.260 --> 01:23:25.390
When they were learning to walk,

01:23:25.390 --> 01:23:26.253
you didn't over parent.

01:23:26.253 --> 01:23:29.030
They fell, you didn't yell, get up.

01:23:29.030 --> 01:23:30.400
We're embarrassed by you.

01:23:30.400 --> 01:23:31.950
You know, your older brother walked in her, right?

01:23:31.950 --> 01:23:33.160
We don't do that.

01:23:33.160 --> 01:23:37.311
We take pictures and clap and laugh and video grandma.

01:23:37.311 --> 01:23:40.270
If we over-parented in that moment,

01:23:40.270 --> 01:23:42.720
it would be to get behind them on there and on our knees

01:23:42.720 --> 01:23:43.968
and put our hands under their armpits

01:23:43.968 --> 01:23:47.648
and slowly walk them forward toward the other parent.

01:23:47.648 --> 01:23:50.530
And then we'd get to the end and say, we walked, right.

01:23:50.530 --> 01:23:51.598
No, you didn't.

01:23:51.598 --> 01:23:52.641
You did, the kid didn't.

01:23:52.641 --> 01:23:55.690
If they forever fall into your strong body,

01:23:55.690 --> 01:23:57.204
they haven't learned the skill.

01:23:57.204 --> 01:24:01.200
So the right line there is remove the objects

01:24:01.200 --> 01:24:03.140
on which they can impale themselves if they fall

01:24:03.140 --> 01:24:04.584
while learning to walk.

01:24:04.584 --> 01:24:06.274
Make sure the environment is safe.

01:24:06.274 --> 01:24:09.290
But let them go and do it.

01:24:09.290 --> 01:24:10.540
Yeah, yeah.

01:24:10.540 --> 01:24:13.550
Over time, of course, this leads to,

01:24:13.550 --> 01:24:17.110
the messaging is you're not capable of doing it yourself.

01:24:17.110 --> 01:24:18.610
And that's why I'm helpings there, right.

01:24:18.610 --> 01:24:21.110
And that, that really is pernicious.

01:24:21.110 --> 01:24:23.520
And it messes with their developing brain

01:24:23.520 --> 01:24:24.607
and their sense of self.

01:24:24.607 --> 01:24:28.340
And it's hard to correlate a parenting style with anything

01:24:28.340 --> 01:24:29.900
because parenting is 24/7,

01:24:29.900 --> 01:24:31.690
and it's hard to have a control group of children

01:24:31.690 --> 01:24:33.830
who weren't parented that way, right.

01:24:33.830 --> 01:24:37.264
But people who study this have correlated

01:24:37.264 --> 01:24:39.164
the over-involved parenting style

01:24:39.164 --> 01:24:42.160
with higher rates of anxiety and depression in children,

01:24:42.160 --> 01:24:45.180
because we are undermining the development of agency,

01:24:45.180 --> 01:24:46.740
which is the sense I can,

01:24:46.740 --> 01:24:48.200
and the development of resilience

01:24:48.200 --> 01:24:50.260
that comes from, you know, things not going great.

01:24:50.260 --> 01:24:52.300
And you discover, I can cope.

01:24:52.300 --> 01:24:54.130
We undermine the development of these things,

01:24:54.130 --> 01:24:57.400
which really are the foundations of a healthy life,

01:24:57.400 --> 01:24:59.000
when we over-help.

01:24:59.000 --> 01:25:01.260
There's an addiction piece in there too, right?

01:25:01.260 --> 01:25:05.790
The more deprived of that agency at a young age,

01:25:05.790 --> 01:25:08.320
the more there is there's a through line

01:25:08.320 --> 01:25:09.680
with addiction later in life,

01:25:09.680 --> 01:25:11.315
or when they get to college, et cetera.

01:25:11.315 --> 01:25:12.680
Tell me, I'm not-

01:25:12.680 --> 01:25:13.860
I know, I thought that was something

01:25:13.860 --> 01:25:17.720
that was addressed in something that I read in your work,

01:25:17.720 --> 01:25:19.920
or at least over consumption like this idea.

01:25:19.920 --> 01:25:21.950
I mean, it's certainly, it's my story.

01:25:21.950 --> 01:25:25.260
Like getting 3000 miles away, suddenly being in college

01:25:25.260 --> 01:25:27.410
and on some level, you know,

01:25:27.410 --> 01:25:32.249
free from the day-to-day kind of oversight,

01:25:32.249 --> 01:25:35.532
that was a little bit overwhelming for me.

01:25:35.532 --> 01:25:37.360
I went in the other direction

01:25:37.360 --> 01:25:38.740
and kind of went a little bit nuts.

01:25:38.740 --> 01:25:41.020
And I, you know, I have to believe

01:25:41.020 --> 01:25:43.780
that that's a fairly common experience.

01:25:43.780 --> 01:25:44.936
I imagine that's true, absolutely.

01:25:44.936 --> 01:25:49.330
You know, what I read about is what I hear about

01:25:49.330 --> 01:25:53.618
when I hear college administrators and high school leaders,

01:25:53.618 --> 01:25:56.214
I'm talking about kids, interacting with kids,

01:25:56.214 --> 01:26:00.120
the pre-gaming that can happen,

01:26:00.120 --> 01:26:02.710
that does happen, of course, before parties,

01:26:02.710 --> 01:26:03.850
and before events.

01:26:03.850 --> 01:26:06.326
This is all pre-pandemic when getting together was easier.

01:26:06.326 --> 01:26:11.326
Many people hypothesize that the pre-gaming today is in part

01:26:11.786 --> 01:26:15.700
to help reduce the tremendous anxiety around,

01:26:15.700 --> 01:26:16.765
I don't know how to talk to anybody.

01:26:16.765 --> 01:26:18.160
I don't know how to be with them.

01:26:18.160 --> 01:26:20.728
And I gotta get a little buzz on before I go to the party.

01:26:20.728 --> 01:26:21.715
So I can, I mean-

01:26:21.715 --> 01:26:22.840
So I can talk.

01:26:22.840 --> 01:26:24.650
I did that.

01:26:24.650 --> 01:26:26.620
Like, I completely relate to that.

01:26:26.620 --> 01:26:27.453
Yeah.

01:26:27.453 --> 01:26:28.286
Yeah, it's tough.

01:26:28.286 --> 01:26:31.760
I mean, that's a good segue into the whole thing

01:26:31.760 --> 01:26:33.071
about talking to strangers, right.

01:26:33.071 --> 01:26:33.904
Yeah.

01:26:33.904 --> 01:26:34.850
You know.
Yeah.

01:26:34.850 --> 01:26:36.900
It's like, I just see this with young people

01:26:36.900 --> 01:26:38.068
and with our youngest,

01:26:38.068 --> 01:26:40.860
the discomfort with having a conversation

01:26:40.860 --> 01:26:41.861
with a real human being

01:26:41.861 --> 01:26:46.468
when everything is app-based and text-based,

01:26:46.468 --> 01:26:48.500
it's like, I can't text the person.

01:26:48.500 --> 01:26:50.530
No, you have to pick up the phone and actually call them.

01:26:50.530 --> 01:26:53.420
And here's the question you need to ask, impossible.

01:26:53.420 --> 01:26:54.410
Right, so we've-
Impossible.

01:26:54.410 --> 01:26:56.920
We've got to train our kids how to use the phone,

01:26:56.920 --> 01:26:57.753
isn't that weird?

01:26:57.753 --> 01:27:00.630
But I'm here to say, I have done that,

01:27:00.630 --> 01:27:03.729
I have said okay, learning to talk on the phone is a skill.

01:27:03.729 --> 01:27:05.280
You've got to practice it.

01:27:05.280 --> 01:27:06.692
It's like hilarious that we are even talking about this.

01:27:06.692 --> 01:27:08.800
Rich, but here's why, but here's why, but here's why.

01:27:08.800 --> 01:27:09.980
You grew up in DC?

01:27:09.980 --> 01:27:10.813
Yeah.
All right.

01:27:10.813 --> 01:27:15.270
When you wanted to call a friend in middle school

01:27:15.270 --> 01:27:18.600
or high school, you called was a phone on the wall.

01:27:18.600 --> 01:27:20.470
Maybe there's a long cord and you took it into your bedroom

01:27:20.470 --> 01:27:21.870
if you're looking for privacy.

01:27:21.870 --> 01:27:26.010
But no idea who would answer the phone for the party line.

01:27:26.010 --> 01:27:28.350
Okay, so you would call and if you had manners,

01:27:28.350 --> 01:27:30.260
which I'm sure you did, you would say,

01:27:30.260 --> 01:27:34.700
hello, this is Rich or Rich Roll.

01:27:34.700 --> 01:27:37.760
May I, hello, Mr. Smith, this is Rich.

01:27:37.760 --> 01:27:40.620
May I please speak to whomever?

01:27:40.620 --> 01:27:41.794
You had to, oftentimes you had

01:27:41.794 --> 01:27:45.695
to get through your friend's parent or older sibling

01:27:45.695 --> 01:27:48.229
before you could get to your friend.

01:27:48.229 --> 01:27:49.940
Our kids don't have to do that.

01:27:49.940 --> 01:27:53.130
They don't have, there aren't these places of interference

01:27:53.130 --> 01:27:54.860
they can directly get to each other.

01:27:54.860 --> 01:27:57.080
So of course it must be scary to think of, oh my gosh,

01:27:57.080 --> 01:27:59.553
I've got to talk to somebody I don't know.

01:27:59.553 --> 01:28:01.700
So yes, we have to teach it.

01:28:01.700 --> 01:28:02.864
Yeah.
Yeah.

01:28:02.864 --> 01:28:04.790
We have to teach it.

01:28:05.630 --> 01:28:07.300
Just like we have to teach email etiquette,

01:28:07.300 --> 01:28:09.470
how do you, if you're gonna thrive in the workplace,

01:28:09.470 --> 01:28:12.170
you got to know how to write an email

01:28:12.170 --> 01:28:15.130
that feels respectful to whomever might be

01:28:15.130 --> 01:28:15.963
on the receiving end,

01:28:15.963 --> 01:28:19.280
whether a customer or a higher up or a colleague, right.

01:28:19.280 --> 01:28:20.491
We've got to teach these things.

01:28:20.491 --> 01:28:25.088
But the big one here is stop pleasing others,

01:28:25.088 --> 01:28:28.010
which goes to the people pleasing thing

01:28:28.010 --> 01:28:29.120
that you and I share.

01:28:29.120 --> 01:28:30.040
Absolutely.

01:28:30.040 --> 01:28:31.220
Yeah.

01:28:31.220 --> 01:28:32.053
Every time you say that-

01:28:32.053 --> 01:28:32.886
That's a tough one.

01:28:32.886 --> 01:28:33.719
It does make me giggle, yeah.

01:28:33.719 --> 01:28:34.570
Yes, no.

01:28:34.570 --> 01:28:37.764
100%, I have very much been a people pleaser.

01:28:37.764 --> 01:28:42.720
And frankly, once I became more self-loving,

01:28:42.720 --> 01:28:47.440
then I got rid of a good chunk of it.

01:28:48.937 --> 01:28:51.330
But it's still very much there, of course.

01:28:51.330 --> 01:28:52.310
I'm just aware of it now.

01:28:52.310 --> 01:28:54.250
And I'm aware of cropping up and I can feel it,

01:28:54.250 --> 01:28:55.730
and be like, oh, that's what you're doing here,

01:28:55.730 --> 01:28:56.850
people pleasing.

01:28:56.850 --> 01:28:58.660
That chapter is stop pleasing others.

01:28:58.660 --> 01:29:00.386
They have no idea who you are.

01:29:00.386 --> 01:29:04.350
And that subtitle is the kicker for me because I am here,

01:29:04.350 --> 01:29:05.760
remember rooting for them to thrive.

01:29:05.760 --> 01:29:08.540
So I'm saying like, it doesn't matter what your parent,

01:29:08.540 --> 01:29:10.830
your dad, your mom, your grandparents, your entire family,

01:29:10.830 --> 01:29:14.496
your ethnic community, your peers think is legitimate.

01:29:14.496 --> 01:29:17.004
This is your one wild and precious life.

01:29:17.004 --> 01:29:20.790
They do not know you better than than you know yourself.

01:29:20.790 --> 01:29:23.440
And if they say they do and you feel they might,

01:29:23.440 --> 01:29:24.848
that is problematic.

01:29:24.848 --> 01:29:27.640
You need a wrestle your life away from the people

01:29:27.640 --> 01:29:29.095
in whose hands it is being held.

01:29:29.095 --> 01:29:32.890
There's a bit of an ego piece in that as well because,

01:29:33.803 --> 01:29:37.660
because it presupposes that, I mean,

01:29:37.660 --> 01:29:38.710
your parents are going to care,

01:29:38.710 --> 01:29:40.800
but most people don't care, right.

01:29:40.800 --> 01:29:44.410
Like be you, most people would tell you that, right?

01:29:44.410 --> 01:29:46.700
But we feel like we have to be a certain way

01:29:46.700 --> 01:29:48.545
in order to get their approval

01:29:48.545 --> 01:29:51.260
or to be perceived in the way

01:29:51.260 --> 01:29:52.600
that we would like to be perceived

01:29:52.600 --> 01:29:55.390
missing the bigger picture,

01:29:55.390 --> 01:29:58.510
which is that people tend to gravitate

01:29:58.510 --> 01:30:00.880
towards people who feel self-actualized.

01:30:00.880 --> 01:30:02.679
Like it was an energy that that person admits

01:30:02.679 --> 01:30:06.810
that makes them, you know, infinitely more attractive

01:30:06.810 --> 01:30:11.276
than the person who is needy and just sort of coveting favor

01:30:11.276 --> 01:30:12.970
in that weird way.

01:30:12.970 --> 01:30:13.803
Absolutely.

01:30:13.803 --> 01:30:18.410
I saw so many college students who were majoring in pre-med

01:30:18.410 --> 01:30:21.020
'cause they had to, and maybe didn't wanna be pre-med

01:30:21.020 --> 01:30:25.780
and were biding the time until they had the white coat

01:30:25.780 --> 01:30:27.510
in the MD degree to tell their folks,

01:30:27.510 --> 01:30:28.898
hey, I don't wanna be a doctor anymore.

01:30:28.898 --> 01:30:33.240
But I also saw students who wanted to be nurses and MTs

01:30:33.240 --> 01:30:36.140
and were consistently told, oh, you're too smart for that.

01:30:36.980 --> 01:30:40.744
You know, go, go all the way and become a doctor,

01:30:40.744 --> 01:30:43.980
as if there's this linear path

01:30:43.980 --> 01:30:47.170
from one of those other professions to being a doctor.

01:30:47.170 --> 01:30:49.100
And somehow it is a failure to stop

01:30:49.100 --> 01:30:50.600
at the level of EMT or nurse.

01:30:50.600 --> 01:30:53.350
I mean, who's more on the front line saving lives

01:30:53.350 --> 01:30:56.090
in the moment constantly than an EMT?

01:30:56.090 --> 01:30:58.220
And who among us has the right to say,

01:30:58.220 --> 01:30:59.648
oh, that's beneath you.

01:30:59.648 --> 01:31:01.507
A lot of people who wanna teach

01:31:01.507 --> 01:31:03.290
and yet are at elite universities.

01:31:03.290 --> 01:31:06.040
Are told, oh no, go for the PhD and be a professor.

01:31:06.040 --> 01:31:07.930
Well, maybe you really wanna teach seventh graders

01:31:07.930 --> 01:31:10.380
because seventh grade was when somebody saw you

01:31:10.380 --> 01:31:12.400
in your capability, you didn't even believe in yourself,

01:31:12.400 --> 01:31:13.660
but they did, and they mentored you

01:31:13.660 --> 01:31:16.180
and now you're this thriving, successful person

01:31:16.180 --> 01:31:17.350
because of your seventh grade teacher

01:31:17.350 --> 01:31:18.770
and you wanna be that in a life

01:31:18.770 --> 01:31:20.106
of a new generation of kids.

01:31:20.106 --> 01:31:22.190
I'm here rooting for that to happen.

01:31:22.190 --> 01:31:26.480
Yeah, I do feel like there's been some tectonic shifts

01:31:26.480 --> 01:31:31.480
in education more broadly due to advances in technology

01:31:32.300 --> 01:31:35.040
and also kind of what the pandemic has reaped

01:31:35.040 --> 01:31:37.850
in terms of how we interface with education.

01:31:37.850 --> 01:31:42.850
And also, I hope, I feel like there's a little bit

01:31:42.980 --> 01:31:46.230
greater appreciation for trade than there used to be.

01:31:46.230 --> 01:31:49.270
Like it used to be, look, if you've got a good job

01:31:49.270 --> 01:31:51.830
as a trades person, you could support your family,

01:31:51.830 --> 01:31:52.942
you could own a home.

01:31:52.942 --> 01:31:56.410
And you know, that was respectful path.

01:31:56.410 --> 01:31:59.240
And along the way, we lost our respect for that.

01:31:59.240 --> 01:32:02.880
But now, because college has become so expensive

01:32:02.880 --> 01:32:05.480
and inaccessible to so many people,

01:32:05.480 --> 01:32:06.966
I'm seeing more and more people say,

01:32:06.966 --> 01:32:08.960
do we really need college?

01:32:08.960 --> 01:32:12.400
Like, is college really the right thing for this kid?

01:32:12.400 --> 01:32:16.020
Maybe they can find, you know, their sense of self

01:32:16.020 --> 01:32:17.946
in an other direction, and there's a permissiveness

01:32:17.946 --> 01:32:20.390
to that that I think didn't exist

01:32:20.390 --> 01:32:22.050
when we were graduating from high school.

01:32:22.050 --> 01:32:23.320
I think that's absolutely right.

01:32:23.320 --> 01:32:25.960
I think it's both because college has gotten more expensive

01:32:25.960 --> 01:32:28.990
and out of reach and because more and more people

01:32:28.990 --> 01:32:30.550
are willing to say, you know what,

01:32:30.550 --> 01:32:32.680
I'm actually good at this thing.

01:32:32.680 --> 01:32:34.623
I love working with my hands and this is what I make

01:32:34.623 --> 01:32:36.049
and I'm good at it, and I wanna get better at it.

01:32:36.049 --> 01:32:38.508
And the wages are good and the benefits are good.

01:32:38.508 --> 01:32:41.340
You know, maybe there's even, you know,

01:32:41.340 --> 01:32:45.000
that sort of old fashioned employer, employee handshake,

01:32:45.000 --> 01:32:47.770
where it's like you work for me and I'll take care of you.

01:32:47.770 --> 01:32:51.140
And that's pretty enticing thing these days.

01:32:51.140 --> 01:32:55.010
Yeah, and I think the internet exposes young people

01:32:55.010 --> 01:32:58.730
to a broader diversity of career paths than, you know,

01:32:58.730 --> 01:33:02.560
the brochures that were on the coffee table at Stanford

01:33:02.560 --> 01:33:03.671
when we were there.

01:33:03.671 --> 01:33:06.540
And I think that allows people at a younger age

01:33:06.540 --> 01:33:11.540
to have a greater depth of imagination

01:33:11.700 --> 01:33:13.240
for what their life could look like.

01:33:13.240 --> 01:33:18.240
And yet, I feel like education is still stuck

01:33:18.776 --> 01:33:23.776
in a certain kind of system or rubric

01:33:23.828 --> 01:33:26.360
that in many ways is outdated

01:33:26.360 --> 01:33:29.570
given all the technological advances that we have.

01:33:29.570 --> 01:33:32.900
And that we're gonna live to 100, right?

01:33:32.900 --> 01:33:35.780
So many of your kids and mine as a routine matter,

01:33:35.780 --> 01:33:36.613
live to 100.

01:33:36.613 --> 01:33:38.453
So the notion that education is finite

01:33:38.453 --> 01:33:40.190
or education is only for the young,

01:33:40.190 --> 01:33:41.380
is just really outdated.

01:33:41.380 --> 01:33:43.899
So yeah, I'm excited to see the ways,

01:33:43.899 --> 01:33:47.996
well I'm optimistic and hopeful that the pandemic

01:33:47.996 --> 01:33:52.996
has so jostled the way in which we try to educate folks

01:33:54.457 --> 01:33:56.965
that the cleverest and most creative

01:33:56.965 --> 01:34:00.820
and forward-thinking among us will seize this moment

01:34:00.820 --> 01:34:03.970
to reimagine K-12 and higher ed

01:34:03.970 --> 01:34:05.700
and the concept of education.

01:34:05.700 --> 01:34:08.390
But of course we see many who are going right back

01:34:08.390 --> 01:34:11.740
into the Rosen Sage on the stage and, you know,

01:34:11.740 --> 01:34:13.930
content up at the front and it all has to, right.

01:34:13.930 --> 01:34:16.080
I mean, there are plenty of people are just dying

01:34:16.080 --> 01:34:18.640
to go right back to what it was, not learning the lessons.

01:34:18.640 --> 01:34:23.640
To institutions that have absurd amounts of wealth,

01:34:24.400 --> 01:34:29.050
you know, these funds and the amount of real estate

01:34:29.050 --> 01:34:31.708
that, you know, Stanford sits on, for example.

01:34:31.708 --> 01:34:36.708
You know, I feel like the university fundamentally

01:34:37.707 --> 01:34:39.505
should be a public servant

01:34:39.505 --> 01:34:42.250
and shouldn't be about restriction,

01:34:42.250 --> 01:34:44.970
but should be about expansion.

01:34:44.970 --> 01:34:49.800
And why, especially after going through 18 months

01:34:49.800 --> 01:34:53.215
of the pandemic, couldn't an institution like Stanford

01:34:53.215 --> 01:34:58.215
pivot to create a state-of-the-art digital experience

01:34:58.460 --> 01:35:02.510
that would be widely available to a much greater number

01:35:02.510 --> 01:35:05.920
of young people at a much more affordable rate.

01:35:05.920 --> 01:35:08.410
They're not gonna be able to go live in Brandner,

01:35:08.410 --> 01:35:11.035
but maybe from the comfort of their own home,

01:35:11.035 --> 01:35:14.439
they could get some version of the Stanford experience.

01:35:14.439 --> 01:35:19.200
That would be a great service to humanity.

01:35:19.200 --> 01:35:22.990
If you took like all these collegiate institutions

01:35:22.990 --> 01:35:27.990
and reformulated them so that they could create

01:35:28.170 --> 01:35:31.950
greater accessibility, there has to be some deep thinking

01:35:31.950 --> 01:35:34.632
around this at these institutions.

01:35:34.632 --> 01:35:35.780
Or inside of them.

01:35:35.780 --> 01:35:38.270
Or is their brand so rooted-

01:35:38.270 --> 01:35:39.310
In the inclusivity.

01:35:39.310 --> 01:35:41.940
In the exclusivity, like that's their bread and butter,

01:35:41.940 --> 01:35:43.530
so I'm not gonna let go of that.

01:35:43.530 --> 01:35:45.290
And certainly Malcolm Gladwell has just,

01:35:45.290 --> 01:35:46.914
in the last couple of weeks written about that.

01:35:46.914 --> 01:35:49.570
I mean, this is his thing which is going back.

01:35:49.570 --> 01:35:50.403
I know.

01:35:50.403 --> 01:35:55.020
Stanford is in his sights and he goes after it

01:35:55.020 --> 01:35:55.853
all the time.

01:35:55.853 --> 01:35:56.686
Yeah, yeah.

01:35:57.920 --> 01:35:59.840
He's just written a beautiful piece on the absurdity

01:35:59.840 --> 01:36:02.430
of the US news rankings, which I-

01:36:02.430 --> 01:36:04.530
Well he's doing something I think

01:36:04.530 --> 01:36:05.810
I think it's coming up in an upcoming episode

01:36:05.810 --> 01:36:07.870
where he tries to game the system.

01:36:07.870 --> 01:36:10.907
He goes to a smaller college and tries to figure out

01:36:10.907 --> 01:36:13.450
what they need to do in order to get higher up

01:36:13.450 --> 01:36:15.710
in the rankings to sort of play a game with it.

01:36:15.710 --> 01:36:16.590
Right.

01:36:16.590 --> 01:36:17.423
'Cause it's bullshit.

01:36:17.423 --> 01:36:18.430
Yeah, is bullshit.

01:36:18.430 --> 01:36:20.840
And that's a big thing of what you talk about,

01:36:20.840 --> 01:36:25.570
which is getting parents comfortable with the idea

01:36:25.570 --> 01:36:27.160
that it's not just bullshit

01:36:27.160 --> 01:36:29.760
but there are all kinds of amazing institutions out there.

01:36:29.760 --> 01:36:32.590
Just like let go of this idea that if your kid

01:36:32.590 --> 01:36:34.023
doesn't get into the one of these colleges

01:36:34.023 --> 01:36:36.530
that not only did he or she failed

01:36:36.530 --> 01:36:37.600
that you failed as well.

01:36:37.600 --> 01:36:40.060
Right, so it's not where you go, it's who you are

01:36:40.060 --> 01:36:43.004
and what you're capable of when you show up wherever you go.

01:36:43.004 --> 01:36:47.180
I am a huge fan of a small liberal arts college.

01:36:47.180 --> 01:36:48.690
And I'll tell you why.

01:36:48.690 --> 01:36:50.280
When I was an administrator,

01:36:50.280 --> 01:36:53.050
a high-level administrator and Dean at Stanford,

01:36:53.050 --> 01:36:56.840
I heard Stanford faculty saying,

01:36:56.840 --> 01:37:01.220
wouldn't it be great if we were like Reed College,

01:37:01.220 --> 01:37:03.340
which is a small liberal arts college in Portland, Oregon,

01:37:03.340 --> 01:37:05.180
and what the context was,

01:37:05.180 --> 01:37:07.100
and Stanford of course, is an amazing place

01:37:07.100 --> 01:37:10.640
and excels at what it does and is,

01:37:10.640 --> 01:37:12.250
but it is not a small liberal arts college.

01:37:12.250 --> 01:37:13.944
And the faculty member was basically saying

01:37:13.944 --> 01:37:17.180
the faculty a Reed only have to care

01:37:17.180 --> 01:37:19.100
about mentoring and teaching undergraduates,

01:37:19.100 --> 01:37:21.660
'cause there isn't, there aren't a set of graduate students.

01:37:21.660 --> 01:37:23.119
There isn't a research imperative,

01:37:23.119 --> 01:37:27.950
unlike a world-class university where the imperative

01:37:27.950 --> 01:37:30.840
is to create new knowledge and disseminate it.

01:37:30.840 --> 01:37:32.120
At a smaller liberal arts college

01:37:32.120 --> 01:37:34.761
it's all about teaching and mentoring the undergrads.

01:37:34.761 --> 01:37:37.853
And places like Stanford are trying their best

01:37:37.853 --> 01:37:39.250
to incentivize faculty

01:37:39.250 --> 01:37:41.634
to also care about the undergraduates.

01:37:41.634 --> 01:37:44.650
And I think Stanford among its peers,

01:37:44.650 --> 01:37:45.950
does a pretty darn good job of that.

01:37:45.950 --> 01:37:48.400
But I'm here to say,

01:37:48.400 --> 01:37:51.640
if you want a truly amazing undergraduate education,

01:37:51.640 --> 01:37:54.520
be sure you're going to a place where the faculty

01:37:54.520 --> 01:37:57.810
can make time to meet with you and to know you

01:37:57.810 --> 01:38:01.420
and take an interest in your interests and your fears

01:38:01.420 --> 01:38:02.410
and your dreams.

01:38:02.410 --> 01:38:05.300
And that, that happens at small liberal arts colleges.

01:38:05.300 --> 01:38:08.160
It also happens at community colleges.

01:38:08.160 --> 01:38:10.660
And so those are two examples of categories

01:38:10.660 --> 01:38:14.139
that folks should be really investigating

01:38:14.139 --> 01:38:16.150
in addition to whatever other schools

01:38:16.150 --> 01:38:17.950
are sort of commonly talked about in your community.

01:38:17.950 --> 01:38:19.410
Yeah, yeah.

01:38:19.410 --> 01:38:23.720
So if you're a young person listening right now,

01:38:23.720 --> 01:38:26.940
and maybe you've got a job that you're gonna be starting

01:38:26.940 --> 01:38:29.730
in the fall, or maybe you don't have a job

01:38:29.730 --> 01:38:32.498
and you're really just not sure what the next step is,

01:38:32.498 --> 01:38:36.380
what are the kinds of things that you say to that person

01:38:36.380 --> 01:38:39.630
to help get them thinking about who they are,

01:38:39.630 --> 01:38:41.170
what they might want to express

01:38:41.170 --> 01:38:43.958
and kind of moving forward on a healthy trajectory?

01:38:43.958 --> 01:38:48.050
I think the first thing I would say is start anywhere.

01:38:48.050 --> 01:38:49.880
This comes from the field of improv.

01:38:49.880 --> 01:38:52.890
This comes from Silicon Valley design thinking,

01:38:52.890 --> 01:38:54.250
bias toward action start.

01:38:54.250 --> 01:38:58.650
Any job is gonna give you some important pieces of data,

01:38:58.650 --> 01:39:01.910
data about that industry and way of being in that industry

01:39:01.910 --> 01:39:03.780
and data about yourself.

01:39:03.780 --> 01:39:06.262
And your job is to try to be as learned

01:39:06.262 --> 01:39:09.610
and effort-making as possible in that environment,

01:39:09.610 --> 01:39:12.100
learn what you can about the place and yourself,

01:39:12.100 --> 01:39:15.490
and then examine those data and say, well, how am I doing?

01:39:15.490 --> 01:39:16.323
Do I like this, why?

01:39:16.323 --> 01:39:18.143
How am I showing up here?

01:39:18.143 --> 01:39:19.676
What does it tell me about myself?

01:39:19.676 --> 01:39:21.680
What does it tell me I might wannna do differently?

01:39:21.680 --> 01:39:24.480
Look, my first job was as a corn detasseler in Wisconsin

01:39:25.420 --> 01:39:27.440
working for minimum wage, I know.

01:39:27.440 --> 01:39:28.550
Corn detass, what?

01:39:28.550 --> 01:39:31.180
Yeah, you have to take the tassels off the tops

01:39:31.180 --> 01:39:34.078
of the corn stock so that something about cross pollination.

01:39:34.078 --> 01:39:37.360
And that was my summer job after our freshman year

01:39:37.360 --> 01:39:38.304
at Stanford.

01:39:38.304 --> 01:39:40.340
And I lasted for two days,

01:39:40.340 --> 01:39:43.360
'cause I was walking in the hot sun and miserable

01:39:43.360 --> 01:39:45.834
and it was a minimum wage job.

01:39:45.834 --> 01:39:49.042
So I quit and I got another minimum wage job

01:39:49.042 --> 01:39:52.460
as a bus girl at Perkins, which was a 24-hour restaurant

01:39:52.460 --> 01:39:54.358
where I got to interact with people.

01:39:54.358 --> 01:39:57.320
And that was one of the earliest clues to me

01:39:57.320 --> 01:39:58.370
that I'm a people person.

01:39:58.370 --> 01:40:01.830
If the job can be really arduous or unpleasant,

01:40:01.830 --> 01:40:03.800
but if there are people with whom I can interact,

01:40:03.800 --> 01:40:04.950
I'll be fine.

01:40:04.950 --> 01:40:06.540
That was an early clue to me.

01:40:06.540 --> 01:40:09.150
So anyway, so to someone who doesn't know,

01:40:09.150 --> 01:40:11.410
I'd say start anywhere and pay attention

01:40:11.410 --> 01:40:13.675
to what you like and what you don't then iterate.

01:40:13.675 --> 01:40:16.090
To people who are like, I could do this, I could do that,

01:40:16.090 --> 01:40:17.680
I could do this, I got to keep all my options open,

01:40:17.680 --> 01:40:20.085
I would say, keep your options open

01:40:20.085 --> 01:40:23.895
is a blunt form of criticism or criticism in disguise,

01:40:23.895 --> 01:40:25.501
usually from your parents are like,

01:40:25.501 --> 01:40:27.980
oh, and when you say, I wanna do this this summer,

01:40:27.980 --> 01:40:29.480
like, oh, keep your options open.

01:40:29.480 --> 01:40:31.100
That's our way of saying, I don't like that option,

01:40:31.100 --> 01:40:32.260
choose a different one.

01:40:32.260 --> 01:40:34.979
Right, or if you fail, bad things are gonna happen.

01:40:34.979 --> 01:40:36.734
It's sort of catastrophe, isn't it?

01:40:36.734 --> 01:40:41.142
Yeah, so I would say your 20s

01:40:41.142 --> 01:40:43.110
as at the design school at Stanford,

01:40:43.110 --> 01:40:44.560
they call them your odyssey years.

01:40:44.560 --> 01:40:46.530
Bill Burnett and Dave Evans have written about this

01:40:46.530 --> 01:40:47.688
in their book and in the class they teach.

01:40:47.688 --> 01:40:49.471
These are your odyssey years.

01:40:49.471 --> 01:40:50.988
And that's, let that not be

01:40:50.988 --> 01:40:52.394
an entirely privileged statement.

01:40:52.394 --> 01:40:55.022
These are the years when you can explore,

01:40:55.022 --> 01:40:57.150
what is my mind interested in?

01:40:57.150 --> 01:40:58.230
What does my body like to do?

01:40:58.230 --> 01:41:00.100
What are the environments in which I thrive?

01:41:00.100 --> 01:41:02.200
What are the ways in which I like to learn and contribute?

01:41:02.200 --> 01:41:03.266
You're gathering data about yourself.

01:41:03.266 --> 01:41:06.453
Long gone are the days of you will do one thing.

01:41:06.453 --> 01:41:09.510
You know, your grandfather, your great-grandfather

01:41:09.510 --> 01:41:10.960
lived that life.

01:41:10.960 --> 01:41:13.070
Your life is gonna have many more facets to it.

01:41:13.070 --> 01:41:13.903
Yeah, no longer.

01:41:13.903 --> 01:41:18.150
And it is a situation in which there's tension

01:41:18.150 --> 01:41:22.170
between sticking it out in a job that's not great

01:41:22.170 --> 01:41:25.260
to kind of develop a little bit of grit and resilience

01:41:25.260 --> 01:41:26.940
around just weathering stuff

01:41:26.940 --> 01:41:28.456
that just kinda sucks to do

01:41:28.456 --> 01:41:32.120
versus, you know, I have a history of staying in jobs

01:41:32.120 --> 01:41:35.250
too long because I thought, well, I'll just figure it out.

01:41:35.250 --> 01:41:37.720
Like I just need to like, you know, suckle down

01:41:37.720 --> 01:41:39.210
and suck it up until, you know,

01:41:39.210 --> 01:41:42.570
I have a full-blown existential crisis.

01:41:42.570 --> 01:41:45.703
But on the other hand, you know,

01:41:45.703 --> 01:41:49.590
just quitting the moment it gets hard, isn't good either.

01:41:49.590 --> 01:41:52.580
So there's a sweet spot in which you've got to develop

01:41:52.580 --> 01:41:54.400
those kinds of whatever the skills are

01:41:54.400 --> 01:41:55.741
that you're trying to learn in that job

01:41:55.741 --> 01:41:58.540
while also having a healthy recognition of like,

01:41:58.540 --> 01:42:00.220
this isn't gonna be my lifelong thing.

01:42:00.220 --> 01:42:02.730
At some point, I'll pull the rip cord and try something new.

01:42:02.730 --> 01:42:05.070
Right, so I would say don't quit in a day.

01:42:05.070 --> 01:42:06.950
When something bad happens, don't just refuse

01:42:06.950 --> 01:42:07.850
to come in the next day.

01:42:07.850 --> 01:42:10.515
We do see that a lot more these days than in the past.

01:42:10.515 --> 01:42:12.510
I would say go find your-

01:42:12.510 --> 01:42:13.910
Probably a lot more.

01:42:13.910 --> 01:42:15.030
A lot more people-
These days.

01:42:15.030 --> 01:42:15.863
Yeah, absolutely.

01:42:15.863 --> 01:42:17.960
I mean, that's good, I don't know.

01:42:17.960 --> 01:42:18.940
Well, I think if you're-

01:42:18.940 --> 01:42:20.067
The Boomer in me is like-

01:42:20.067 --> 01:42:22.450
You're not a Boomer, you're Gen X.

01:42:22.450 --> 01:42:23.420
We need all the Gen Xes-

01:42:23.420 --> 01:42:25.882
Yeah, but a Gen X, anybody who's not Gen X

01:42:25.882 --> 01:42:27.400
is okay, Boomer.

01:42:27.400 --> 01:42:28.631
I know, that's right, that's right.

01:42:28.631 --> 01:42:30.330
I mean, I think here's the deal.

01:42:31.360 --> 01:42:34.164
If your boss is tyrannical, you're never gonna fix that.

01:42:34.164 --> 01:42:36.970
I would leave a situation with a truly tyrannical,

01:42:36.970 --> 01:42:38.035
awful toxic boss.

01:42:38.035 --> 01:42:41.730
The question is if they raise their voice at you,

01:42:41.730 --> 01:42:44.330
are you considering that tyranny or toxicity?

01:42:44.330 --> 01:42:45.725
You might need to develop a thicker skin.

01:42:45.725 --> 01:42:50.150
And this is where talking to peers and talking to mentors

01:42:50.150 --> 01:42:51.370
in the workplace or older workers,

01:42:51.370 --> 01:42:53.410
who've been there longer, who know the environment

01:42:53.410 --> 01:42:56.664
can give you a bit of a sense of reality check, you know.

01:42:56.664 --> 01:42:59.573
Is this, how often am I likely to experience this?

01:42:59.573 --> 01:43:00.970
How would you recommend I handle this?

01:43:00.970 --> 01:43:03.743
You wanna try to stay at a job for two years.

01:43:03.743 --> 01:43:07.210
You certainly don't wanna repeat, repeat, repeat, leaving

01:43:07.210 --> 01:43:09.440
sooner than two years, more than a couple of times

01:43:09.440 --> 01:43:10.280
in a row on your resume,

01:43:10.280 --> 01:43:12.068
'cause it looks like you don't have staying power

01:43:12.068 --> 01:43:13.431
or resilience or grit.

01:43:13.431 --> 01:43:15.540
Yeah, yeah.

01:43:17.482 --> 01:43:21.110
On the idea of keeping our options open.

01:43:21.110 --> 01:43:25.730
The one thing I would say is, you know,

01:43:25.730 --> 01:43:28.590
people ask me like, what would you tell yourself at 20?

01:43:28.590 --> 01:43:30.100
Or what advice do you have for young people?

01:43:30.100 --> 01:43:35.100
And I always say to invest in experience and live leanly,

01:43:35.850 --> 01:43:37.550
like I think there's this, you know,

01:43:37.550 --> 01:43:40.080
social pressure to accumulate like,

01:43:40.080 --> 01:43:41.740
oh, I have to live in this kind of apartment

01:43:41.740 --> 01:43:42.976
or lease this kind of car.

01:43:42.976 --> 01:43:46.860
And, you know, my heart always breaks

01:43:46.860 --> 01:43:48.890
when I see young people like over leveraged

01:43:48.890 --> 01:43:50.717
because that does short circuit

01:43:50.717 --> 01:43:52.920
the choices that they can make,

01:43:52.920 --> 01:43:54.510
if they do wanna pull the rip cord

01:43:54.510 --> 01:43:56.960
on a certain career path, because it's not working out.

01:43:56.960 --> 01:43:57.793
That's good advice.

01:43:57.793 --> 01:43:59.100
Like to have, you know, to always have

01:43:59.100 --> 01:44:00.660
like a bit of a trap door

01:44:00.660 --> 01:44:03.120
or just the flexibility financially,

01:44:03.120 --> 01:44:05.608
to be able to segue into something else.

01:44:05.608 --> 01:44:08.810
You know, I think the, I agree with all of that.

01:44:08.810 --> 01:44:12.110
And the one piece of advice, I think the universe

01:44:12.110 --> 01:44:15.029
tried to give me twice in my young 20s

01:44:15.029 --> 01:44:18.093
was on the value of compound interest.

01:44:18.093 --> 01:44:22.210
So I had a small windfall twice that I spent

01:44:22.210 --> 01:44:24.520
and I can now look back and do the math

01:44:24.520 --> 01:44:25.900
and see how much money I would have now

01:44:25.900 --> 01:44:27.280
had I invested that money.

01:44:28.930 --> 01:44:30.837
And so that's a message I try to make clear in the book.

01:44:30.837 --> 01:44:34.739
The universe, just sort of parachuted in a little,

01:44:34.739 --> 01:44:36.750
you know, cash and said, look,

01:44:36.750 --> 01:44:39.460
if you wanna out, like, here's your little where you could-

01:44:39.460 --> 01:44:42.348
Well, you know, they were things that at the time seemed,

01:44:42.348 --> 01:44:44.940
at the time I felt I was making the right choice.

01:44:44.940 --> 01:44:49.560
My father said to me, I'd rather give you this money.

01:44:49.560 --> 01:44:52.120
He was spending $25,000 on my wedding,

01:44:52.120 --> 01:44:53.447
which I realize is a privilege for many,

01:44:53.447 --> 01:44:56.420
and, you know, nothing compared

01:44:56.420 --> 01:44:57.770
to what many people spend today.

01:44:57.770 --> 01:44:59.311
But he said, I'd rather give you this money

01:44:59.311 --> 01:45:02.600
than spend it on a party that's gonna last for five hours.

01:45:02.600 --> 01:45:04.060
And all I could say was, oh, daddy,

01:45:04.060 --> 01:45:05.102
how could I not have a wedding?

01:45:05.102 --> 01:45:07.893
But I'm able to see the value of that 25 grand

01:45:07.893 --> 01:45:09.616
being 200 grand tonight.

01:45:09.616 --> 01:45:14.480
We had a fire in our moving truck on the way to California

01:45:14.480 --> 01:45:15.838
after I graduated law school.

01:45:15.838 --> 01:45:20.838
And we had a settlement from beacons for about $25,000.

01:45:21.270 --> 01:45:23.650
And we spent it on new furniture

01:45:23.650 --> 01:45:25.250
and maybe even upgrading our furniture.

01:45:25.250 --> 01:45:27.180
We're newlyweds, how could we not?

01:45:27.180 --> 01:45:30.440
But I wish we had lived a little bit more like grad students

01:45:30.440 --> 01:45:31.934
for a few more years and pocketed that cash.

01:45:31.934 --> 01:45:33.419
Right, right.

01:45:33.419 --> 01:45:35.650
But you're like, hey, I'm gonna go be

01:45:35.650 --> 01:45:36.968
in this corporate law firm.

01:45:36.968 --> 01:45:39.051
In Palo Alto.

01:45:39.051 --> 01:45:43.467
I need a couch fitting my arise, yeah.

01:45:43.467 --> 01:45:44.990
I know.
Yeah.

01:45:44.990 --> 01:45:47.740
Yeah, it's tough to gauge those things

01:45:47.740 --> 01:45:48.710
in the moment, isn't it?

01:45:48.710 --> 01:45:49.543
Absolutely.

01:45:50.660 --> 01:45:54.640
What are some of the things that in your experience

01:45:54.640 --> 01:45:56.354
of talking to all of these young people

01:45:56.354 --> 01:45:59.340
and the challenges that they have,

01:45:59.340 --> 01:46:01.888
or the obstacles that they're facing,

01:46:01.888 --> 01:46:04.870
what are some of the other kind of themes

01:46:04.870 --> 01:46:08.244
that emerged from that, that are common,

01:46:08.244 --> 01:46:10.890
but perhaps unique to this generation?

01:46:12.640 --> 01:46:14.720
Well, interesting question.

01:46:14.720 --> 01:46:15.860
I don't know the extent

01:46:15.860 --> 01:46:17.420
to which they're unique to this generation.

01:46:17.420 --> 01:46:18.597
I'll have to think on that.

01:46:18.597 --> 01:46:21.295
What came to mind was we've been talking about work,

01:46:21.295 --> 01:46:25.288
but discovering the identities one carries

01:46:25.288 --> 01:46:29.580
is a huge task in young adulthood.

01:46:29.580 --> 01:46:30.920
Hopefully it happens in young adulthood

01:46:30.920 --> 01:46:33.450
as opposed to you don't figure it out until you're 40.

01:46:35.370 --> 01:46:39.090
And oftentimes people are asking me when I do assemblies

01:46:39.090 --> 01:46:42.450
for high school seniors, or talk to people slightly older,

01:46:42.450 --> 01:46:44.550
they'll say, how do I talk to my parents about this?

01:46:44.550 --> 01:46:47.904
You know, whether it's around work or identity.

01:46:47.904 --> 01:46:50.530
And I have sort of five-step thing,

01:46:50.530 --> 01:46:52.910
which is, steps one through four just prepare the way

01:46:52.910 --> 01:46:54.414
for you to be successful at it.

01:46:54.414 --> 01:46:56.530
Step one, is you say to your parents,

01:46:56.530 --> 01:47:00.030
hey parents, can we find some time to talk later this week

01:47:00.030 --> 01:47:00.863
or this weekend?

01:47:00.863 --> 01:47:02.197
Smile on your face, respectful tone of voice.

01:47:02.197 --> 01:47:04.240
You're signaling it's serious.

01:47:04.240 --> 01:47:05.350
You're signaling I'm mature.

01:47:05.350 --> 01:47:06.940
They're gonna be a little bit back on their heels,

01:47:06.940 --> 01:47:07.870
that's fine.

01:47:07.870 --> 01:47:09.641
You want them a little bit like, whoa, okay.

01:47:09.641 --> 01:47:13.090
(extreme crosstalk)

01:47:13.090 --> 01:47:15.401
Step two, you're in meeting

01:47:15.401 --> 01:47:17.820
and you open with gratitude and praise

01:47:17.820 --> 01:47:19.390
with whatever language works in your family,

01:47:19.390 --> 01:47:20.630
whether it's love or respect.

01:47:20.630 --> 01:47:23.007
You say, I know you love me and I love you,

01:47:23.007 --> 01:47:25.870
which will further freak them out, right.

01:47:25.870 --> 01:47:27.600
Or you say, thank you so much for raising me

01:47:27.600 --> 01:47:30.601
with these values, and you've taught me, you know,

01:47:30.601 --> 01:47:33.690
you've taught me so much, and I'm grateful.

01:47:33.690 --> 01:47:36.480
You offer respect in whatever language

01:47:36.480 --> 01:47:37.313
works in your family.

01:47:37.313 --> 01:47:38.310
Now they'll be more freaked out.

01:47:38.310 --> 01:47:41.054
Step three, you say, I'd like to talk with you about X.

01:47:41.054 --> 01:47:43.770
I'd like to talk to about my plans for grad school.

01:47:43.770 --> 01:47:44.970
I'd like to talk with you about my plans

01:47:44.970 --> 01:47:47.380
for after high school, or I'd like to talk to you

01:47:47.380 --> 01:47:48.480
about my relationship.

01:47:50.130 --> 01:47:53.250
And I'd like to hear your plans for me.

01:47:53.250 --> 01:47:56.650
And then I'd like to share mine, is that okay?

01:47:56.650 --> 01:47:57.737
And they'll be like, yeah, okay.

01:47:57.737 --> 01:48:00.290
It's like NVC, nonviolent communication.

01:48:00.290 --> 01:48:01.250
Right, exactly.

01:48:01.250 --> 01:48:02.930
And so step four, the parent talks,

01:48:02.930 --> 01:48:04.440
I said, well, we've always known you're so smart.

01:48:04.440 --> 01:48:05.930
We have always known you'd be this or that.

01:48:05.930 --> 01:48:07.530
We just want, we want what's best for you,

01:48:07.530 --> 01:48:09.580
But you know, we really think that (mumbles) whatever it is,

01:48:09.580 --> 01:48:12.031
whatever barrier you're facing will likely come out

01:48:12.031 --> 01:48:14.780
and you actively listen and reflect back what you've heard

01:48:14.780 --> 01:48:16.400
so they feel heard and you say, did I get that right?

01:48:16.400 --> 01:48:17.970
And you really don't be defensive.

01:48:17.970 --> 01:48:19.270
Don't be mad, whatever they say.

01:48:19.270 --> 01:48:22.735
Just try to listen dispassionately and interestedly,

01:48:22.735 --> 01:48:24.290
and then finally, step five,

01:48:24.290 --> 01:48:25.350
you can say, you know,

01:48:25.350 --> 01:48:26.860
I'd like to share with you my thoughts now,

01:48:26.860 --> 01:48:27.894
is that all right?

01:48:27.894 --> 01:48:30.230
And steps one through four will have made it

01:48:30.230 --> 01:48:33.540
so much more likely that their ears and hearts

01:48:33.540 --> 01:48:36.700
and spirit will be open when you dare to say,

01:48:36.700 --> 01:48:37.533
you know what?

01:48:37.533 --> 01:48:42.190
The person I'm serious about is X.

01:48:42.190 --> 01:48:44.810
And I realize you may not like, or appreciate

01:48:44.810 --> 01:48:48.055
or understand or accept people like them,

01:48:48.055 --> 01:48:49.860
but this is what's right for me.

01:48:49.860 --> 01:48:53.270
And even if you can't understand or it wouldn't be

01:48:53.270 --> 01:48:54.607
what you would have done,

01:48:54.607 --> 01:48:56.100
it's what I need to do.

01:48:56.100 --> 01:48:58.638
And I hope you will love me anyway.

01:48:58.638 --> 01:49:00.942
You know, this is where it gets big and like, right.

01:49:00.942 --> 01:49:03.400
How many parents and adult children

01:49:03.400 --> 01:49:04.600
don't speak to each other anymore

01:49:04.600 --> 01:49:07.290
because those conversations never happened

01:49:07.290 --> 01:49:11.840
or really went awry because the parent insisted,

01:49:11.840 --> 01:49:13.050
no, no, no, I know what's best for you.

01:49:13.050 --> 01:49:15.650
And you shall not, whether it's about work

01:49:15.650 --> 01:49:16.760
or about relationships.

01:49:16.760 --> 01:49:17.861
Right, right, right.

01:49:17.861 --> 01:49:20.670
It's almost like a coming out.

01:49:20.670 --> 01:49:21.640
Yeah, it is.

01:49:21.640 --> 01:49:22.550
It's similar to that.

01:49:22.550 --> 01:49:24.470
It is, even if it's, I wanna be an AMT

01:49:24.470 --> 01:49:26.900
instead of a doctor, you know,

01:49:26.900 --> 01:49:28.680
or I wanna be a wilderness naturalist

01:49:28.680 --> 01:49:30.172
and I realize we're a family of lawyers.

01:49:30.172 --> 01:49:32.127
Or I wanna go to Wall Street,

01:49:32.127 --> 01:49:34.608
even though y'all are theater geeks.

01:49:34.608 --> 01:49:36.860
Like I'm agnostic, I don't have anything

01:49:36.860 --> 01:49:37.740
against Wall Street.

01:49:37.740 --> 01:49:39.180
If a kid wants to go to Wall Street

01:49:39.180 --> 01:49:40.225
and they're from a family of artists,

01:49:40.225 --> 01:49:42.101
they're struggling in the same way,

01:49:42.101 --> 01:49:44.450
but they have more societal validation.

01:49:44.450 --> 01:49:46.810
It's a rig that way, you know.

01:49:46.810 --> 01:49:48.800
The artist's parents who raise their kids

01:49:48.800 --> 01:49:50.160
in the artistic environment,

01:49:50.160 --> 01:49:54.087
those kids end up rebelling by going full finance.

01:49:54.087 --> 01:49:57.880
Like Alex speaking, family ties back in our day.

01:49:57.880 --> 01:50:00.983
Yeah, and that's how our kids become our teachers.

01:50:00.983 --> 01:50:02.520
I think in many ways.

01:50:03.407 --> 01:50:06.360
My daughter is a dual major right now

01:50:06.360 --> 01:50:09.747
in cultural anthropology and dance.

01:50:09.747 --> 01:50:12.248
And there were years when I could not accept

01:50:12.248 --> 01:50:17.248
or be proud of the fact that my baby girl is an artist.

01:50:17.710 --> 01:50:19.826
And working with other people's kids,

01:50:19.826 --> 01:50:23.096
denied the opportunity to pursue their dreams

01:50:23.096 --> 01:50:28.096
woke me up to the reality that my child is an artist.

01:50:28.520 --> 01:50:31.990
And I do not want her at 20 to be in a college environment

01:50:31.990 --> 01:50:34.650
in tears trying to explain to an adviser

01:50:34.650 --> 01:50:37.210
why her parents are making her be X, Y, and Z,

01:50:37.210 --> 01:50:39.440
instead of letting her be who she is.

01:50:39.440 --> 01:50:41.669
I appreciate that honesty, and vulnerability,

01:50:41.669 --> 01:50:46.669
because as somebody who's this authority on parenting

01:50:46.930 --> 01:50:50.090
and who has observed so much in the space

01:50:50.090 --> 01:50:53.970
of how parents have improperly raised kids

01:50:53.970 --> 01:50:58.490
or set them up for failure when they were endeavoring

01:50:58.490 --> 01:51:01.195
to set them up for success, to acknowledge that,

01:51:01.195 --> 01:51:04.910
you know, we all have our blind spots with this stuff.

01:51:04.910 --> 01:51:06.270
You know, I think is important.

01:51:06.270 --> 01:51:10.140
Yeah, I mean, I think when people hear me tell my stories,

01:51:10.140 --> 01:51:10.991
they don't feel judged.

01:51:10.991 --> 01:51:14.310
I hope they feel seen, and we can laugh and giggle

01:51:14.310 --> 01:51:15.770
a little bit about why we over-parent,

01:51:15.770 --> 01:51:17.714
and why our egos are to invest and we're too enmeshed.

01:51:17.714 --> 01:51:20.410
But then I can tell a little bit more

01:51:20.410 --> 01:51:21.945
of the outcome side of it

01:51:21.945 --> 01:51:24.645
and what I've learned the hard way.

01:51:24.645 --> 01:51:26.621
And I hope that it'll help other people

01:51:26.621 --> 01:51:28.190
course correct a little sooner.

01:51:29.112 --> 01:51:32.470
Yeah, I think I've gone on a journey with this as well,

01:51:32.470 --> 01:51:34.300
to the extent that, you know,

01:51:34.300 --> 01:51:37.134
you and I are both products of a certain type of education

01:51:37.134 --> 01:51:42.134
and a way of orienting your life around that ladder, right.

01:51:46.200 --> 01:51:47.924
Like you do this, you do this, you do this,

01:51:47.924 --> 01:51:50.065
it worked for a while for me,

01:51:50.065 --> 01:51:53.144
we each got out in our own respective ways,

01:51:53.144 --> 01:51:56.040
and even though I'm aware of the pitfalls

01:51:56.040 --> 01:51:59.503
or where, you know, where and how it kind of led me astray,

01:51:59.503 --> 01:52:01.490
that's my imprinting.

01:52:01.490 --> 01:52:02.680
So when I'm raising kids,

01:52:02.680 --> 01:52:05.050
I'm like, well, this is the way it's gonna be.

01:52:05.050 --> 01:52:07.750
And now my kids were like, well, not so fast, you know.

01:52:07.750 --> 01:52:09.220
And I'm like, what do you mean, you know?

01:52:09.220 --> 01:52:12.760
And then I have to grapple

01:52:12.760 --> 01:52:15.493
with my attachment to that, right,

01:52:15.493 --> 01:52:18.840
in the many ways that you talk about in your books.

01:52:18.840 --> 01:52:21.950
Like, where am I projecting onto my kid

01:52:21.950 --> 01:52:24.830
a certain expectation or an assumption

01:52:24.830 --> 01:52:27.315
about the way that they should pursue

01:52:27.315 --> 01:52:29.269
their young adulthood

01:52:29.269 --> 01:52:34.269
and how much of that is in a meshed relationship

01:52:34.580 --> 01:52:39.530
where in my ego is associated with certain outcomes

01:52:39.530 --> 01:52:40.980
that have nothing to do with me.

01:52:40.980 --> 01:52:45.980
Yeah, ego is certainly possibly piece of it for you,

01:52:46.390 --> 01:52:47.672
for me, for anybody listening.

01:52:47.672 --> 01:52:52.570
It's also just hardcore love and fear.

01:52:52.570 --> 01:52:55.130
We know more about the world and how it works.

01:52:55.130 --> 01:52:57.500
We want them to be successful, healthy,

01:52:57.500 --> 01:53:01.700
safe, happy, and we think we know the path to those things.

01:53:01.700 --> 01:53:04.880
So why would we not curate their environment?

01:53:04.880 --> 01:53:09.880
And yet, and yet we say, I just want my kids to be happy.

01:53:10.740 --> 01:53:13.890
I just want my kids to be happy.

01:53:13.890 --> 01:53:15.120
So unpack that.

01:53:15.120 --> 01:53:16.700
Right, and yet we do all these things

01:53:16.700 --> 01:53:18.540
that lead to a straight jacket in childhood,

01:53:18.540 --> 01:53:20.120
and you only can do X, Y, and Z,

01:53:20.120 --> 01:53:22.620
and then they're not happy and they resent us.

01:53:22.620 --> 01:53:25.100
Or maybe they are sort of wildly successful

01:53:25.100 --> 01:53:27.970
in a career that we chose and resent us

01:53:27.970 --> 01:53:29.718
because they didn't get to do what they wanted.

01:53:29.718 --> 01:53:33.320
You know, we we've lost sight, Rich,

01:53:33.320 --> 01:53:36.020
I think of what it means to be happy.

01:53:36.020 --> 01:53:38.282
I mean, I think, you know, I think I know

01:53:38.282 --> 01:53:41.073
we've gotten to those places,

01:53:41.073 --> 01:53:43.977
but too many of us think happiness equals

01:53:43.977 --> 01:53:48.420
the corporate degree with X salary.

01:53:48.420 --> 01:53:53.020
When in fact happiness comes from being content

01:53:53.020 --> 01:53:57.739
with what you have and with who you are, right.

01:53:57.739 --> 01:54:00.770
Yeah, aligning your life in accordance

01:54:00.770 --> 01:54:05.410
with your particular values and being engaged

01:54:05.410 --> 01:54:08.676
with the world in a manner in which you're able to,

01:54:08.676 --> 01:54:11.200
you know, harness the things that you care about

01:54:11.200 --> 01:54:12.780
and share them with others.

01:54:12.780 --> 01:54:14.074
So lemme be super practical.

01:54:14.074 --> 01:54:19.055
A kid who grows up here in LA or where I live in the Bay,

01:54:19.055 --> 01:54:24.055
high cost of living, may not be able to live as an adult

01:54:26.100 --> 01:54:29.070
where they grew up if they choose a profession,

01:54:29.070 --> 01:54:32.434
a career, a trade where they don't earn a lot.

01:54:32.434 --> 01:54:35.750
But they can go be that person in a more affordable city.

01:54:35.750 --> 01:54:39.182
I mean, I think we're gonna see a lot of Gen Zers

01:54:39.182 --> 01:54:43.066
flocking to cities and towns that have jobs,

01:54:43.066 --> 01:54:44.568
at a reasonable cost of living,

01:54:44.568 --> 01:54:47.620
better around climate change, right.

01:54:47.620 --> 01:54:50.270
There are places that are hospitable to young people.

01:54:51.500 --> 01:54:52.650
There are those places.

01:54:52.650 --> 01:54:53.620
Yeah, those places exist.

01:54:53.620 --> 01:54:55.550
Those places exist and they need to exist.

01:54:55.550 --> 01:54:57.390
Something is very wrong macro economically.

01:54:57.390 --> 01:55:00.300
When not enough of those places exist,

01:55:00.300 --> 01:55:02.997
and of course, we're seeing that in your town and mine,

01:55:02.997 --> 01:55:05.070
but I'm here to say to young people listening,

01:55:05.070 --> 01:55:08.018
there are places where you can be a social worker

01:55:08.018 --> 01:55:10.140
or a third grade teacher

01:55:10.140 --> 01:55:12.520
and afford a decent place to live

01:55:12.520 --> 01:55:14.252
and have a great quality of life.

01:55:14.252 --> 01:55:16.670
It just might not be in Palo Alto.

01:55:16.670 --> 01:55:19.550
Right, but if you choose to stay in one of these places

01:55:19.550 --> 01:55:22.532
on your checklist of the things we traditionally look at

01:55:22.532 --> 01:55:27.070
as steps along the path towards adulthood,

01:55:27.070 --> 01:55:31.190
it's like go to college, you know, move out,

01:55:31.190 --> 01:55:33.170
get the job, get married, et cetera.

01:55:33.170 --> 01:55:36.220
But on the move out piece, like leaving home,

01:55:36.220 --> 01:55:39.520
that's less and less possible for a lot of young people.

01:55:39.520 --> 01:55:42.550
We're seeing young people returning to the home

01:55:42.550 --> 01:55:44.760
and staying there for periods

01:55:44.760 --> 01:55:47.360
that extend beyond what we were used to

01:55:47.360 --> 01:55:50.144
when we were younger because cost of living is so high.

01:55:50.144 --> 01:55:54.850
And yet we have the opportunity to reframe that.

01:55:54.850 --> 01:55:56.252
Not as some kind of failure,

01:55:56.252 --> 01:55:59.860
but as an opportunity to continue

01:55:59.860 --> 01:56:02.670
to develop your adult skills,

01:56:02.670 --> 01:56:05.020
even though you're within the confines

01:56:05.020 --> 01:56:06.440
of your parents' home.

01:56:06.440 --> 01:56:07.850
Yah, like you have to live at home,

01:56:07.850 --> 01:56:11.322
that doesn't excuse you from developing these other skills

01:56:11.322 --> 01:56:13.100
about how to become an adult.

01:56:13.100 --> 01:56:14.070
That's right, Rich.

01:56:14.070 --> 01:56:17.410
So it's not with whom you live or where,

01:56:17.410 --> 01:56:20.060
it's, how are you behaving wherever it is you live.

01:56:20.060 --> 01:56:21.796
And so there are young adults who came home in the pandemic

01:56:21.796 --> 01:56:24.040
and showed up as young adults.

01:56:24.040 --> 01:56:25.540
They'd been accustomed to freedom,

01:56:25.540 --> 01:56:26.640
college or the workplace,

01:56:26.640 --> 01:56:27.940
they're back at home in their childhood bedroom,

01:56:27.940 --> 01:56:29.030
but they sat down with their folks,

01:56:29.030 --> 01:56:31.800
and they're like, hey, let me go get the groceries.

01:56:31.800 --> 01:56:33.110
It's safer for me out there.

01:56:33.110 --> 01:56:34.574
Can I make a meal X times per week

01:56:34.574 --> 01:56:36.511
because I wanna contribute,

01:56:36.511 --> 01:56:39.550
like mature, grownup participant.

01:56:39.550 --> 01:56:40.663
There were others who came home

01:56:40.663 --> 01:56:43.080
and retreated to their childhood bedroom

01:56:43.080 --> 01:56:45.110
and some combination of their parents.

01:56:45.110 --> 01:56:50.110
And they, you know, their behaviors (indistinct) I5 again.

01:56:52.550 --> 01:56:55.020
So there's nothing inherently wrong with choosing

01:56:55.020 --> 01:56:57.537
to live with your parents as an adult.

01:56:57.537 --> 01:57:01.450
It's imperative, however, that everybody in that arrangement

01:57:01.450 --> 01:57:05.730
be given the independence and freedom

01:57:05.730 --> 01:57:08.260
and take on the responsibilities

01:57:08.260 --> 01:57:10.470
inherent with that independence and freedom.

01:57:10.470 --> 01:57:12.230
So that everybody's needs are met

01:57:12.230 --> 01:57:14.010
and boundaries are respected.

01:57:14.010 --> 01:57:17.230
Yeah, that's been our experiment over the last 18 months.

01:57:17.230 --> 01:57:18.510
Our boys had moved out.

01:57:18.510 --> 01:57:19.910
They were living on the other side of town

01:57:19.910 --> 01:57:21.417
in an apartment with some friends.

01:57:21.417 --> 01:57:24.680
When the pandemic hit, we were like, please come home.

01:57:24.680 --> 01:57:25.901
We don't know what's going on over there.

01:57:25.901 --> 01:57:28.465
And they ended up just moving back in,

01:57:28.465 --> 01:57:31.640
and 'cause it was ridiculous for them to pay rent

01:57:31.640 --> 01:57:33.130
in an apartment that was tiny,

01:57:33.130 --> 01:57:35.240
that they, you know, we're gonna have to sit in

01:57:35.240 --> 01:57:37.042
by themselves for all this period of time.

01:57:37.042 --> 01:57:42.042
But it's been this exploration of, you know,

01:57:42.480 --> 01:57:45.650
collective living with, you know, young adults

01:57:45.650 --> 01:57:50.650
who aren't kids, but now they're back at home living

01:57:51.370 --> 01:57:52.900
and we're not charging them rent,

01:57:52.900 --> 01:57:55.040
but there's a certain set of expectations

01:57:55.040 --> 01:57:55.873
that come with that.

01:57:55.873 --> 01:57:57.160
They're gonna cook meals

01:57:57.160 --> 01:57:59.750
a certain number of nights a week.

01:57:59.750 --> 01:58:01.830
They're helping out with the carpooling

01:58:01.830 --> 01:58:03.480
and the driving of the other kids.

01:58:03.480 --> 01:58:06.320
And you know, we've been able to like find our way

01:58:06.320 --> 01:58:08.050
with all of that and make it all work.

01:58:08.050 --> 01:58:08.980
And it's actually been great.

01:58:08.980 --> 01:58:10.560
I've loved having them back home.

01:58:10.560 --> 01:58:12.930
Like I don't want them to move by now.

01:58:12.930 --> 01:58:14.660
They're going to be again soon,

01:58:14.660 --> 01:58:16.070
'cause they have dreams and goals

01:58:16.070 --> 01:58:17.143
that they're chasing, et cetera.

01:58:17.143 --> 01:58:21.730
But I've come to look at it as really precious

01:58:21.730 --> 01:58:23.650
and something that would have not have happened

01:58:23.650 --> 01:58:26.400
had this, you know, pandemic, not occurred.

01:58:26.400 --> 01:58:28.040
Absolutely, I've had the same thing.

01:58:28.040 --> 01:58:30.890
Mine are a little younger than your boys, I think, right.

01:58:32.544 --> 01:58:34.830
And we worked on, I love that,

01:58:34.830 --> 01:58:37.394
I think you just use the term repatterning or patterning.

01:58:37.394 --> 01:58:39.885
I use that term with respect to mine,

01:58:39.885 --> 01:58:43.020
particular my older one who I write about in the book

01:58:43.020 --> 01:58:46.520
as having had some challenges around ADHD

01:58:46.520 --> 01:58:49.040
and anxiety in his young adulthood.

01:58:49.040 --> 01:58:51.100
And he allows me to talk about this.

01:58:51.100 --> 01:58:53.756
So I'm not outside of bounds here.

01:58:53.756 --> 01:58:58.756
We've been working as a couple, my partner, Dan and I,

01:58:59.130 --> 01:59:02.036
on repatterning our way of being with this kid.

01:59:02.036 --> 01:59:05.310
We realize we've been too worried

01:59:05.310 --> 01:59:08.300
about some of his sensitivities and things

01:59:08.300 --> 01:59:11.640
such that we've over-managed and handled over-helped,

01:59:11.640 --> 01:59:13.989
which conveys, I don't think you can,

01:59:13.989 --> 01:59:17.266
which a psychologist at Yale has written about

01:59:17.266 --> 01:59:19.060
in "The Atlantic" so beautifully.

01:59:19.060 --> 01:59:21.550
When we try to curate the environment

01:59:21.550 --> 01:59:22.710
to meet their every need,

01:59:22.710 --> 01:59:25.900
we can turn their fears and to full-blown anxiety.

01:59:25.900 --> 01:59:27.530
It's as if we're saying that thing you fear

01:59:27.530 --> 01:59:28.810
is such a big deal.

01:59:28.810 --> 01:59:30.830
I agree, I will make sure it never happens.

01:59:30.830 --> 01:59:34.186
And it undermines their ability to develop

01:59:34.186 --> 01:59:36.690
an ability to cope and and the resilience

01:59:36.690 --> 01:59:38.600
that comes as you cope.

01:59:38.600 --> 01:59:40.560
And so we're repatterning with that kid

01:59:40.560 --> 01:59:43.781
and in family therapy, which is so wonderful

01:59:43.781 --> 01:59:46.850
and learning how to stand shoulder to shoulder

01:59:46.850 --> 01:59:50.048
with this man, I have a 22-year-old son,

01:59:50.048 --> 01:59:52.790
in a way I don't mean shoulder to shoulder right next to,

01:59:52.790 --> 01:59:55.441
I mean, near him walking the journey of life

01:59:55.441 --> 01:59:59.840
in his vicinity, rather than pushing him from behind

01:59:59.840 --> 02:00:02.570
holding him by the hand or dragging him from the front.

02:00:02.570 --> 02:00:04.139
Yeah, that's great.

02:00:04.139 --> 02:00:05.533
Yeah, it is great.

02:00:05.533 --> 02:00:10.533
One of the things that I plead guilty to doing (chuckles)

02:00:10.680 --> 02:00:15.680
is sitting down my kids at various stages

02:00:16.020 --> 02:00:19.630
over the course of their young lives and saying, listen,

02:00:19.630 --> 02:00:23.100
you have everything you need to be successful.

02:00:23.100 --> 02:00:26.280
You literally could be anything that you wanna be

02:00:26.280 --> 02:00:27.794
if you set your mind to it.

02:00:27.794 --> 02:00:32.427
And I felt so proud of myself for that big speech.

02:00:32.427 --> 02:00:35.021
So what did I do wrong here?

02:00:35.021 --> 02:00:40.021
Well, I think the only missing component is,

02:00:41.360 --> 02:00:43.040
I mean, you added, if you put your mind to it,

02:00:43.040 --> 02:00:44.630
a lot of parents just say you can be anything

02:00:44.630 --> 02:00:45.650
you wanna be.

02:00:45.650 --> 02:00:48.074
If you put your mind to it, A,

02:00:48.074 --> 02:00:52.730
if you work damn hard is B, and that was missing, I think.

02:00:54.560 --> 02:00:58.220
And I think, C, I would add things

02:00:58.220 --> 02:01:00.060
may often not go your way,

02:01:00.060 --> 02:01:02.790
but with hard work and continued effort,

02:01:02.790 --> 02:01:05.540
you'll be surprised at how much you can accomplish.

02:01:05.540 --> 02:01:08.127
So I think what we're trying to do is embed in the message

02:01:08.127 --> 02:01:11.260
that it's tremendous hard work that is required

02:01:11.260 --> 02:01:13.265
to be anything you wanna be.

02:01:13.265 --> 02:01:16.840
Yeah, I always try to hammer home that piece of it.

02:01:16.840 --> 02:01:18.090
I figured.

02:01:18.090 --> 02:01:20.290
'Cause that's kind of like my jam.

02:01:20.290 --> 02:01:22.560
But, but I also, you know,

02:01:22.560 --> 02:01:27.560
a big piece for me also is just modeling the behavior.

02:01:27.930 --> 02:01:31.688
Like I'm not big on telling them what to do or not to do,

02:01:31.688 --> 02:01:35.660
but I'm all about just making sure that I'm modeling

02:01:35.660 --> 02:01:38.260
the kind of habits and behaviors that I wanna see in them.

02:01:38.260 --> 02:01:40.250
Not that there's an expectation

02:01:40.250 --> 02:01:41.590
that they're gonna follow in that regard,

02:01:41.590 --> 02:01:45.510
but just so there's a bit of a north star there.

02:01:45.510 --> 02:01:49.320
Like they know, you know, like this is how I conduct myself

02:01:49.320 --> 02:01:50.760
in the world.

02:01:50.760 --> 02:01:51.593
Absolutely.

02:01:51.593 --> 02:01:55.479
So how has that, like, how does that measure up

02:01:55.479 --> 02:02:00.479
in terms of, like how impactful is the modeling of behavior

02:02:01.250 --> 02:02:03.600
versus like what's coming out of your mouth

02:02:03.600 --> 02:02:06.240
and the things that you're saying to your kids.

02:02:06.240 --> 02:02:08.213
I think anybody who studies or writes on this subject

02:02:08.213 --> 02:02:10.010
would say they watch what you do

02:02:10.010 --> 02:02:12.881
and pay far more attention to that

02:02:12.881 --> 02:02:15.900
than they listen to what you say, right.

02:02:15.900 --> 02:02:18.488
So obviously, we have to walk the walk.

02:02:18.488 --> 02:02:20.080
We absolutely have to walk the walk.

02:02:20.080 --> 02:02:21.470
And my kids called me on it.

02:02:21.470 --> 02:02:23.830
When I talk the talk about any college is fine.

02:02:23.830 --> 02:02:25.600
Oh, any college is fine, any college is fine,

02:02:25.600 --> 02:02:29.730
I was actively counteracting the Palo Alto message

02:02:29.730 --> 02:02:32.180
of you have to go to one of these four colleges.

02:02:32.180 --> 02:02:33.650
It's gotta be crazy in Palo Alto.

02:02:33.650 --> 02:02:34.483
It is crazy.

02:02:34.483 --> 02:02:35.380
Well, it's gotta be crazy here-

02:02:35.380 --> 02:02:37.080
All could change with all those Silicon valley people,

02:02:37.080 --> 02:02:38.498
like, I can't imagine the links

02:02:38.498 --> 02:02:40.920
to which some of those parents are going to get in the-

02:02:40.920 --> 02:02:43.990
Of course, well, in the college admission scandal,

02:02:43.990 --> 02:02:46.720
the varsity blues was an indicator.

02:02:46.720 --> 02:02:50.530
But when I, you know, I was with my daughter,

02:02:50.530 --> 02:02:53.788
I was not happy with her false schedule senior year.

02:02:53.788 --> 02:02:56.720
And I let her know and she's like,

02:02:56.720 --> 02:02:58.330
mom, there literally is nothing else I wanna take.

02:02:58.330 --> 02:02:59.163
They screwed up my schedule,

02:02:59.163 --> 02:03:00.210
I'm gonna fill it with a study hall.

02:03:00.210 --> 02:03:01.764
And I was like, find another class to take,

02:03:01.764 --> 02:03:02.846
find another class to take.

02:03:02.846 --> 02:03:04.990
That college you've said you wanna go to,

02:03:04.990 --> 02:03:06.820
we'll want you to have had another class to take.

02:03:06.820 --> 02:03:09.174
She said, are you telling me to just take any class

02:03:09.174 --> 02:03:10.768
to please some college?

02:03:10.768 --> 02:03:14.290
I'm pretty sure, I'm pretty sure

02:03:14.290 --> 02:03:15.560
that's not what you've said.

02:03:15.560 --> 02:03:19.383
I just looked at her sort of like, oh, shit.

02:03:19.383 --> 02:03:21.835
Even I who tells other people what to do

02:03:21.835 --> 02:03:23.635
I'm still getting it wrong.

02:03:23.635 --> 02:03:26.820
And she called me on it and I owned up to it

02:03:26.820 --> 02:03:28.120
and told her, thank you.

02:03:28.120 --> 02:03:30.130
And I apologize and stepped back.

02:03:30.130 --> 02:03:31.400
I had to step off.

02:03:31.400 --> 02:03:34.648
I was like, I was on the platform with her

02:03:34.648 --> 02:03:37.400
forming the way in which she would dive.

02:03:37.400 --> 02:03:41.020
Yeah, and let me be clear when I say modeling behavior,

02:03:41.020 --> 02:03:44.640
like that's probably 10% of how I actually behave.

02:03:44.640 --> 02:03:46.273
Like I'm an idiot most of the time.

02:03:46.273 --> 02:03:47.739
And I do all kinds of crazy stuff

02:03:47.739 --> 02:03:49.150
that my kids are like,

02:03:49.150 --> 02:03:51.940
I can't believe you did this or that, so.

02:03:51.940 --> 02:03:53.930
You know, talking about, first of all, that's fine.

02:03:53.930 --> 02:03:55.970
That's okay, and it's wonderful for people

02:03:55.970 --> 02:03:58.180
to know that you're a normal person.

02:03:59.390 --> 02:04:01.740
You talked about the chapter, don't talk to strangers.

02:04:01.740 --> 02:04:04.815
And we talked about how important it is to teach kids how.

02:04:04.815 --> 02:04:07.970
My husband and I, Dan, my partner, husband,

02:04:07.970 --> 02:04:09.436
we were in the kitchen one day recently,

02:04:09.436 --> 02:04:12.670
Sawyer, our 20, now 22-year-old was in the kitchen

02:04:12.670 --> 02:04:13.503
making food.

02:04:13.503 --> 02:04:14.336
We were at the other end.

02:04:14.336 --> 02:04:16.390
We were talking about the fact that we had purchased

02:04:16.390 --> 02:04:17.986
this expensive thing from a man

02:04:17.986 --> 02:04:21.492
and we were not happy with how it had all gone down.

02:04:21.492 --> 02:04:25.490
And so we were engaging around how do we talk to this guy?

02:04:25.490 --> 02:04:27.960
What do we say that is respectful of him,

02:04:27.960 --> 02:04:29.370
but also communicates our needs.

02:04:29.370 --> 02:04:33.333
And we were brainstorming how to write an email to this guy.

02:04:33.333 --> 02:04:37.370
Sawyer quips, thank you for having this conversation

02:04:37.370 --> 02:04:38.460
in front of me.

02:04:38.460 --> 02:04:40.650
We looked at him like, what are you talking about?

02:04:40.650 --> 02:04:44.074
He said, it's helpful to me to hear that adults

02:04:44.074 --> 02:04:46.932
have anxiety about how to talk to other adults

02:04:46.932 --> 02:04:50.000
and to hear you talk through your options

02:04:50.000 --> 02:04:50.890
about how to do it.

02:04:50.890 --> 02:04:55.170
And we realized we'd been having all of those tough regular,

02:04:55.170 --> 02:04:57.453
but sort of how to live life,

02:04:57.453 --> 02:05:00.550
how to negotiate the complicated terrain

02:05:00.550 --> 02:05:03.100
of human interaction or a bureaucracy.

02:05:03.100 --> 02:05:04.970
We had negotiated all of that

02:05:04.970 --> 02:05:06.990
out of the earshot of our kids.

02:05:06.990 --> 02:05:08.130
'Cause some wise person said,

02:05:08.130 --> 02:05:11.510
don't let your kids hear you struggle or be an,

02:05:11.510 --> 02:05:12.887
ah-ah, that's how they learn.

02:05:12.887 --> 02:05:14.970
So we said, you're welcome,

02:05:14.970 --> 02:05:16.270
and we'll try to do it more often.

02:05:16.270 --> 02:05:18.347
Yeah, I'm a big believer in that as well.

02:05:18.347 --> 02:05:21.780
The flip side of that, of course,

02:05:21.780 --> 02:05:23.700
is that your kids learn early and often

02:05:23.700 --> 02:05:26.118
that like parents don't like (indistinct) know anything,

02:05:26.118 --> 02:05:27.960
and nobody knows what they're doing,

02:05:27.960 --> 02:05:29.470
which is kind of like their bursts.

02:05:29.470 --> 02:05:31.170
It's bursting this, you know, bubble

02:05:31.170 --> 02:05:34.834
that like, oh, when I grow up, like I'm gonna get it.

02:05:34.834 --> 02:05:38.807
I think what you don't wanna do is blubber

02:05:38.807 --> 02:05:40.050
all of your difficult.

02:05:40.050 --> 02:05:42.500
You don't wanna be hyper-vulnerable with your kids.

02:05:42.500 --> 02:05:45.010
They need to see you are an authority,

02:05:45.010 --> 02:05:47.508
they are safe, you are confident.

02:05:47.508 --> 02:05:49.998
So they can't, they don't want to see you fall apart.

02:05:49.998 --> 02:05:52.685
But, again, it's about balance.

02:05:52.685 --> 02:05:54.380
It's like, let them see-

02:05:54.380 --> 02:05:56.470
They need to know that like you've got their back

02:05:56.470 --> 02:05:57.561
and they're safe and all of that.

02:05:57.561 --> 02:06:02.550
But the idea that your life is not without, you know,

02:06:02.550 --> 02:06:05.410
struggling with how to manage certain aspects of life,

02:06:05.410 --> 02:06:06.460
I think is important.

02:06:07.390 --> 02:06:08.760
That's Telegraph to them.

02:06:08.760 --> 02:06:09.873
And the other thing is money.

02:06:09.873 --> 02:06:11.680
You know, money's a big thing too.

02:06:11.680 --> 02:06:15.510
I mean, I grew up never once, you know,

02:06:15.510 --> 02:06:19.713
did anything, you know, helpful,

02:06:19.713 --> 02:06:22.630
was anything helpful, shared about like,

02:06:22.630 --> 02:06:23.560
how to deal with money,

02:06:23.560 --> 02:06:27.040
which is just a verboten subject off the table completely.

02:06:27.040 --> 02:06:29.117
So I had no sense of that whatsoever.

02:06:29.117 --> 02:06:32.140
You know, simple things like balancing your checkbook,

02:06:32.140 --> 02:06:36.000
but just like that whole world that suddenly

02:06:36.000 --> 02:06:37.750
when you're a young adult and you're in the world,

02:06:37.750 --> 02:06:41.029
becomes absolutely crucial to your survival.

02:06:41.029 --> 02:06:44.800
And was just a complete black hole

02:06:44.800 --> 02:06:46.700
in my learning experience.

02:06:46.700 --> 02:06:49.469
Same here, and then I was perhaps like you,

02:06:49.469 --> 02:06:53.180
one of many who find themselves embarrassed and bewildered,

02:06:53.180 --> 02:06:56.014
I'm so educated, and yet they don't seem to understand

02:06:56.014 --> 02:06:56.847
how money works.

02:06:56.847 --> 02:06:58.850
In my money chapter I talk about how critical it is

02:06:58.850 --> 02:07:00.330
that as a young person,

02:07:00.330 --> 02:07:02.340
you care about your 65-year-old self

02:07:02.340 --> 02:07:05.110
who will have wanted you to pay attention to its needs,

02:07:05.110 --> 02:07:07.030
to their needs at the same time

02:07:07.030 --> 02:07:09.870
to not be an indentured servant to your 65-year-old self,

02:07:09.870 --> 02:07:11.260
'cause who you are now matters too.

02:07:11.260 --> 02:07:13.995
It's about a balance of the now and the future.

02:07:13.995 --> 02:07:16.950
And in that chapter, I profile somebody

02:07:16.950 --> 02:07:20.250
who came out of the working poor into community college,

02:07:20.250 --> 02:07:23.170
into a job with UPS as a driver,

02:07:23.170 --> 02:07:24.245
and is now finished 35 years

02:07:24.245 --> 02:07:27.820
and has such a beautiful career trajectory,

02:07:27.820 --> 02:07:29.658
is now solidly middle class with a second home,

02:07:29.658 --> 02:07:33.300
Gainesville, Florida, and a stock.

02:07:33.300 --> 02:07:35.991
And, you know, has the American dream.

02:07:35.991 --> 02:07:40.710
And how he did it, and I have someone else profiled

02:07:40.710 --> 02:07:43.100
who's an artist from Stanford, majored in dance,

02:07:43.100 --> 02:07:44.690
took out student loans for Stanford

02:07:44.690 --> 02:07:46.990
and for University of Florida MFA

02:07:46.990 --> 02:07:49.720
to be a dancer in New York City.

02:07:49.720 --> 02:07:51.690
So she's got all of the student loan debt,

02:07:51.690 --> 02:07:53.200
and finally realizes I've got to pay this.

02:07:53.200 --> 02:07:55.300
I'm only earning this tiny amount as a dancer,

02:07:55.300 --> 02:07:56.133
I gotta get rid of my debt.

02:07:56.133 --> 02:07:58.520
And she became this disciplined person

02:07:58.520 --> 02:08:01.740
who would walk around three blocks out of her way

02:08:01.740 --> 02:08:04.150
to make sure the food smells from her favorite restaurant,

02:08:04.150 --> 02:08:06.520
we're offering in the air so that she wasn't tempted

02:08:06.520 --> 02:08:08.950
to spend money at a restaurant instead of her budgeted food

02:08:08.950 --> 02:08:10.300
from a grocery store.

02:08:10.300 --> 02:08:11.829
And she paid down her student loan debt

02:08:11.829 --> 02:08:15.380
with 50 grand with interest, 32 principal.

02:08:15.380 --> 02:08:18.097
She paid it down at three years on an artist's salary.

02:08:18.097 --> 02:08:20.950
And how she did it, and the fact that she's now

02:08:20.950 --> 02:08:24.000
a financial planner with a side gig, when she's not dancing,

02:08:24.000 --> 02:08:25.238
she can advise people on their finances,

02:08:25.238 --> 02:08:27.350
you know, that's who she is.

02:08:27.350 --> 02:08:28.830
And she advises people.

02:08:28.830 --> 02:08:29.993
She went out on Facebook and said,

02:08:29.993 --> 02:08:31.765
I'm gonna be debt-free in three years,

02:08:31.765 --> 02:08:32.989
I'm debt-free today.

02:08:32.989 --> 02:08:35.250
And she didn't get any comments.

02:08:35.250 --> 02:08:36.123
She got a ton of likes,

02:08:36.123 --> 02:08:38.990
but she got all these direct messages

02:08:38.990 --> 02:08:41.510
and she realized people are ashamed to comment

02:08:41.510 --> 02:08:42.720
I need your help.

02:08:42.720 --> 02:08:45.750
in the direct messages it was how did you do it?

02:08:45.750 --> 02:08:46.928
How do I start?

02:08:46.928 --> 02:08:48.420
Can you help me?

02:08:48.420 --> 02:08:49.762
Nobody would say it in public.

02:08:49.762 --> 02:08:52.880
And so this is now, it's funny.

02:08:52.880 --> 02:08:55.260
She's like a finance person, that's her side hustle

02:08:55.260 --> 02:08:56.670
where the art is the main gig.

02:08:56.670 --> 02:08:58.140
For many people it would be the reverse.

02:08:58.140 --> 02:08:58.973
Right.
Yeah.

02:08:58.973 --> 02:08:59.870
Right, right, right.

02:08:59.870 --> 02:09:02.250
Yeah, I think it's a huge problem,

02:09:02.250 --> 02:09:04.160
especially amongst young people

02:09:04.160 --> 02:09:06.880
who are emerging from college or in situations

02:09:06.880 --> 02:09:10.892
where they're able to get those loans, which, you know,

02:09:10.892 --> 02:09:14.380
feels like in certain instances are handed out

02:09:14.380 --> 02:09:17.900
without kind of a manual for like what this actually means

02:09:17.900 --> 02:09:19.800
and what to do with it and all of that-

02:09:19.800 --> 02:09:20.680
And credit cards.

02:09:20.680 --> 02:09:23.080
And we're just basically sending young people

02:09:23.080 --> 02:09:24.889
out into the world saddled with unbelievable debt

02:09:24.889 --> 02:09:27.550
that shortcuts, you know, the choices

02:09:27.550 --> 02:09:28.520
that they're capable of making.

02:09:28.520 --> 02:09:31.510
And, you know, it's just wrong.

02:09:31.510 --> 02:09:33.260
There's gotta be a better way to do this.

02:09:33.260 --> 02:09:35.350
Absolutely, it is wrong.

02:09:35.350 --> 02:09:37.380
This generation has more student loan debt

02:09:37.380 --> 02:09:38.380
than any of us ever had.

02:09:38.380 --> 02:09:40.170
It's terrible, and you can't get rid of it.

02:09:40.170 --> 02:09:41.964
You could declare bankruptcy, it's never goes away.

02:09:41.964 --> 02:09:43.070
It's ridiculous.

02:09:43.070 --> 02:09:45.650
And the cost of living is too high in many cities

02:09:45.650 --> 02:09:48.870
such that you can't afford a one bedroom, right.

02:09:48.870 --> 02:09:49.810
What are they supposed to do?

02:09:49.810 --> 02:09:51.770
And their granddad's saying, what's wrong with you?

02:09:51.770 --> 02:09:52.603
I got a job in my day.

02:09:52.603 --> 02:09:54.370
It's like shut up, its not you day.

02:09:54.370 --> 02:09:56.890
Exactly, right, when you could go work at the factory

02:09:56.890 --> 02:09:58.852
and own a home and a car and all that like that,

02:09:58.852 --> 02:10:00.288
you just can't do that anymore.

02:10:00.288 --> 02:10:03.306
That's right, maybe one day, maybe soon.

02:10:03.306 --> 02:10:07.080
Yeah. Well, we got to wrap this up, but final words,

02:10:07.080 --> 02:10:09.870
maybe like a final word for the parent

02:10:09.870 --> 02:10:12.270
and a final word for the young person,

02:10:13.530 --> 02:10:15.938
somebody who's new to these ideas,

02:10:15.938 --> 02:10:18.383
who's got a young person in their house

02:10:18.383 --> 02:10:20.910
and maybe they've made a few mistakes,

02:10:20.910 --> 02:10:21.980
they're trying to get it right.

02:10:21.980 --> 02:10:23.362
Like where does this person begin,

02:10:23.362 --> 02:10:25.910
other than with picking up your books, of course.

02:10:25.910 --> 02:10:26.896
Yeah.

02:10:26.896 --> 02:10:28.540
To any parent listening,

02:10:28.540 --> 02:10:31.470
I hope it's clear I'm with you, I'm in it.

02:10:31.470 --> 02:10:32.820
I'm screwing up left and right.

02:10:32.820 --> 02:10:34.797
I'm just really mindful of what I'm doing

02:10:34.797 --> 02:10:36.654
and trying to iterate and get better.

02:10:36.654 --> 02:10:39.480
So I'm here to believe in you.

02:10:39.480 --> 02:10:41.750
If you're hearing yourself in any of the examples

02:10:41.750 --> 02:10:43.611
we've been discussing, that's step one.

02:10:43.611 --> 02:10:45.324
Now go to step two and ask yourself

02:10:45.324 --> 02:10:46.809
what am I gonna do about it?

02:10:46.809 --> 02:10:50.080
And be in community with other parents

02:10:50.080 --> 02:10:51.236
who are tired of the rat race

02:10:51.236 --> 02:10:53.812
and are tired of micromanaging your kid's existence

02:10:53.812 --> 02:10:57.130
in order to get them to the right future,

02:10:57.130 --> 02:10:59.010
the right future as a kid who leaves your home

02:10:59.010 --> 02:11:02.220
with agency and resilience and good character,

02:11:02.220 --> 02:11:05.330
those are three things you can instill for free.

02:11:05.330 --> 02:11:09.895
And you don't have to play this crazy game of lunatics,

02:11:09.895 --> 02:11:11.122
making everybody crazy.

02:11:11.122 --> 02:11:12.940
That's exactly right.

02:11:12.940 --> 02:11:14.770
And to young people listening,

02:11:14.770 --> 02:11:17.577
I would say, keep listening to great podcasts

02:11:17.577 --> 02:11:20.220
because there's so much to learn

02:11:20.220 --> 02:11:22.750
from the experience of others who've gone before you

02:11:22.750 --> 02:11:23.839
or going through it with you.

02:11:23.839 --> 02:11:27.442
And this is your one wild and precious life.

02:11:27.442 --> 02:11:30.699
It is nobody's business to tell you how to live it.

02:11:30.699 --> 02:11:33.920
I want you to figure out who you are,

02:11:33.920 --> 02:11:36.263
by which I mean, what are you good at?

02:11:36.263 --> 02:11:41.263
And what do you love and where do you feel

02:11:41.470 --> 02:11:42.960
a deep sense of belonging?

02:11:43.800 --> 02:11:46.659
Find the Venn diagram, intersection of those things,

02:11:46.659 --> 02:11:49.390
go out and make that life happen.

02:11:49.390 --> 02:11:50.678
It's waiting for you.

02:11:50.678 --> 02:11:53.020
I think you just stuck the landing

02:11:53.020 --> 02:11:55.850
like a Simone Biles floor routine.

02:11:57.930 --> 02:11:59.100
Powerful, thank you.
Thank you.

02:11:59.100 --> 02:11:59.933
Thank you so much.

02:11:59.933 --> 02:12:01.840
It's beautiful, that was super fun.

02:12:01.840 --> 02:12:02.673
Is it done?

02:12:02.673 --> 02:12:04.120
How do you feel, we're still recording,

02:12:04.120 --> 02:12:05.200
but like, you good?

02:12:05.200 --> 02:12:06.033
I'm good.

02:12:06.033 --> 02:12:07.020
Did we hit everything?

02:12:07.020 --> 02:12:07.980
I think so.

02:12:07.980 --> 02:12:10.100
Yeah, it was fantastic, thank you so much.

02:12:10.100 --> 02:12:11.959
In the meantime, everybody pick up your turn,

02:12:11.959 --> 02:12:14.840
"How To Be an Adult," is quite the book.

02:12:14.840 --> 02:12:17.740
Like I said, I think this will be a perennial bestseller

02:12:17.740 --> 02:12:19.290
for parents and young people alike,

02:12:19.290 --> 02:12:22.431
something that you'll wanna gift your young person

02:12:22.431 --> 02:12:26.960
upon graduation and a book for every young person

02:12:26.960 --> 02:12:30.290
who's entering the work world or the world of grownups

02:12:30.290 --> 02:12:33.540
and trying to make sense of this insane spinning planet

02:12:33.540 --> 02:12:34.693
that we're all sharing.

02:12:34.693 --> 02:12:37.578
In the meantime, if you wanna connect with Julie,

02:12:37.578 --> 02:12:40.590
what's the best place to send them to?

02:12:40.590 --> 02:12:43.720
Your website, your Twitter, your Instagram?

02:12:43.720 --> 02:12:45.900
Absolutely, I'm everywhere on social

02:12:45.900 --> 02:12:47.800
@jlythcotthaims, that's my first initial,

02:12:47.800 --> 02:12:49.074
last name, no hyphen.

02:12:49.074 --> 02:12:52.000
Maybe even TikTok one day soon, we shall see,

02:12:52.000 --> 02:12:53.650
but definitely Twitter, Instagram,

02:12:53.650 --> 02:12:54.820
Clubhouse and Facebook.

02:12:54.820 --> 02:12:56.320
You have points for me?

